[{:db/id 17592186188339 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-047bb67d-8c03-57ec-a81f-c32911be6893" "question-e9ff9199-1184-598e-a945-b789f3d5b0b6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-047bb67d-8c03-57ec-a81f-c32911be6893" :question/id #uuid "047bb67d-8c03-57ec-a81f-c32911be6893" :question/math-academy-id "q-146226" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{27}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{27}$ | $\\frac{7}{27}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=0]$ given that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|0}=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $X,$ corresponding to the row totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{27}$ | $\\frac{16}{27}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{27}$ | $\\frac{7}{27}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{11}{27}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding row total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x:$\n\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{9}{16}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{11}$ | $\\frac{7}{11}$ | $\\frac{3}{11}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=0] & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,0)]-{\\mu }_{Y|0}^{2} \\\\  & =[{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{16}+{4}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}+{8}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}]-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =0+6+4-4 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8e701f23-45b5-584b-9ff7-052c5c629f38"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8e701f23-45b5-584b-9ff7-052c5c629f38" :answer-field/id #uuid "8e701f23-45b5-584b-9ff7-052c5c629f38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e3c50614-2143-5835-91a2-26951415d4a3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e3a0e51e-a2b2-5d25-84bb-27f6878f5ff2" "answer-e38c50ad-e0fc-52f2-b45a-0aed3bf8753b" "answer-e3c50614-2143-5835-91a2-26951415d4a3" "answer-3ec68043-b866-5e5b-8bb7-32d1408e60c0" "answer-c5e9e267-a7b8-546e-8ca5-7597e4f9d784"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3a0e51e-a2b2-5d25-84bb-27f6878f5ff2" :answer/id #uuid "e3a0e51e-a2b2-5d25-84bb-27f6878f5ff2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e38c50ad-e0fc-52f2-b45a-0aed3bf8753b" :answer/id #uuid "e38c50ad-e0fc-52f2-b45a-0aed3bf8753b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3c50614-2143-5835-91a2-26951415d4a3" :answer/id #uuid "e3c50614-2143-5835-91a2-26951415d4a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ec68043-b866-5e5b-8bb7-32d1408e60c0" :answer/id #uuid "3ec68043-b866-5e5b-8bb7-32d1408e60c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5e9e267-a7b8-546e-8ca5-7597e4f9d784" :answer/id #uuid "c5e9e267-a7b8-546e-8ca5-7597e4f9d784" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188687 :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=1]$ given that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|1}=\\frac{6}{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $X,$ corresponding to the row totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding row total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x:$\n\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{4}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)]-{\\mu }_{Y|1}^{2} \\\\  & =[{1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}+{2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}]-{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{36}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186186402 :answer-field/correct 17592186186403 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-e9ff9199-1184-598e-a945-b789f3d5b0b6" :question/id #uuid "e9ff9199-1184-598e-a945-b789f3d5b0b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-146236" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=5$ | $Y=10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{4}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{25}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{25}$ | $\\frac{1}{25}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=6]$ given that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|6}=\\frac{15}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $X,$ corresponding to the row totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=5$ | $Y=10$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{4}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{25}$ | $\\frac{23}{25}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{25}$ | $\\frac{1}{25}$ | $\\frac{2}{25}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding row total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x:$\n\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ | $Y=5$ | $Y=10$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{20}{23}$ | $\\frac{3}{23}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=6] & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,6)]-{\\mu }_{Y|6}^{2} \\\\  & =[{5}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+{10}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}]-{(\\frac{15}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}+50-\\frac{225}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-273fb450-78e1-578c-ad1c-ea60b9f9611a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-273fb450-78e1-578c-ad1c-ea60b9f9611a" :answer-field/id #uuid "273fb450-78e1-578c-ad1c-ea60b9f9611a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e44fbbd6-2948-5df7-8863-f9b14dba4caa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-46451ad3-292e-5818-b57f-512919c1b762" "answer-9848b01d-ae3d-5b11-a03c-4137d1ec2d54" "answer-f1966108-9bd3-50ba-ae71-0b6fda1b9652" "answer-85f1ae38-1f12-5af1-9e47-1e397e55e732" "answer-e44fbbd6-2948-5df7-8863-f9b14dba4caa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46451ad3-292e-5818-b57f-512919c1b762" :answer/id #uuid "46451ad3-292e-5818-b57f-512919c1b762" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9848b01d-ae3d-5b11-a03c-4137d1ec2d54" :answer/id #uuid "9848b01d-ae3d-5b11-a03c-4137d1ec2d54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1966108-9bd3-50ba-ae71-0b6fda1b9652" :answer/id #uuid "f1966108-9bd3-50ba-ae71-0b6fda1b9652" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85f1ae38-1f12-5af1-9e47-1e397e55e732" :answer/id #uuid "85f1ae38-1f12-5af1-9e47-1e397e55e732" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e44fbbd6-2948-5df7-8863-f9b14dba4caa" :answer/id #uuid "e44fbbd6-2948-5df7-8863-f9b14dba4caa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188328 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4b9363aa-00d2-51a2-9bff-045f9cb8f2d6" "question-fa585366-16bd-5221-b5b1-a80000ac4783"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4b9363aa-00d2-51a2-9bff-045f9cb8f2d6" :question/id #uuid "4b9363aa-00d2-51a2-9bff-045f9cb8f2d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-146278" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=9$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=2]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|2}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution of $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=9$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{5}{16}$ | $\\frac{5}{16}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y:$\n\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ | $\\frac{4}{5}$ |\n| $X=9$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=2] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)]-{\\mu }_{X|2}^{2} \\\\  & =[{3}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}+{9}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}]-{5}^{2} \\\\  & =6+27-25 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-798593a5-cfe0-5f7d-84fa-43e7795c761a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-798593a5-cfe0-5f7d-84fa-43e7795c761a" :answer-field/id #uuid "798593a5-cfe0-5f7d-84fa-43e7795c761a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b3aef80f-ba63-5a2a-892f-31618b540913" :answer-field/choices ["answer-82fe3a83-b24a-5a3c-bffb-cf8e5344fd6a" "answer-66d1570e-0fc2-5b26-944e-0848dd40f138" "answer-9f3745f3-2e12-5887-955e-9fd687c8f6c3" "answer-b3aef80f-ba63-5a2a-892f-31618b540913" "answer-7e4bbbd8-2767-5014-b1ea-d2f03b69e408"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82fe3a83-b24a-5a3c-bffb-cf8e5344fd6a" :answer/id #uuid "82fe3a83-b24a-5a3c-bffb-cf8e5344fd6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66d1570e-0fc2-5b26-944e-0848dd40f138" :answer/id #uuid "66d1570e-0fc2-5b26-944e-0848dd40f138" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f3745f3-2e12-5887-955e-9fd687c8f6c3" :answer/id #uuid "9f3745f3-2e12-5887-955e-9fd687c8f6c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3aef80f-ba63-5a2a-892f-31618b540913" :answer/id #uuid "b3aef80f-ba63-5a2a-892f-31618b540913" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e4bbbd8-2767-5014-b1ea-d2f03b69e408" :answer/id #uuid "7e4bbbd8-2767-5014-b1ea-d2f03b69e408" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fa585366-16bd-5221-b5b1-a80000ac4783" :question/id #uuid "fa585366-16bd-5221-b5b1-a80000ac4783" :question/math-academy-id "q-146282" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=2]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|2}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution of $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y:$\n\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=2] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)]-{\\mu }_{X|2}^{2} \\\\  & =[{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+{1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+{2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}]-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2}+1-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0f312ee4-2cb7-5564-9f11-50b74da2484d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0f312ee4-2cb7-5564-9f11-50b74da2484d" :answer-field/id #uuid "0f312ee4-2cb7-5564-9f11-50b74da2484d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-14738cb2-b471-5d99-b3eb-4afbb5fa348a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-518547db-16ad-50af-8a24-b4267aa35a9c" "answer-a50bc467-b427-559a-829c-9f60582a46c2" "answer-14738cb2-b471-5d99-b3eb-4afbb5fa348a" "answer-20ae5337-49f2-5726-8ce9-b5fca604cf7a" "answer-ee546c20-c60e-59e8-af15-1c8202fb7fba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-518547db-16ad-50af-8a24-b4267aa35a9c" :answer/id #uuid "518547db-16ad-50af-8a24-b4267aa35a9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a50bc467-b427-559a-829c-9f60582a46c2" :answer/id #uuid "a50bc467-b427-559a-829c-9f60582a46c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14738cb2-b471-5d99-b3eb-4afbb5fa348a" :answer/id #uuid "14738cb2-b471-5d99-b3eb-4afbb5fa348a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20ae5337-49f2-5726-8ce9-b5fca604cf7a" :answer/id #uuid "20ae5337-49f2-5726-8ce9-b5fca604cf7a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee546c20-c60e-59e8-af15-1c8202fb7fba" :answer/id #uuid "ee546c20-c60e-59e8-af15-1c8202fb7fba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-2295589f-1938-53e1-a9aa-ded9c732eec7" :activity/id #uuid "2295589f-1938-53e1-a9aa-ded9c732eec7" :activity/title "Conditional Variance for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049871 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-0300a9fc-85df-55bb-885b-ea046fb4bb5e" "step-d245c012-0e70-5d3b-bb3f-3e7de5fe0a63" "step-30c58b82-3c11-5385-abbd-de1c4e8158be" "step-dba60abd-e9be-5c2c-86c8-da39dba6d99b" "step-54fc286d-ca29-5168-94f4-61b515ef6f44"] :activity/first-step "step-0300a9fc-85df-55bb-885b-ea046fb4bb5e" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-0300a9fc-85df-55bb-885b-ea046fb4bb5e" :step/id #uuid "0300a9fc-85df-55bb-885b-ea046fb4bb5e" :step/content "question-047bb67d-8c03-57ec-a81f-c32911be6893" :step/next "step-d245c012-0e70-5d3b-bb3f-3e7de5fe0a63" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d245c012-0e70-5d3b-bb3f-3e7de5fe0a63" :step/id #uuid "d245c012-0e70-5d3b-bb3f-3e7de5fe0a63" :step/content 17592186188687 :step/next "step-30c58b82-3c11-5385-abbd-de1c4e8158be" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-30c58b82-3c11-5385-abbd-de1c4e8158be" :step/id #uuid "30c58b82-3c11-5385-abbd-de1c4e8158be" :step/content "question-e9ff9199-1184-598e-a945-b789f3d5b0b6" :step/next "step-dba60abd-e9be-5c2c-86c8-da39dba6d99b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-dba60abd-e9be-5c2c-86c8-da39dba6d99b" :step/id #uuid "dba60abd-e9be-5c2c-86c8-da39dba6d99b" :step/content "question-4b9363aa-00d2-51a2-9bff-045f9cb8f2d6" :step/next "step-54fc286d-ca29-5168-94f4-61b515ef6f44" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-54fc286d-ca29-5168-94f4-61b515ef6f44" :step/id #uuid "54fc286d-ca29-5168-94f4-61b515ef6f44" :step/content "question-fa585366-16bd-5221-b5b1-a80000ac4783" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068717 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-dfe6edfc-d27b-50ff-aa28-a96fc85e1d12" "question-f658914c-7028-577a-9b28-3f8336734446"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068491 :question/problem "Solve the absolute value inequality $\\left\\vert\\dfrac{x-1}{6}\\right\\vert \\leq 1.$" :question/worked-solution "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $\\dfrac{x-1}{6}$ whose distance from zero is less than or equal to $1.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\dfrac{x-1}{6} \\leq  1\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\frac{x-1}{6}\\le 1 \\\\ -6 & \\le x-1\\le 6 \\\\ -5 & \\le x\\le 7\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068718 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8ba584f9-4667-5d0f-bd62-9fcff937c1e1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068510 :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|10-2x|+1 & <5 \\\\ |10-2x| & <4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $10-2x$ whose distance from zero is less than $4.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-4 < 10 - 2x < 4\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods. (Remember that whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we have to flip the inequality signs.)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4<10-2x & <4 \\\\ -14<-2x & <-6 \\\\ 14>2x & >6 \\\\ 7>x & >3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo make the final result easier to read, we will rewrite it so that the smaller number is on the left:\n\n$$\n3 < x < 7\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068719 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e891fb42-cafd-57b7-9773-6b5a4bb29926" "question-0f90e4de-4957-59b8-990a-09a9092a26e8" "question-4a81effc-bede-5f14-bdb7-3f40c0a9e067"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068493 :question/problem "Solve the absolute value inequality $|12x - 5| > 7.$" :question/worked-solution "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $12x - 5$ whose distance from zero is greater than $7.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-5 & <-7 & \\text{or} &  & 12x-5 & >7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve each inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-5 & <-7 &  &  & 12x-5 & >7 \\\\ 12x & <-2 &  &  & 12x & >12 \\\\ x & <-\\frac{2}{12} &  &  & x & >\\frac{12}{12} \\\\ x & <-\\frac{1}{6} &  &  & x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & <-\\frac{1}{6} & \\text{or} &  & x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-dfe6edfc-d27b-50ff-aa28-a96fc85e1d12" :question/id #uuid "dfe6edfc-d27b-50ff-aa28-a96fc85e1d12" :question/math-academy-id "q-212474" :question/problem "Solve the absolute value inequality $|3x+2|<7.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x<$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $3x+2$ whose distance from zero is less than $7.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-7<3x+2<7\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7<3x+2 & <7 \\\\ -9<3x & <5 \\\\ -3<x & <\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c5a87638-c365-5e45-84cf-a39c2de661dd" "answer-field-48861394-50ec-54df-8b93-dcc5fd4bb869" "answer-field-fab47d47-48f0-502b-9d99-7f0d398eb9b2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c5a87638-c365-5e45-84cf-a39c2de661dd" :answer-field/id #uuid "c5a87638-c365-5e45-84cf-a39c2de661dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a23146c8-3400-5c70-8a31-ad8b237f9f51" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a23146c8-3400-5c70-8a31-ad8b237f9f51"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a23146c8-3400-5c70-8a31-ad8b237f9f51" :answer/id #uuid "a23146c8-3400-5c70-8a31-ad8b237f9f51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-48861394-50ec-54df-8b93-dcc5fd4bb869" :answer-field/id #uuid "48861394-50ec-54df-8b93-dcc5fd4bb869" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-fd4e8030-94fe-56aa-b5a3-23f94ae0c956" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fd4e8030-94fe-56aa-b5a3-23f94ae0c956"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd4e8030-94fe-56aa-b5a3-23f94ae0c956" :answer/id #uuid "fd4e8030-94fe-56aa-b5a3-23f94ae0c956" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "<" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fab47d47-48f0-502b-9d99-7f0d398eb9b2" :answer-field/id #uuid "fab47d47-48f0-502b-9d99-7f0d398eb9b2" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-88d348bc-0475-5cca-be53-e26c51c508ed" :answer-field/choices ["answer-88d348bc-0475-5cca-be53-e26c51c508ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88d348bc-0475-5cca-be53-e26c51c508ed" :answer/id #uuid "88d348bc-0475-5cca-be53-e26c51c508ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f658914c-7028-577a-9b28-3f8336734446" :question/id #uuid "f658914c-7028-577a-9b28-3f8336734446" :question/math-academy-id "q-215469" :question/problem "Solve the absolute value inequality $|\\frac{2x+1}{4}|-\\frac{3}{2}\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{2x+1}{4}|-\\frac{3}{2} & \\le 1 \\\\ |\\frac{2x+1}{4}| & \\le \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $\\frac{2x+1}{4}$ whose distance from zero is less than or equal to $\\frac{5}{2}.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\text{ }-\\frac{5}{2}\\le \\frac{2x+1}{4}\\le \\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}\\le \\frac{2x+1}{4} & \\le \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -10\\le 2x+1 & \\le 10 \\\\ -11\\le 2x & \\le 9 \\\\ -\\frac{11}{2}\\le x & \\le \\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f8aa3d41-3265-55e2-8610-64e07f1b1be0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f8aa3d41-3265-55e2-8610-64e07f1b1be0" :answer-field/id #uuid "f8aa3d41-3265-55e2-8610-64e07f1b1be0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9f3f8dfc-bd30-57a3-8e58-10050f40289f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8b02d4c3-cb7d-502b-979e-90622d4eb3c6" "answer-9f3f8dfc-bd30-57a3-8e58-10050f40289f" "answer-3d0dffa0-b169-5ae1-a016-418de2b2d5ed" "answer-13817b1d-5fce-529d-8f9b-7b0617149423" "answer-36abe3a8-28f2-5cc2-9bad-e83391fce48b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b02d4c3-cb7d-502b-979e-90622d4eb3c6" :answer/id #uuid "8b02d4c3-cb7d-502b-979e-90622d4eb3c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{7}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f3f8dfc-bd30-57a3-8e58-10050f40289f" :answer/id #uuid "9f3f8dfc-bd30-57a3-8e58-10050f40289f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{9}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d0dffa0-b169-5ae1-a016-418de2b2d5ed" :answer/id #uuid "3d0dffa0-b169-5ae1-a016-418de2b2d5ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13817b1d-5fce-529d-8f9b-7b0617149423" :answer/id #uuid "13817b1d-5fce-529d-8f9b-7b0617149423" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36abe3a8-28f2-5cc2-9bad-e83391fce48b" :answer/id #uuid "36abe3a8-28f2-5cc2-9bad-e83391fce48b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{9}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8ba584f9-4667-5d0f-bd62-9fcff937c1e1" :question/id #uuid "8ba584f9-4667-5d0f-bd62-9fcff937c1e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-215488" :question/problem "Solve the absolute value inequality $\\frac{3}{5}+|\\frac{1-3x}{5}|<2.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}+|\\frac{1-3x}{5}| & <2 \\\\ |\\frac{1-3x}{5}| & <\\frac{7}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $\\frac{1-3x}{5}$ whose distance from zero is less than $\\frac{7}{5}.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{7}{5}<\\frac{1-3x}{5}<\\frac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods. (Remember that whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we have to flip the inequality signs.)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{5}<\\frac{1-3x}{5} & <\\frac{7}{5} \\\\ -7<1-3x & <7 \\\\ -8<-3x & <6 \\\\ 8>3x & >-6 \\\\ \\frac{8}{3}>x & >-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo make the final result easier to read, we will rewrite it so that the smaller number is on the left:\n\n$$\n-2<x<\\frac{8}{3}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-99213336-50fc-5fea-97c9-bdf16997d0ec" "answer-field-894d5fc0-61ba-5e40-9d87-aa14a2b88f4d" "answer-field-5abd1c81-55cb-5df8-b5f1-f81a16167daf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-99213336-50fc-5fea-97c9-bdf16997d0ec" :answer-field/id #uuid "99213336-50fc-5fea-97c9-bdf16997d0ec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-240db0bb-0455-5f41-a0df-c1e8f1a12e7f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-240db0bb-0455-5f41-a0df-c1e8f1a12e7f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-240db0bb-0455-5f41-a0df-c1e8f1a12e7f" :answer/id #uuid "240db0bb-0455-5f41-a0df-c1e8f1a12e7f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-894d5fc0-61ba-5e40-9d87-aa14a2b88f4d" :answer-field/id #uuid "894d5fc0-61ba-5e40-9d87-aa14a2b88f4d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2615d33c-12ce-5201-84d1-8b5ec2754f69" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2615d33c-12ce-5201-84d1-8b5ec2754f69"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2615d33c-12ce-5201-84d1-8b5ec2754f69" :answer/id #uuid "2615d33c-12ce-5201-84d1-8b5ec2754f69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "<" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5abd1c81-55cb-5df8-b5f1-f81a16167daf" :answer-field/id #uuid "5abd1c81-55cb-5df8-b5f1-f81a16167daf" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e0d1786b-ef7a-50ba-a2c1-15bd2b2e52bb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e0d1786b-ef7a-50ba-a2c1-15bd2b2e52bb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0d1786b-ef7a-50ba-a2c1-15bd2b2e52bb" :answer/id #uuid "e0d1786b-ef7a-50ba-a2c1-15bd2b2e52bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069133 :question/problem "Solve the absolute value inequality $1+|17-3h|\\le 9.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$h\\le $\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+|17-3h| & \\le 9 \\\\ |17-3h| & \\le 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $17-3h$ whose distance from zero is less than or equal to $8.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-8\\le 17-3h\\le 8\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods. (Remember that whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we have to flip the inequality signs.)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8\\le 17-3h & \\le 8 \\\\ -25\\le -3h & \\le -9 \\\\ 25\\ge 3h & \\ge 9 \\\\ \\frac{25}{3}\\ge h & \\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo make the final result easier to read, we will rewrite it so that the smaller number is on the left:\n\n$$\n3\\le h\\le \\frac{25}{3}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186066144 :answer-field/correct "answer-4e030f78-1887-5b4f-91f6-e9f93a8adee3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4e030f78-1887-5b4f-91f6-e9f93a8adee3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e030f78-1887-5b4f-91f6-e9f93a8adee3" :answer/id #uuid "4e030f78-1887-5b4f-91f6-e9f93a8adee3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186066143 :answer-field/correct "answer-c429898a-21b3-561e-94f6-8ff522fbd96c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c429898a-21b3-561e-94f6-8ff522fbd96c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c429898a-21b3-561e-94f6-8ff522fbd96c" :answer/id #uuid "c429898a-21b3-561e-94f6-8ff522fbd96c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\le" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186066145 :answer-field/correct "answer-16f3530a-e641-5d6c-b5f8-7527ed951265" :answer-field/choices ["answer-16f3530a-e641-5d6c-b5f8-7527ed951265"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16f3530a-e641-5d6c-b5f8-7527ed951265" :answer/id #uuid "16f3530a-e641-5d6c-b5f8-7527ed951265" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e891fb42-cafd-57b7-9773-6b5a4bb29926" :question/id #uuid "e891fb42-cafd-57b7-9773-6b5a4bb29926" :question/math-academy-id "q-29942" :question/problem "Solve the absolute value inequality $|4z+2|>6.$" :question/worked-solution "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $4z+2$ whose distance from zero is greater than $6.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4z+2 & <-6 & \\text{or} &  & 4z+2 & >6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve each inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4z+2 & <-6 &  &  & 4z+2 & >6 \\\\ 4z & <-8 &  &  & 4z & >4 \\\\ z & <-2 &  &  & z & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & <-2 & \\text{or} &  & z & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fdeb6dc8-1ec1-52bb-af0d-d8dc93ce7c2f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fdeb6dc8-1ec1-52bb-af0d-d8dc93ce7c2f" :answer-field/id #uuid "fdeb6dc8-1ec1-52bb-af0d-d8dc93ce7c2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-67885a2b-3782-5eff-9553-a065c134d72c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a14a9c3c-aba8-5d6f-a2ba-dea60b34d636" "answer-22bca241-56fc-57ff-91be-5e58e2055c04" "answer-67885a2b-3782-5eff-9553-a065c134d72c" "answer-79fd5220-180b-5a65-9715-68413d928a20" "answer-c8e7ca7d-647e-5b45-8cc4-20f7f026eba1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a14a9c3c-aba8-5d6f-a2ba-dea60b34d636" :answer/id #uuid "a14a9c3c-aba8-5d6f-a2ba-dea60b34d636" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1<z<1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22bca241-56fc-57ff-91be-5e58e2055c04" :answer/id #uuid "22bca241-56fc-57ff-91be-5e58e2055c04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6<z<2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67885a2b-3782-5eff-9553-a065c134d72c" :answer/id #uuid "67885a2b-3782-5eff-9553-a065c134d72c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$z<-2\\,$ or $\\,z>1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79fd5220-180b-5a65-9715-68413d928a20" :answer/id #uuid "79fd5220-180b-5a65-9715-68413d928a20" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$z<-2\\,$ or $\\,z>4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8e7ca7d-647e-5b45-8cc4-20f7f026eba1" :answer/id #uuid "c8e7ca7d-647e-5b45-8cc4-20f7f026eba1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$z<1\\,$ or $\\,z>6$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069135 :question/problem "Solve the absolute value inequality $|\\frac{5-9a}{2}|\\ge 7.$\n\n$a$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$\\,$ or $\\,a\\ge $\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $\\frac{5-9a}{2}$ whose distance from zero is greater than or equal to $7.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5-9a}{2} & \\le -7 & \\text{or} &  & \\frac{5-9a}{2} & \\ge 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve each inequality using the usual methods. (Remember that whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we have to flip the inequality signs.)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5-9a}{2} & \\le -7 &  &  & \\frac{5-9a}{2} & \\ge 7 \\\\ 5-9a & \\le -14 &  &  & 5-9a & \\ge 14 \\\\ -9a & \\le -19 &  &  & -9a & \\ge 9 \\\\ 9a & \\ge 19 &  &  & 9a & \\le -9 \\\\ a & \\ge \\frac{19}{9} &  &  & a & \\le -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & \\le -1 & \\text{or} &  & a & \\ge \\frac{19}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186066150 :answer-field/correct "answer-38782a03-b1f9-5e6d-8e0e-795314ea535c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-38782a03-b1f9-5e6d-8e0e-795314ea535c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38782a03-b1f9-5e6d-8e0e-795314ea535c" :answer/id #uuid "38782a03-b1f9-5e6d-8e0e-795314ea535c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\le" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186066151 :answer-field/correct "answer-56ae4ec4-c928-5d91-9771-f55187807779" :answer-field/choices ["answer-56ae4ec4-c928-5d91-9771-f55187807779"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56ae4ec4-c928-5d91-9771-f55187807779" :answer/id #uuid "56ae4ec4-c928-5d91-9771-f55187807779" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186066149 :answer-field/correct "answer-88027a38-f810-5e63-aba9-d53d0556696f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-88027a38-f810-5e63-aba9-d53d0556696f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88027a38-f810-5e63-aba9-d53d0556696f" :answer/id #uuid "88027a38-f810-5e63-aba9-d53d0556696f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{19}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0f90e4de-4957-59b8-990a-09a9092a26e8" :question/id #uuid "0f90e4de-4957-59b8-990a-09a9092a26e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-19350" :question/problem "Solve the absolute value inequality $|5x+2|\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $5x+2$ whose distance from zero is greater than or equal to $1.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2 & \\le -1 & \\text{or} &  & 5x+2 & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve each inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2 & \\le -1 &  &  & 5x+2 & \\ge 1 \\\\ 5x & \\le -3 &  &  & 5x & \\ge -1 \\\\ x & \\le -\\frac{3}{5} &  &  & x & \\ge -\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\le -\\frac{3}{5} & \\text{or} &  & x & \\ge -\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d8530bcb-8b8e-598a-ac17-38a2a589074a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d8530bcb-8b8e-598a-ac17-38a2a589074a" :answer-field/id #uuid "d8530bcb-8b8e-598a-ac17-38a2a589074a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-43ac02f0-bbf5-50c5-96dd-5aba08909745" :answer-field/choices ["answer-23f17c66-ee5f-5862-bbd9-df7e2337719f" "answer-43aad9b6-06b8-5090-a861-c22e239fefd7" "answer-43ac02f0-bbf5-50c5-96dd-5aba08909745" "answer-94a95ba2-27e3-51dd-94e1-78a5fe680a7d" "answer-44b0c010-8c4f-5f8b-9e9c-1cd9075b38eb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23f17c66-ee5f-5862-bbd9-df7e2337719f" :answer/id #uuid "23f17c66-ee5f-5862-bbd9-df7e2337719f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x\\le -2\\,$ or $\\,x\\ge 5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43aad9b6-06b8-5090-a861-c22e239fefd7" :answer/id #uuid "43aad9b6-06b8-5090-a861-c22e239fefd7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x\\le -4\\,$ or $\\,x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43ac02f0-bbf5-50c5-96dd-5aba08909745" :answer/id #uuid "43ac02f0-bbf5-50c5-96dd-5aba08909745" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x\\le -\\frac{3}{5}\\,$ or $\\,x\\ge -\\frac{1}{5}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94a95ba2-27e3-51dd-94e1-78a5fe680a7d" :answer/id #uuid "94a95ba2-27e3-51dd-94e1-78a5fe680a7d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x\\le 1\\,$ or $\\,x\\ge 2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44b0c010-8c4f-5f8b-9e9c-1cd9075b38eb" :answer/id #uuid "44b0c010-8c4f-5f8b-9e9c-1cd9075b38eb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x\\le \\frac{5}{2}\\,$ or $\\,x\\ge 4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4a81effc-bede-5f14-bdb7-3f40c0a9e067" :question/id #uuid "4a81effc-bede-5f14-bdb7-3f40c0a9e067" :question/math-academy-id "q-29943" :question/problem "Solve the absolute value inequality $|8-2z|>2.$" :question/worked-solution "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $8-2z$ whose distance from zero is greater than $2.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-2z & <-2 & \\text{or} &  & 8-2z & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve each inequality using the usual methods. (Remember that whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we have to flip the inequality signs.)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-2z & <-2 &  &  & 8-2z & >2 \\\\ -2z & <-10 &  &  & -2z & >-6 \\\\ z & >5 &  &  & z & <3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z<3 &  & \\text{or} &  & z>5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ee0ed7d8-cbd1-55a5-898d-36649e01d3f7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ee0ed7d8-cbd1-55a5-898d-36649e01d3f7" :answer-field/id #uuid "ee0ed7d8-cbd1-55a5-898d-36649e01d3f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0213c4e3-50ba-556f-ba2d-7eab3e633c74" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5099b5f9-89a1-57ab-b422-907c29d9b8cf" "answer-648b105b-a272-5e72-89b9-c740ccf253ba" "answer-dea0ca30-4acf-5241-b898-f1f35edf8ceb" "answer-0213c4e3-50ba-556f-ba2d-7eab3e633c74" "answer-953d6fbb-c6b7-5205-9208-c5a86b5d1c0f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5099b5f9-89a1-57ab-b422-907c29d9b8cf" :answer/id #uuid "5099b5f9-89a1-57ab-b422-907c29d9b8cf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$z<-2\\,$ or $\\,z>2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-648b105b-a272-5e72-89b9-c740ccf253ba" :answer/id #uuid "648b105b-a272-5e72-89b9-c740ccf253ba" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$z<-4\\,$ or $\\,z>-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dea0ca30-4acf-5241-b898-f1f35edf8ceb" :answer/id #uuid "dea0ca30-4acf-5241-b898-f1f35edf8ceb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$z<-8\\,$ or $\\,z>2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0213c4e3-50ba-556f-ba2d-7eab3e633c74" :answer/id #uuid "0213c4e3-50ba-556f-ba2d-7eab3e633c74" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$z<3\\,$ or $\\,z>5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-953d6fbb-c6b7-5205-9208-c5a86b5d1c0f" :answer/id #uuid "953d6fbb-c6b7-5205-9208-c5a86b5d1c0f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$z<4$ or $z>8$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186070026 :tutorial/content "**Absolute value inequalities** combine two basic concepts: absolute value and inequality. The absolute value of a number is its distance from zero, while an inequality specifies a range of values.\n\nConsider a simple absolute value inequality, as follows:\n\n$$\n|x| < 7\n$$\n\nThis inequality specifies every number $x$ that is less than a distance of $7$ from $0$. So, it represents the same thing as the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-7 < x < 7\n$$\n\nTo solve an absolute value inequality, we first convert it to a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. Then, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods.\n\nTo illustrate, let's solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n|2x+3| \\leq 5\n$$\n\nFirst, we interpret the inequality: the inequality specifies every number $2x+3$ whose distance from zero is less than or equal to $5.$ So, it represents the same thing as the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-5 \\leq 2x+3 \\leq 5\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\le 2x+3\\le 5 \\\\ -8\\le 2x\\le 2 \\\\ -4\\le x\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186070027 :tutorial/content "We take a similar approach for absolute value inequalities involving the \"greater than\" symbol. To illustrate, consider the following absolute value inequality:\n\n$$\n|x| \\geq 6\n$$\n\nTo solve this absolute value inequality, we convert it into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. The inequality specifies every number $x$ whose distance from zero is greater than or equal to $6.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\le -6 & \\text{or} &  & x & \\ge 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use the word \"or\" instead of \"and\" since only one of the inequalities $x\\leq-6$ or $x\\geq 6$ needs to be true for $|x| \\geq 6$ to be true." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068978 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110630 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c3360eb5-04da-59fb-81da-119f0aeb047a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c3360eb5-04da-59fb-81da-119f0aeb047a" :question/id #uuid "c3360eb5-04da-59fb-81da-119f0aeb047a" :question/math-academy-id "q-142529" :question/problem "![](images/96/96a76584c5ddbcc39d221334803c6791cb3b459178e70c310e4eca54a4e85462.png)\n\nFind an expression in terms of $x$ for the measure of the angle $∠AOE.$\n\n$∠AOE=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $∠AOD$ is a straight angle, the angles $∠AOE$ and $∠DOE$ are supplementary. Two angles are supplementary if their measures add up to ${180}^{∘}.$ \n\nSo, the measure of the angle $∠AOE$ that's supplementary to the angle of $∠DOE=5x-{5}^{∘}$ must be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠AOE & ={180}^{∘}-(5x-5)^{∘} \\\\  & =({180}^{∘}+{5}^{∘})-5{x}^{∘} \\\\  & ={(185-5x)}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-deda8d33-afb6-5faa-bc94-281563101948"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-deda8d33-afb6-5faa-bc94-281563101948" :answer-field/id #uuid "deda8d33-afb6-5faa-bc94-281563101948" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-bd13dfdd-ebf1-5572-a777-5c750dd784e2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bd13dfdd-ebf1-5572-a777-5c750dd784e2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd13dfdd-ebf1-5572-a777-5c750dd784e2" :answer/id #uuid "bd13dfdd-ebf1-5572-a777-5c750dd784e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "185-5x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105694 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c19df22d-0aea-5f35-9eda-6b27f1f826b3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c19df22d-0aea-5f35-9eda-6b27f1f826b3" :question/id #uuid "c19df22d-0aea-5f35-9eda-6b27f1f826b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-142509" :question/problem "If $AD=8,$ then $OC=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the points $A,C,$ and $D$ lie on the circle. We know that the radius of the circle is $r=OC.$\n\nWe first notice that\n\n$$\nAD=AO+OD.\n$$\n\nNow, $\\overset{―}{AO}$ and $\\overset{―}{OD}$ are both segments between the center of the circle and the circle itself. So, they are both radii of the circle, and because $AD=8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =8 \\\\ AO+OD & =8 \\\\ r+r & =8 \\\\ 2r & =8 \\\\ r & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nOC=r=4.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-09f71703-4d67-5e26-a308-758da21e5e6f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-09f71703-4d67-5e26-a308-758da21e5e6f" :answer-field/id #uuid "09f71703-4d67-5e26-a308-758da21e5e6f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-35f29ca2-0fa4-5b57-89ea-772c55ec32c8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-35f29ca2-0fa4-5b57-89ea-772c55ec32c8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35f29ca2-0fa4-5b57-89ea-772c55ec32c8" :answer/id #uuid "35f29ca2-0fa4-5b57-89ea-772c55ec32c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110759 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-99fbfa91-9f2d-531e-9a33-54900502faa9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-99fbfa91-9f2d-531e-9a33-54900502faa9" :question/id #uuid "99fbfa91-9f2d-531e-9a33-54900502faa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-142504" :question/problem "![](images/f3/f3f38ca1b55a12dcaffc5a52eb4f08fc6d6ec3d5c746cb8215ad3e0ed7671153.png)\n\nFind an expression in terms of $x$ for the measure of $∠COD.$\n\n$m∠COD=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $∠BOE$ is a straight angle, so\n\n$$\nm∠BOE={180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, the angle addition postulate gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOC+m∠COD+m∠DOE & =m∠BOE \\\\ (5x+5)^{∘}+m∠COD+(5x-5)^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 10{x}^{∘}+m∠COD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠COD & =(180-10x)^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e17cb2f5-7c1b-57d6-8e9f-2a843b07d5bd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e17cb2f5-7c1b-57d6-8e9f-2a843b07d5bd" :answer-field/id #uuid "e17cb2f5-7c1b-57d6-8e9f-2a843b07d5bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3f80b711-19ed-52be-8297-190fa8f36a8c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3f80b711-19ed-52be-8297-190fa8f36a8c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f80b711-19ed-52be-8297-190fa8f36a8c" :answer/id #uuid "3f80b711-19ed-52be-8297-190fa8f36a8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "180-10x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105738 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-839696c7-ab7c-5fbc-a053-f39b3696e2a3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-839696c7-ab7c-5fbc-a053-f39b3696e2a3" :question/id #uuid "839696c7-ab7c-5fbc-a053-f39b3696e2a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-142517" :question/problem "![](images/72/72c697336f9518e5be1c8b89d9244c0952a7b8f5e77f007638e6a9405d98d8fd.png)\n\nFind an expression in terms of $x$ for the measure of $∠ODC.$\n\n$m∠ODC=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\overset{―}{OC}$ and $\\overset{―}{OD}$ are radii of the circle, the triangle $△OCD$ is isosceles. Consequently, the base angles must have the same measure:\n\n$$\n∠OCD=∠ODC.\n$$\n\nAlso, from step 3, we know that $m∠COD={(180-10x)}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/50/509c6fd7ffabd5c4f7b71e89dd94cb29c374dd6d2d64407d65bcdef44553c60e.png)\n\nNow, since the internal angles of any triangle sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠ODC+m∠OCD+m∠COD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠ODC+(m∠ODC)+(180-10x)^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2m∠ODC-10{x}^{∘} & ={0}^{∘} \\\\ 2m∠ODC & =10{x}^{∘} \\\\ m∠ODC & =5{x}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $m∠ODC=5{x}^{∘}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d30ec5d2-7185-5122-ad13-5d37e66f9e20"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d30ec5d2-7185-5122-ad13-5d37e66f9e20" :answer-field/id #uuid "d30ec5d2-7185-5122-ad13-5d37e66f9e20" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-964ae523-a001-56a9-918a-6ed65ac952c3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-964ae523-a001-56a9-918a-6ed65ac952c3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-964ae523-a001-56a9-918a-6ed65ac952c3" :answer/id #uuid "964ae523-a001-56a9-918a-6ed65ac952c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192742 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c18e889b-e4ac-54b0-ba7c-c7c1982b19f4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c18e889b-e4ac-54b0-ba7c-c7c1982b19f4" :question/id #uuid "c18e889b-e4ac-54b0-ba7c-c7c1982b19f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-142519" :question/problem "![](images/82/828ac748b9d8c87e4a7a66dfddb2e926a3362830d551653260b5974b6bd2ca1a.png)\n\nGiven that the line segments $\\overset{―}{CD}$ and $\\overset{―}{AF}$ are parallel and $m∠FAO={(3x+18)}^{∘},$ find the measures of $∠FAO$ and $∠AOE.$\n\n$∠FAO=$\n{{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}$\n\n$∠AOE=$\n{{field-2}}\n${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "The parallel line segments $\\overset{―}{CD}∥\\overset{―}{AF}$ are cut by the transversal $\\overset{―}{AD}.$\n\nThe angles $∠ODC$ and $∠FAO$ are interior to the parallel line segments and are on opposite sides of the transversal. So, they are alternate interior angles and consequently, they have the same measure.\n\nIn step 4, we found that $m∠ODC=5{x}^{∘}.$\n\nSetting the measures of $∠ODC$ and $∠FAO$ equal to each other, we have an equation that we can solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠ODC & =m∠FAO \\\\ 5{x}^{∘} & =(3x+18)^{∘} \\\\ 5x & =3x+18 \\\\ 2x & =18 \\\\ x & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=9$ into the expressions for the measures of $∠FAO$ and $∠AOE$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠FAO & =(3x+18)^{∘} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 9+18)^{∘} \\\\  & ={45}^{∘} \\\\ m∠AOE & =(185-5x)^{∘} \\\\  & =(185-5\\cdot 9)^{∘} \\\\  & ={140}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $m∠FAO={45}^{∘}$ and $m∠AOE={140}^{∘}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3d4044b8-9993-5869-ad0e-3ffc963c1a2e" "answer-field-ca229fee-40f1-5f93-90d7-5d892f402841"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3d4044b8-9993-5869-ad0e-3ffc963c1a2e" :answer-field/id #uuid "3d4044b8-9993-5869-ad0e-3ffc963c1a2e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-527947cd-9a5e-5103-893d-eef7754c5992" :answer-field/choices ["answer-527947cd-9a5e-5103-893d-eef7754c5992"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-527947cd-9a5e-5103-893d-eef7754c5992" :answer/id #uuid "527947cd-9a5e-5103-893d-eef7754c5992" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "45" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ca229fee-40f1-5f93-90d7-5d892f402841" :answer-field/id #uuid "ca229fee-40f1-5f93-90d7-5d892f402841" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f1bd62cd-fffe-5e11-866e-8b077124e4eb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f1bd62cd-fffe-5e11-866e-8b077124e4eb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1bd62cd-fffe-5e11-866e-8b077124e4eb" :answer/id #uuid "f1bd62cd-fffe-5e11-866e-8b077124e4eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "140" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105718 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-db1a27df-5d87-50c3-9b60-42007a434557"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-db1a27df-5d87-50c3-9b60-42007a434557" :question/id #uuid "db1a27df-5d87-50c3-9b60-42007a434557" :question/math-academy-id "q-142717" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4ba93d3eb8944d086b001c0aa01cb28251705a6af9e55a9fd58e2affce12acaa.png)\n\nIn the quadrilateral $AOEF,$ the angle $∠E$ measures ${65}^{∘}.$ Then, $m∠F=${{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "A quadrilateral has $n=4$ sides, so the sum of its interior angles must be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom step 5, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & =m∠FAO={45}^{∘}, \\\\ m∠O & =m∠AOE={140}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/f6/f67441e3999a8b81e7ac63555c729f0f6c31bff015477efc3031364c6fb22003.png)\n\nAdding up the interior angles, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠O+m∠E+m∠F & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {45}^{∘}+{140}^{∘}+{65}^{∘}+m∠F & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {250}^{∘}+m∠F & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠F & ={110}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $m∠F={110}^{∘}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-792a9d88-7c6f-5b31-aae0-b4d4d44254ea"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-792a9d88-7c6f-5b31-aae0-b4d4d44254ea" :answer-field/id #uuid "792a9d88-7c6f-5b31-aae0-b4d4d44254ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9c4e7a13-93c7-5102-ac8c-81d508b2eeb1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9c4e7a13-93c7-5102-ac8c-81d508b2eeb1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c4e7a13-93c7-5102-ac8c-81d508b2eeb1" :answer/id #uuid "9c4e7a13-93c7-5102-ac8c-81d508b2eeb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "110" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "multistep-2da1acf2-080b-52d3-9b85-82ed9d0b8cb2" :multistep/id #uuid "2da1acf2-080b-52d3-9b85-82ed9d0b8cb2" :multistep/context "![](images/23/2364a0b3b1f379c1b091528ffeed46caafad8db153d63979e36ab9cc6b0a6c9d.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $O$ is the center of the circle, $∠AOD$ and $∠BOE$ are straight angles, $m∠BOC={(5x+5)}^{∘},$ and $m∠DOE={(5x-5)}^{∘}.$" :multistep/steps ["step-ce50a472-77e1-5d95-aaa6-ceb0722f1141" "step-36e26965-83de-5b3a-a837-f8ef84516946" "step-867555f1-c71e-5f0b-92e3-462ed0c033fd" "step-d96f6e55-4607-5688-9493-18aef8f0e341" "step-e0458b15-e3cd-5149-8fd1-535fa754ff89" "step-aa8ca3fb-cd47-59bb-82d0-c65e6c47f3c7"] :multistep/first-step "step-ce50a472-77e1-5d95-aaa6-ceb0722f1141" :db/ensure [:multistep/validate]} {:db/id "step-ce50a472-77e1-5d95-aaa6-ceb0722f1141" :step/id #uuid "ce50a472-77e1-5d95-aaa6-ceb0722f1141" :step/content "question-c3360eb5-04da-59fb-81da-119f0aeb047a" :step/next "step-36e26965-83de-5b3a-a837-f8ef84516946" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-36e26965-83de-5b3a-a837-f8ef84516946" :step/id #uuid "36e26965-83de-5b3a-a837-f8ef84516946" :step/content "question-c19df22d-0aea-5f35-9eda-6b27f1f826b3" :step/next "step-867555f1-c71e-5f0b-92e3-462ed0c033fd" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-867555f1-c71e-5f0b-92e3-462ed0c033fd" :step/id #uuid "867555f1-c71e-5f0b-92e3-462ed0c033fd" :step/content "question-99fbfa91-9f2d-531e-9a33-54900502faa9" :step/next "step-d96f6e55-4607-5688-9493-18aef8f0e341" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d96f6e55-4607-5688-9493-18aef8f0e341" :step/id #uuid "d96f6e55-4607-5688-9493-18aef8f0e341" :step/content "question-839696c7-ab7c-5fbc-a053-f39b3696e2a3" :step/next "step-e0458b15-e3cd-5149-8fd1-535fa754ff89" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e0458b15-e3cd-5149-8fd1-535fa754ff89" :step/id #uuid "e0458b15-e3cd-5149-8fd1-535fa754ff89" :step/content "question-c18e889b-e4ac-54b0-ba7c-c7c1982b19f4" :step/next "step-aa8ca3fb-cd47-59bb-82d0-c65e6c47f3c7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-aa8ca3fb-cd47-59bb-82d0-c65e6c47f3c7" :step/id #uuid "aa8ca3fb-cd47-59bb-82d0-c65e6c47f3c7" :step/content "question-db1a27df-5d87-50c3-9b60-42007a434557" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "assigned-problem-c0924dd4-d44b-56a7-9578-fec2204b0185" :assigned-problem/id #uuid "c0924dd4-d44b-56a7-9578-fec2204b0185" :assigned-problem/content "multistep-2da1acf2-080b-52d3-9b85-82ed9d0b8cb2" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [17592186048764 17592186049856 17592186048174 17592186049027 17592186049376 17592186049223] :db/ensure [:assigned-problem/validate]} {:db/id "activity-c5317150-479c-5e7c-86c3-dd80600c2591" :activity/id #uuid "c5317150-479c-5e7c-86c3-dd80600c2591" :activity/title "Lines and Angles in Circles" :activity/type :activity.type/assignment :activity/steps ["step-8497e842-c3b2-58b8-aae2-6d721a1570af"] :activity/first-step "step-8497e842-c3b2-58b8-aae2-6d721a1570af" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-8497e842-c3b2-58b8-aae2-6d721a1570af" :step/id #uuid "8497e842-c3b2-58b8-aae2-6d721a1570af" :step/content "assigned-problem-c0924dd4-d44b-56a7-9578-fec2204b0185" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186155667 :answer-field/choices 17592186155668] [:db/retract 17592186155667 :answer-field/choices 17592186155669] [:db/retract 17592186155667 :answer-field/choices 17592186155670] [:db/retract 17592186155667 :answer-field/choices 17592186155671] [:db/retract 17592186155667 :answer-field/choices 17592186155672] {:db/id 17592186157791 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-y & -x\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-y & -x\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}-y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-y)=6x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-y)=-1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}6x & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186155667 :answer-field/correct "answer-7cfd7658-f969-5d24-b54b-e78400ec350a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-42ad8ac2-0cae-5d02-b554-16bcbaa98c5d" "answer-9734b10a-54a0-50b1-8bf9-29f1e1b884d0" "answer-77498169-1361-5d9a-8c47-6032457435c9" "answer-7cfd7658-f969-5d24-b54b-e78400ec350a" "answer-1845ba91-aa39-523c-b7fb-24b0540f22a4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42ad8ac2-0cae-5d02-b554-16bcbaa98c5d" :answer/id #uuid "42ad8ac2-0cae-5d02-b554-16bcbaa98c5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -x\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9734b10a-54a0-50b1-8bf9-29f1e1b884d0" :answer/id #uuid "9734b10a-54a0-50b1-8bf9-29f1e1b884d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-y & -1 \\\\ -1 & -x\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77498169-1361-5d9a-8c47-6032457435c9" :answer/id #uuid "77498169-1361-5d9a-8c47-6032457435c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-y & -x \\\\ -x & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7cfd7658-f969-5d24-b54b-e78400ec350a" :answer/id #uuid "7cfd7658-f969-5d24-b54b-e78400ec350a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6x & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1845ba91-aa39-523c-b7fb-24b0540f22a4" :answer/id #uuid "1845ba91-aa39-523c-b7fb-24b0540f22a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6x & -x \\\\ -x & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157425 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b2d69fc5-a88d-5940-a2a9-d7cc001b13e7" "question-46310383-5e9b-5711-9143-446211dee40a" "question-0614088a-4b37-5f45-a256-f7dd1a417f0a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b2d69fc5-a88d-5940-a2a9-d7cc001b13e7" :question/id #uuid "b2d69fc5-a88d-5940-a2a9-d7cc001b13e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-81033" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(1,\\pi )$ for the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(1,\\pi ).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,\\pi )=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dc17abaf-f11c-5b72-9671-ca1fd6cb7d81"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dc17abaf-f11c-5b72-9671-ca1fd6cb7d81" :answer-field/id #uuid "dc17abaf-f11c-5b72-9671-ca1fd6cb7d81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5c6f1e5c-0d83-5707-9be7-6186e6c7f868" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ba9c170b-091c-52e8-887d-30f59a09cad0" "answer-62703d69-369b-5ace-a8d1-0c23e81a72c7" "answer-5c6f1e5c-0d83-5707-9be7-6186e6c7f868" "answer-8a6bad3b-8668-59c6-b072-43c794a5f205" "answer-359a8021-5740-5c7d-85ad-b70c09368f55"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba9c170b-091c-52e8-887d-30f59a09cad0" :answer/id #uuid "ba9c170b-091c-52e8-887d-30f59a09cad0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62703d69-369b-5ace-a8d1-0c23e81a72c7" :answer/id #uuid "62703d69-369b-5ace-a8d1-0c23e81a72c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c6f1e5c-0d83-5707-9be7-6186e6c7f868" :answer/id #uuid "5c6f1e5c-0d83-5707-9be7-6186e6c7f868" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a6bad3b-8668-59c6-b072-43c794a5f205" :answer/id #uuid "8a6bad3b-8668-59c6-b072-43c794a5f205" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-359a8021-5740-5c7d-85ad-b70c09368f55" :answer/id #uuid "359a8021-5740-5c7d-85ad-b70c09368f55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-46310383-5e9b-5711-9143-446211dee40a" :question/id #uuid "46310383-5e9b-5711-9143-446211dee40a" :question/math-academy-id "q-80945" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y)) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y) & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y))=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y))=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y) \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y))=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y))=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a8dc6b69-1ae0-561f-a21b-1f490d47e054"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a8dc6b69-1ae0-561f-a21b-1f490d47e054" :answer-field/id #uuid "a8dc6b69-1ae0-561f-a21b-1f490d47e054" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-df6ec264-721c-5c32-b562-4dd7d6f6e9fb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9df6896f-980a-5433-b50d-177ed45dbdf5" "answer-81e448c4-be6f-5b7c-9911-58f7b720cb69" "answer-7e106318-5cd1-562e-bb83-a7e1e65c6c69" "answer-df6ec264-721c-5c32-b562-4dd7d6f6e9fb" "answer-3317f54b-1416-55a3-8ca3-d53a13390c95"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9df6896f-980a-5433-b50d-177ed45dbdf5" :answer/id #uuid "9df6896f-980a-5433-b50d-177ed45dbdf5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81e448c4-be6f-5b7c-9911-58f7b720cb69" :answer/id #uuid "81e448c4-be6f-5b7c-9911-58f7b720cb69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e106318-5cd1-562e-bb83-a7e1e65c6c69" :answer/id #uuid "7e106318-5cd1-562e-bb83-a7e1e65c6c69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df6ec264-721c-5c32-b562-4dd7d6f6e9fb" :answer/id #uuid "df6ec264-721c-5c32-b562-4dd7d6f6e9fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3317f54b-1416-55a3-8ca3-d53a13390c95" :answer/id #uuid "3317f54b-1416-55a3-8ca3-d53a13390c95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0614088a-4b37-5f45-a256-f7dd1a417f0a" :question/id #uuid "0614088a-4b37-5f45-a256-f7dd1a417f0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-80943" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7c34ea07-7e19-56a7-ba03-b874c7a7954b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7c34ea07-7e19-56a7-ba03-b874c7a7954b" :answer-field/id #uuid "7c34ea07-7e19-56a7-ba03-b874c7a7954b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f08cd101-4751-528e-9c91-8bd8e073764f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-88aa9963-0fec-593b-849b-6f365aef81b0" "answer-af47d397-3313-5f02-895b-4079622ccc89" "answer-695ca77d-0c24-5a11-989b-13dbb21f08ac" "answer-9b60e2a2-ee1f-5fb2-9669-c7f3d20a997a" "answer-f08cd101-4751-528e-9c91-8bd8e073764f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88aa9963-0fec-593b-849b-6f365aef81b0" :answer/id #uuid "88aa9963-0fec-593b-849b-6f365aef81b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af47d397-3313-5f02-895b-4079622ccc89" :answer/id #uuid "af47d397-3313-5f02-895b-4079622ccc89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-695ca77d-0c24-5a11-989b-13dbb21f08ac" :answer/id #uuid "695ca77d-0c24-5a11-989b-13dbb21f08ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ -{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b60e2a2-ee1f-5fb2-9669-c7f3d20a997a" :answer/id #uuid "9b60e2a2-ee1f-5fb2-9669-c7f3d20a997a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & \\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f08cd101-4751-528e-9c91-8bd8e073764f" :answer/id #uuid "f08cd101-4751-528e-9c91-8bd8e073764f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157424 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-223797a4-3fdc-5efb-a9f2-b733398cd415"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-223797a4-3fdc-5efb-a9f2-b733398cd415" :question/id #uuid "223797a4-3fdc-5efb-a9f2-b733398cd415" :question/math-academy-id "q-80946" :question/problem "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & {e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}],$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & {e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & -{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ -{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & {e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=(-1)\\cdot 1-{0}^{2}=-1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e7c15a59-5adc-5127-8074-bf8ca6a5dc2b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e7c15a59-5adc-5127-8074-bf8ca6a5dc2b" :answer-field/id #uuid "e7c15a59-5adc-5127-8074-bf8ca6a5dc2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-81decfb0-353d-554c-a766-c2d475210694" :answer-field/choices ["answer-81decfb0-353d-554c-a766-c2d475210694" "answer-c08ac167-4934-5d02-a974-ed7fd8f64367" "answer-06acb265-9aa4-5e5c-afdc-84b8445440f4" "answer-17e65082-0896-55d8-99e6-6536ee35ed4d" "answer-9f7d0305-07ee-5c49-a171-c47f7420e291"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81decfb0-353d-554c-a766-c2d475210694" :answer/id #uuid "81decfb0-353d-554c-a766-c2d475210694" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c08ac167-4934-5d02-a974-ed7fd8f64367" :answer/id #uuid "c08ac167-4934-5d02-a974-ed7fd8f64367" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06acb265-9aa4-5e5c-afdc-84b8445440f4" :answer/id #uuid "06acb265-9aa4-5e5c-afdc-84b8445440f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17e65082-0896-55d8-99e6-6536ee35ed4d" :answer/id #uuid "17e65082-0896-55d8-99e6-6536ee35ed4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f7d0305-07ee-5c49-a171-c47f7420e291" :answer/id #uuid "9f7d0305-07ee-5c49-a171-c47f7420e291" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186100545 :answer-field/choices 17592186100549] [:db/retract 17592186100185 :answer-field/choices 17592186100186] [:db/retract 17592186100185 :answer-field/choices 17592186100187] [:db/retract 17592186100185 :answer-field/choices 17592186100188] [:db/retract 17592186100185 :answer-field/choices 17592186100189] [:db/retract 17592186100185 :answer-field/choices 17592186100190] {:db/id 17592186101017 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bb428e79-eefe-5650-9407-e82260832c0f" "question-3f837bd1-8f94-5b36-a5b6-46a5ca94e710" "question-300985dc-198c-5051-b5a4-3809b4e56dd0" "question-626a28da-89e6-52c5-8bca-1f554918b829" "question-c7844b31-dcef-5082-a108-686c79e5bf57"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100661 :question/problem "The director of a newspaper estimates that the company's profit, in hundreds of dollars, is given by \n\n$$\nP(x) = -x^2 + 44x + 5,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x$ is the number of newspapers, in thousands, produced daily. Given that the company has the capacity to produce $30\\,000$ newspapers every day, how many newspapers per day should the company produce to maximize profit?" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the permitted domain is \n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 30,\n$$\n\n since $x = 30$ corresponds to $30 \\cdot 1\\,000 = 30\\,000$ copies of the newspaper. \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0 <       x <       30.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nP'(x) = -2x + 44,\n$$\n\nand solving $P'(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ -2x+44 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =44 \\\\ x & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=22$ lies in the interval $0 \\leq x \\leq 30.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=22$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\nP''(x) = -2 < 0.\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=22$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=22$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the company should produce $22 \\cdot 1\\,000 = 22\\,000$ copies of the newspaper per day to maximize its profit." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101018 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-83c84e0b-efbe-5ed1-89f6-e89dc6ca66e5" "question-13ee04f2-9803-50aa-b0c0-1f54d4ebdeb4" "question-397e9b41-cb22-58e3-8777-87698586bfce" "question-1ae7f739-7e2b-55d5-873a-c21108d944ca" "question-a0e359a4-15e8-5a4f-83b1-ea0f9d507e49"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100788 :question/problem "A textile center sells $x$ hundreds of meters of fabric at a price $p(x),$ in dollars, given by\n\n$$\np(x) = x^2 - 15x + 48 + \\dfrac{78}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the textile center sells between $100$ and $900$ meters of fabric daily, how many meters of fabric must the textile center sell to maximize its revenue?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ hundreds of meters in total, the total revenue $R(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =x\\cdot p(x) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-15x+48+\\frac{78}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+48x+78.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that the permitted domain is $1 \\leq x \\leq 9,$ since $x = 1$ corresponds to $1 \\cdot 100 = 100$ and $x = 9$ corresponds to $9 \\cdot 100 = 900.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=9$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $1 <         x <         9.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nR'(x) = 3x^2 - 30x + 48,\n$$\n\nand solving $R'(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-30x+48 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+16 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-8) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=2$ and $x=8.$\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,2, 8$ and $9.$ Computing $R(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =(1)^{3}-15(1)^{2}+48(1)+78=112 \\\\ R(2) & =(2)^{3}-15(2)^{2}+48(2)+78=122\\,✓ \\\\ R(8) & =(8)^{3}-15(8)^{2}+48(8)+78=14 \\\\ R(9) & =(9)^{3}-15(9)^{2}+48(9)+78=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize its revenue, the textile center should sell $2 \\cdot 100 = 200$ meters of fabric." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101019 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-896ab313-4af7-5cfc-9d60-946032fe5a62" "question-de3d69db-3561-564d-a214-9ac683352f20" "question-a7b5b31e-6d04-57eb-9051-cd4496c07c09"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100660 :question/worked-solution "First, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per month, the company has a monthly revenue (income) of $R(x)=180\\cdot x=180 x$ dollars. \nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =180x-({x}^{2}+80x) \\\\  & =100x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the question statement, we know that the permitted domain is $0\\leq x \\leq 60.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0 < x < 60.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nP'(x)=100-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving $P'(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 100-2x & =0 \\\\ x & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=50$ lies in the interval $0 <  x < 60.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=50$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\nP''(x) = -2 <           0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=50$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=50$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the maximum monthly profit is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(50) & =100(50)-(50)^{2}=\\text{\\[}math]2\\,500.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101020 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2888ed58-7e1b-57a5-91ea-2124b7fe040e" "question-8babed00-8ea0-55fe-9919-358ba07b0746" "question-eefd8d60-720e-5861-a3ae-27d7c6377bff" "question-244652f5-cacd-5369-8e7e-0fc003669027"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100784 :question/problem "A hotel has $21$ rooms. The manager has observed that when the room rate is $\\[math]12$ per night,  all rooms are occupied and, for every $\\[/math]1$ increase in the rate, a single room is vacated. If the maintenance of each rented room is $\\[math]3$ per night, what rate per room should the manager set to obtain the maximum profit, and what is the maximum profit?" :question/worked-solution "First, note that if there are $x$ *vacant* rooms, the hotel has $(21-x)$ occupied rooms, and the rate per room is $(12+x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in dollars) is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(21-x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{occupied rooms}}\\times \\,\\,\\,{{(12+x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{rate per room}} \\\\  & =252+21x-12x-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =252+9x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for having $(21-x)$ rooms rented out, the hotel has a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =3(21-x) \\\\  & =63-3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\ P(x) & =(252+9x-{x}^{2})-(63-3x) \\\\  & =189+12x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are $21$ rooms, the permitted domain is $0 \\leq x\\leq 21.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0 \\lt x\\lt 21.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nP'(x) = 12 - 2x \\,.\n$$\n\nand solving $P'(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 12-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =12 \\\\ x & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=6$ lies in the interval $0 <  x < 21.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=6$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\nP''(x) = -2 <             0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=6$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=6$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the hotel will have a maximum profit when there are $x=6$ vacant rooms.  \n\nWe know that the rate per room is $(12+x)$ dollars. If there are $6$ vacant rooms, then the rate per room is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12+6 & =\\text{\\[}math]18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the manager should charge a room rate of $\\[/math]18$ per night." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-bb428e79-eefe-5650-9407-e82260832c0f" :question/id #uuid "bb428e79-eefe-5650-9407-e82260832c0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-346953" :question/problem "The cost for a company to produce $x$ bicycles, in hundreds of dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nC(x)={x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+48x+300.\n$$\n\nGiven that the company can produce at most $10$ bicycles per week, how many bicycles should the company produce per week to minimize its weekly cost?\n\nThe company should produce\n{{field-1}}\nbicycles per week to minimize its weekly cost." :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ represent the number of bicycles produced per week. Since the company can produce at most $10$ bicycles per week, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 10.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $C(x)$ for $0<x<10.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-30x+48,\n$$\n\nand solving ${C}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-30x+48 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-10x+16) & =0 \\\\ 3(x-2)(x-8) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to find which of $x=2$ or $x=8$ gives a minimum value of $C(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(x)=6x-30\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ and $x=8$ into ${C}^{″}(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{″}(2) & =12-30=-18<0, \\\\ {C}^{″}(8) & =48-30=18>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive at $x=8,$ the point $x=8$ is a local minimum of $C(x).$\n\nOur candidates are $x=0,$ $x=8$ and $x=10.$ Computing $C(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(0) & =300 \\\\ C(8) & =(8)^{3}-15(8)^{2}+48(8)+300=236\\,✓ \\\\ C(10) & =(10)^{3}-15(10)^{2}+48(10)+300=280\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=8$ is the global minimum of $C(x),$ and the company should produce $8$ bicycles per week to minimize weekly cost." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cecc26af-aa31-562c-ae17-33a4a09196f3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cecc26af-aa31-562c-ae17-33a4a09196f3" :answer-field/id #uuid "cecc26af-aa31-562c-ae17-33a4a09196f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-60260afa-81b2-5401-a999-9c5593207039" :answer-field/choices ["answer-60260afa-81b2-5401-a999-9c5593207039"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60260afa-81b2-5401-a999-9c5593207039" :answer/id #uuid "60260afa-81b2-5401-a999-9c5593207039" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3f837bd1-8f94-5b36-a5b6-46a5ca94e710" :question/id #uuid "3f837bd1-8f94-5b36-a5b6-46a5ca94e710" :question/math-academy-id "q-346940" :question/problem "The manager of a bakery estimates that the profit of the bakery, in dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nP(x)=-2{x}^{2}+120x-1\\,500,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the number of loaves baked daily. Given that the bakery can bake at most $400$ loaves per day, how many loaves should the bakery bake per day to maximize its daily profit?\n\nThe bakery should bake\n{{field-1}} loaves per day to maximize its daily profit." :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ represents the number of loaves baked daily and the bakery can bake at most $400$ loaves per day, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 400.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<400.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=-4x+120,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ -4x+120 & =0 \\\\ x & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=30$ lies in the interval $0<x<400.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=30$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-4<0.\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=30$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=30$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$\n\nTherefore, the bakery should bake $30$ loaves per day to maximize its daily profit." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-96e90f42-a40e-5675-933f-77aa92860dd4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-96e90f42-a40e-5675-933f-77aa92860dd4" :answer-field/id #uuid "96e90f42-a40e-5675-933f-77aa92860dd4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ce0f7359-5ce8-5e71-89e3-480ebd8e48f6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ce0f7359-5ce8-5e71-89e3-480ebd8e48f6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce0f7359-5ce8-5e71-89e3-480ebd8e48f6" :answer/id #uuid "ce0f7359-5ce8-5e71-89e3-480ebd8e48f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-300985dc-198c-5051-b5a4-3809b4e56dd0" :question/id #uuid "300985dc-198c-5051-b5a4-3809b4e56dd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-119010" :question/problem "A coffee producer company estimates that the profit of its coffee production, in dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nP(x)=-0.002{x}^{2}+3.9x-3,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ are the tens of kilograms of coffee produced daily. Given that the company can produce at most $10\\,000$ kilograms per day, how many kilograms should the company produce per day to maximize its daily profit?" :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ represents the number of tens of kilograms of coffee produced daily, and since the company produces at most $10\\,000$ kilograms per day, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 1\\,000.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<1\\,000.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=-0.004x+3.9,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ -0.004x+3.9 & =0 \\\\ x & =975\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=975$ lies in the interval $0<x<1\\,000.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=975$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-0.004<0.\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=975$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=975$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the company should produce $975\\cdot 10=9\\,750$ kilograms of coffee per day to maximize its daily profit." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-96c86f48-8fdd-5608-aed8-3ae8938afdea"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-96c86f48-8fdd-5608-aed8-3ae8938afdea" :answer-field/id #uuid "96c86f48-8fdd-5608-aed8-3ae8938afdea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-25bc201d-66ea-53fc-9b0e-c3e2d40079a9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-39f3400f-def2-582b-b868-8b4f355233c3" "answer-a36a5800-821a-5eb0-99da-dfc32bfe6ada" "answer-cb5e05a4-3c93-5c87-834b-f68d2820cbc0" "answer-f1c37437-ce26-5498-8d60-f163e1765a66" "answer-25bc201d-66ea-53fc-9b0e-c3e2d40079a9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39f3400f-def2-582b-b868-8b4f355233c3" :answer/id #uuid "39f3400f-def2-582b-b868-8b4f355233c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a36a5800-821a-5eb0-99da-dfc32bfe6ada" :answer/id #uuid "a36a5800-821a-5eb0-99da-dfc32bfe6ada" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "375" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb5e05a4-3c93-5c87-834b-f68d2820cbc0" :answer/id #uuid "cb5e05a4-3c93-5c87-834b-f68d2820cbc0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,900" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1c37437-ce26-5498-8d60-f163e1765a66" :answer/id #uuid "f1c37437-ce26-5498-8d60-f163e1765a66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25bc201d-66ea-53fc-9b0e-c3e2d40079a9" :answer/id #uuid "25bc201d-66ea-53fc-9b0e-c3e2d40079a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\,750" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-626a28da-89e6-52c5-8bca-1f554918b829" :question/id #uuid "626a28da-89e6-52c5-8bca-1f554918b829" :question/math-academy-id "q-346947" :question/problem "Upon launching a new online course, the chief financial officer (CFO) of a company estimates that its profit, in thousands of dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nP(x)=0.3{x}^{3}-3.6{x}^{2}+10.8x+5,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the price, in dollars, of a single course. If the CFO estimates that the course price should be between $$1$ and $$7,$ at what price is the maximum profit obtained?\n\nThe profit is maximum when a single course costs $$$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ represents the price of a single course and since the CFO estimates that the course price should be between $$1$ and $$7,$  the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 7.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $1<x<7.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=0.9{x}^{2}-7.2x+10.8.\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 0.9{x}^{2}-7.2x+10.8 & =0 \\\\ 0.9({x}^{2}-8x+12) & =0 \\\\ 0.9(x-2)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to find which of $x=2$ or $x=6$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=1.8x-7.2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ and $x=6$ into ${P}^{″}(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(2) & =1.8(2)-7.2=-3.6<0, \\\\ {P}^{″}(6) & =1.8(6)-7.2=3.6>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative at $x=2,$ the point $x=2$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,$ $x=2$ and $x=7.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =0.3(1)^{3}-3.6(1)^{2}+10.8(1)+5=12.5 \\\\ P(2) & =0.3(2)^{3}-3.6(2)^{2}+10.8(2)+5=14.6\\,✓ \\\\ P(7) & =0.3(7)^{3}-3.6(7)^{2}+10.8(7)+5=7.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is the global maximum of $P(x).$ So, the profit is maximum when a single course costs $$\\,2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-612e0cf9-0fec-5ac7-8aa7-de4c56b9b84c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-612e0cf9-0fec-5ac7-8aa7-de4c56b9b84c" :answer-field/id #uuid "612e0cf9-0fec-5ac7-8aa7-de4c56b9b84c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6fa2cd2b-240f-554e-bc3d-a20b62887645" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6fa2cd2b-240f-554e-bc3d-a20b62887645"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6fa2cd2b-240f-554e-bc3d-a20b62887645" :answer/id #uuid "6fa2cd2b-240f-554e-bc3d-a20b62887645" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c7844b31-dcef-5082-a108-686c79e5bf57" :question/id #uuid "c7844b31-dcef-5082-a108-686c79e5bf57" :question/math-academy-id "q-15324" :question/problem "A fast-food chain wants to open some new restaurants. The company estimates that its annual profit, in hundreds of thousands of dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-12x+20,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the number of new restaurants it opens up. If the company has a budget to open up between $1$ and $8$ restaurants, how many restaurants should the company open to maximize its profit?" :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ represents the number of  new restaurants opened, and since the company can open between $1$ and $8$ restaurants, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 8.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $1<x<8.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=-{x}^{2}+8x-12.\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+8x-12 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-8x+12) & =0 \\\\ -(x-2)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to find which of $x=2$ or $x=6$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-2x+8\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ and $x=6$ into ${P}^{″}(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(2) & =-2(2)+8=4>0, \\\\ {P}^{″}(6) & =-2(6)+8=-4<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative at $x=6,$ the point $x=6$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,$ $x=6$ and $x=8.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =-\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}+4(1)^{2}-12(1)+20=\\frac{35}{3}≈12 \\\\ P(6) & =-\\frac{1}{3}(6)^{3}+4(6)^{2}-12(6)+20=20\\,✓ \\\\ P(8) & =-\\frac{1}{3}(8)^{3}+4(8)^{2}-12(8)+20=\\frac{28}{3}≈9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=6$ is the global maximum of $P(x).$ So, the company must open $6$ restaurants to maximize its profit." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7f434994-c85a-56bb-adb3-a969e9d9b29b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7f434994-c85a-56bb-adb3-a969e9d9b29b" :answer-field/id #uuid "7f434994-c85a-56bb-adb3-a969e9d9b29b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e83b28f6-aa90-51cc-9eba-ba8f1cc6231d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c0858bb9-0eb9-573d-9bea-d595cf1d7421" "answer-f36cae25-67e6-5172-9f1f-88f47f2ea096" "answer-3d97af0b-7e54-5d34-9126-07d44330312c" "answer-e83b28f6-aa90-51cc-9eba-ba8f1cc6231d" "answer-dc8ead9f-c9de-5346-ac44-db1b6b8f2faf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0858bb9-0eb9-573d-9bea-d595cf1d7421" :answer/id #uuid "c0858bb9-0eb9-573d-9bea-d595cf1d7421" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f36cae25-67e6-5172-9f1f-88f47f2ea096" :answer/id #uuid "f36cae25-67e6-5172-9f1f-88f47f2ea096" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d97af0b-7e54-5d34-9126-07d44330312c" :answer/id #uuid "3d97af0b-7e54-5d34-9126-07d44330312c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e83b28f6-aa90-51cc-9eba-ba8f1cc6231d" :answer/id #uuid "e83b28f6-aa90-51cc-9eba-ba8f1cc6231d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc8ead9f-c9de-5346-ac44-db1b6b8f2faf" :answer/id #uuid "dc8ead9f-c9de-5346-ac44-db1b6b8f2faf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-83c84e0b-efbe-5ed1-89f6-e89dc6ca66e5" :question/id #uuid "83c84e0b-efbe-5ed1-89f6-e89dc6ca66e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-119028" :question/problem "A school is organizing a trip to a theme park. The trip is offered to at least $12$ kids, and it can be attended by at most $20$ kids. The cost of each ticket is $$80$ if there are exactly $12$ kids. If there are more than $12$ kids, the price per ticket drops by $$4$ for each kid in excess of $12.$ Every kid pays the same amount for going on the trip.\n\nHow many kids must go on the trip for the theme park to maximize its revenue?" :question/worked-solution "First, note that if there are $12+x$ kids, then the price per ticket is $80-4x.$ So, the total revenue is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(12+x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{number of kids}}\\times \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{(80-4x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{price per ticket}} \\\\  & =(12+x)(80-4x) \\\\  & =960-48x+80x-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =960+32x-4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there can only be between $12$ and $20$ kids, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & \\le x+12\\le 20 \\\\ 0 & \\le x\\le 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=8$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $0<x<8.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=32-8x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 32-8x & =0 \\\\ 8x & =32 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=4$ lies in the interval $0<x<8.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=4$ does indeed give a maximum value of $R(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{R}^{″}(x)=-8<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=4$ is indeed a local maximum of $R(x).$ And since $R(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=4$ is a global maximum of $R(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the theme park should admit $12+4=16$ kids to maximize its revenue." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-732a4786-9390-5f34-af22-8bd5a4c708b1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-732a4786-9390-5f34-af22-8bd5a4c708b1" :answer-field/id #uuid "732a4786-9390-5f34-af22-8bd5a4c708b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2dde279e-504b-5975-b666-d6e44cfae064" :answer-field/choices ["answer-469492a5-d663-54f3-a329-e35b77e8e445" "answer-2dde279e-504b-5975-b666-d6e44cfae064" "answer-e826da33-9921-51cd-a0e7-44f6662e2dbf" "answer-fbf1c2ad-b715-5a9e-82cc-eb26e807f1e8" "answer-9d81dc2a-f44f-5831-8528-42073dd5e870"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-469492a5-d663-54f3-a329-e35b77e8e445" :answer/id #uuid "469492a5-d663-54f3-a329-e35b77e8e445" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2dde279e-504b-5975-b666-d6e44cfae064" :answer/id #uuid "2dde279e-504b-5975-b666-d6e44cfae064" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e826da33-9921-51cd-a0e7-44f6662e2dbf" :answer/id #uuid "e826da33-9921-51cd-a0e7-44f6662e2dbf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbf1c2ad-b715-5a9e-82cc-eb26e807f1e8" :answer/id #uuid "fbf1c2ad-b715-5a9e-82cc-eb26e807f1e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d81dc2a-f44f-5831-8528-42073dd5e870" :answer/id #uuid "9d81dc2a-f44f-5831-8528-42073dd5e870" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-13ee04f2-9803-50aa-b0c0-1f54d4ebdeb4" :question/id #uuid "13ee04f2-9803-50aa-b0c0-1f54d4ebdeb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-87569" :question/problem "An auto parts store sells $x$ units of a certain product at a price per unit $p(x),$ in dollars, given by\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2}-15x+63+\\frac{11}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the store sells between $1$ and $6$ units of the product each day, how many units should the store sell to maximize its revenue?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units in total, the total revenue $R(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =x\\cdot p(x) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-15x+63+\\frac{11}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+63x+11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 6.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=6$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $1<x<6.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-30x+63,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-30x+63 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+21 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)(x-7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=3$ and $x=7.$ However, the value $x=7$ is not in the permitted domain, so the only new candidate is $x=3.$\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,3$ and $6.$ Computing $R(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =(1)^{3}-15(1)^{2}+63(1)+11=60 \\\\ R(3) & =(3)^{3}-15(3)^{2}+63(3)+11=92\\,✓ \\\\ R(6) & =(6)^{3}-15(6)^{2}+63(6)+11=65\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize its revenue, the auto parts store should sell $3$ units." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-17a706a9-a55b-54d0-8173-3f2f3ca6d82c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-17a706a9-a55b-54d0-8173-3f2f3ca6d82c" :answer-field/id #uuid "17a706a9-a55b-54d0-8173-3f2f3ca6d82c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0e7b3e86-4e95-5add-a8e0-4126a0921329" :answer-field/choices ["answer-72f9ab55-9f87-5491-a690-8052899d1b7f" "answer-0e7b3e86-4e95-5add-a8e0-4126a0921329" "answer-9a52c40d-9a2e-5a1c-8e75-2faaa165db38" "answer-df333b26-81a8-5312-8a45-e34486c7331f" "answer-c6b730d7-1e7f-50fd-818e-0b1d5da48b33"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72f9ab55-9f87-5491-a690-8052899d1b7f" :answer/id #uuid "72f9ab55-9f87-5491-a690-8052899d1b7f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e7b3e86-4e95-5add-a8e0-4126a0921329" :answer/id #uuid "0e7b3e86-4e95-5add-a8e0-4126a0921329" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a52c40d-9a2e-5a1c-8e75-2faaa165db38" :answer/id #uuid "9a52c40d-9a2e-5a1c-8e75-2faaa165db38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df333b26-81a8-5312-8a45-e34486c7331f" :answer/id #uuid "df333b26-81a8-5312-8a45-e34486c7331f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6b730d7-1e7f-50fd-818e-0b1d5da48b33" :answer/id #uuid "c6b730d7-1e7f-50fd-818e-0b1d5da48b33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-397e9b41-cb22-58e3-8777-87698586bfce" :question/id #uuid "397e9b41-cb22-58e3-8777-87698586bfce" :question/math-academy-id "q-119021" :question/problem "A clothes store sells $x$ hundreds of pants at a price $p(x),$  in dollars, given by\n\n$$\np(x)=200+40x-\\frac{10}{3}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the store sells between $500$ and $1\\,000$ pants per month, how many pants should it sell monthly to maximize its revenue?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ pants, the total revenue $R(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =x\\cdot p(x) \\\\  & =x(200+40x-\\frac{10}{3}{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =200x+40{x}^{2}-\\frac{10}{3}{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $5\\le x\\le 10,$  since $x=5$  corresponds to $5\\cdot 100=500$  and $x=10$ corresponds to $10\\cdot 100=1\\,000.$  Therefore, $x=5$ and $x=10$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $5<x<10.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=200+80x-10{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}-80x-200 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x-20 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-10) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=-2$ and $x=10.$ However, the value $x=-2$  is not in the permitted domain. Moreover, $x=10$ is already a candidate, so our candidates are  $x=5$ and $x=10.$ Computing $R(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(5) & =200(5)+40(5)^{2}-\\frac{10}{3}{5}^{3}≈1\\,583 \\\\ R(10) & =200(10)+40(10)^{2}-\\frac{10}{3}{10}^{3}≈2\\,667\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize its revenue, the clothes store should sell $1\\,000$ pants monthly." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f3f83acf-0c32-5e5e-8073-81cec7cfab01"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f3f83acf-0c32-5e5e-8073-81cec7cfab01" :answer-field/id #uuid "f3f83acf-0c32-5e5e-8073-81cec7cfab01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3d538af1-335c-51bd-b85a-93ea9a161e00" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3d538af1-335c-51bd-b85a-93ea9a161e00" "answer-a464b164-3d5e-5265-8432-b765f2245c04" "answer-94bc2a7c-6ef5-5e08-91ef-2c4935fbdb11" "answer-600e5b0c-e158-5ee6-8730-7bcf0774f0c6" "answer-080330c5-a425-5e6c-b28c-e7be16c7d10c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d538af1-335c-51bd-b85a-93ea9a161e00" :answer/id #uuid "3d538af1-335c-51bd-b85a-93ea9a161e00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a464b164-3d5e-5265-8432-b765f2245c04" :answer/id #uuid "a464b164-3d5e-5265-8432-b765f2245c04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "500" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94bc2a7c-6ef5-5e08-91ef-2c4935fbdb11" :answer/id #uuid "94bc2a7c-6ef5-5e08-91ef-2c4935fbdb11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "540" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-600e5b0c-e158-5ee6-8730-7bcf0774f0c6" :answer/id #uuid "600e5b0c-e158-5ee6-8730-7bcf0774f0c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "700" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-080330c5-a425-5e6c-b28c-e7be16c7d10c" :answer/id #uuid "080330c5-a425-5e6c-b28c-e7be16c7d10c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "820" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1ae7f739-7e2b-55d5-873a-c21108d944ca" :question/id #uuid "1ae7f739-7e2b-55d5-873a-c21108d944ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-347034" :question/problem "A private art studio can enroll between $8$ and $14$ students in a painting workshop. The fee that each student pays is $$72$ if exactly $8$ students enroll, and if more than $8$ students are enrolled, the fee drops by $$4$ for each additional student. Each student pays the same fee.\n\nHow many students must the workshop have to maximize its revenue?\n\nTo maximize its revenue, the workshop must have\n{{field-1}}\nstudents." :question/worked-solution "First, note that if there are $8+x$ students, then the fee is $72-4x.$ So, the total revenue is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(8+x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{number of students}}\\times {{(72-4x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fee}} \\\\  & =(8+x)(72-4x) \\\\  & =576-32x+72x-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =576+40x-4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the workshop can only have between $8$ and $14$ students, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n8\\le x+8\\le 14⇒0\\le x\\le 6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=6$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $0<x<6.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=40-8x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 40-8x & =0 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=5$ lies in the domain $0<x<6.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=5$ gives a maximum. To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{R}^{″}(x)=-8<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=5$ is indeed a local maximum of $R(x).$ And since $R(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=5$ is a global maximum of $R(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the workshop must have $8+5=13$ students enrolled to maximize its revenue." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2ae518f5-b8f5-5525-bcc5-e90c4f502064"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2ae518f5-b8f5-5525-bcc5-e90c4f502064" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ae518f5-b8f5-5525-bcc5-e90c4f502064" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0052cd3e-3060-5730-98da-8af2605187af" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0052cd3e-3060-5730-98da-8af2605187af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0052cd3e-3060-5730-98da-8af2605187af" :answer/id #uuid "0052cd3e-3060-5730-98da-8af2605187af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a0e359a4-15e8-5a4f-83b1-ea0f9d507e49" :question/id #uuid "a0e359a4-15e8-5a4f-83b1-ea0f9d507e49" :question/math-academy-id "q-346992" :question/problem "A photography studio books $x$ photo sessions per day at a price per session $p(x),$ in dollars, given by\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2}-9x+15+\\frac{45}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the studio books between $1$ and $6$ sessions per day, how many sessions should it book to maximize its revenue?\n\nTo maximize revenue, the studio should book\n{{field-1}}\nphoto sessions per day." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units in total, the total revenue $R(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =x\\cdot p(x) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-9x+15+\\frac{45}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+15x+45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 6.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=6$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $1<x<6.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-18x+15,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-18x+15 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=1$ and $x=5.$ However, $x=1$ is already a candidate, so the new candidate is $x=5.$\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,\\,5,$ and $6.$ Computing $R(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =(1)^{3}-9(1)^{2}+15(1)+45=1-9+15+45=52\\,✓ \\\\ R(5) & =(5)^{3}-9(5)^{2}+15(5)+45=125-225+75+45=20 \\\\ R(6) & =(6)^{3}-9(6)^{2}+15(6)+45=216-324+90+45=27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize revenue, the studio should book $1$ photo session per day." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-277fac87-c14e-510e-81e5-ecb4ba9ef95c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-277fac87-c14e-510e-81e5-ecb4ba9ef95c" :answer-field/id #uuid "277fac87-c14e-510e-81e5-ecb4ba9ef95c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-51dff2bc-6adb-539d-8c94-2b1ff31cd0fa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-51dff2bc-6adb-539d-8c94-2b1ff31cd0fa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51dff2bc-6adb-539d-8c94-2b1ff31cd0fa" :answer/id #uuid "51dff2bc-6adb-539d-8c94-2b1ff31cd0fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-896ab313-4af7-5cfc-9d60-946032fe5a62" :question/id #uuid "896ab313-4af7-5cfc-9d60-946032fe5a62" :question/math-academy-id "q-347242" :question/problem "A plant spends $C(x)=150+20x+24{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ thousands of dollars to produce $x$ units of a certain product per month. The plant sells the product for $$200\\,000$ per unit. Given that the plant can produce between $2$ and $9$ units per month, how many units should the plant produce per month to maximize its profit?\n\nThe optimal number of plant production is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product, the plant has a revenue (income) of $R(x)=200\\cdot x=200x$ thousands of dollars.\n\nTherefore, the profit function (in thousands of dollars) is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =200x-(150+20x+24{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}+180x-150.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $2\\le x\\le 9.$ Therefore, $x=2$ and $x=9$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $2<x<9.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-48x+180,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-48x+180 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-16x+60 & =0 \\\\ (x-6)(x-10) & =0 \\\\ x & =6,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the critical points $x=6$ and $x=10,$ only $x=6$ is within the permitted domain.\n\nAll that's left now is to check if $x=6$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(x) & =6x-48 \\\\ {P}^{″}(6) & =-12<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative at $x=6$ is negative, the point $x=6$ is indeed a maximum.\n\nOur candidates are $x=2,x=6$ and $x=9.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2) & =(2)^{3}-24(2)^{2}+180(2)-150=122 \\\\ P(6) & =(6)^{3}-24(6)^{2}+180(6)-150=282\\,✓ \\\\ P(9) & =(9)^{3}-24(9)^{2}+180(9)-150=255\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the plant will have a maximum profit level when it produces $6$ units per month." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-167a66ac-c75b-5fb3-804d-27eb25cf3aa4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-167a66ac-c75b-5fb3-804d-27eb25cf3aa4" :answer-field/id #uuid "167a66ac-c75b-5fb3-804d-27eb25cf3aa4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1fbeab81-4eff-5381-a265-fe79b799fdee" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1fbeab81-4eff-5381-a265-fe79b799fdee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fbeab81-4eff-5381-a265-fe79b799fdee" :answer/id #uuid "1fbeab81-4eff-5381-a265-fe79b799fdee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-de3d69db-3561-564d-a214-9ac683352f20" :question/id #uuid "de3d69db-3561-564d-a214-9ac683352f20" :question/math-academy-id "q-347261" :question/problem "A candle company spends $C(x)=120x-24{x}^{2}+16{x}^{3}-3{x}^{4}$ dollars to produce $x$ candles per week. The company sells each candle for $$120.$ Given that the company can produce between $1$ and $6$ candles per week, what is the maximum weekly profit that the company can make?\n\nThe maximum weekly profit is $$$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ candles, the company has a revenue (income) of $R(x)=120\\cdot x=120x$ dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =120x-(120x-24{x}^{2}+16{x}^{3}-3{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =24{x}^{2}-16{x}^{3}+3{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 6.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=6$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $1<x<6.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=48x-48{x}^{2}+12{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 48x-48{x}^{2}+12{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 12x(4-4x+{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 12x(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ However, the value $x=0$ is not in the permitted domain, so the only possible new candidate is $x=2.$\n\nLet's check if $x=2$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=48-96x+36{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(2)=48-192+144=0,\n$$\n\nwhich does not confirm that $x=2$ is a maximum. Therefore, we must include $x=2$ as a candidate.\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,$ $x=2,$ and $x=6.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =24(1)^{2}-16(1)^{3}+3(1)^{4}=11 \\\\ P(2) & =24(2)^{2}-16(2)^{3}+3(2)^{4}=32 \\\\ P(6) & =24(6)^{2}-16(6)^{3}+3(6)^{4}=1296\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the maximum weekly profit that the company can make is $$\\,1296.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-888e6519-6f88-5145-b927-7bc3da422884"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-888e6519-6f88-5145-b927-7bc3da422884" :answer-field/id #uuid "888e6519-6f88-5145-b927-7bc3da422884" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b2b9bdb1-70a2-566d-88d8-367ecbac51d0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b2b9bdb1-70a2-566d-88d8-367ecbac51d0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2b9bdb1-70a2-566d-88d8-367ecbac51d0" :answer/id #uuid "b2b9bdb1-70a2-566d-88d8-367ecbac51d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1296" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101564 :question/problem "A computer science center organizes a machine learning course. The course is activated if there are at least $3$ participants, and it can be attended by at most $13$ participants. The price for each participant is $$200.$ The cost of providing the course is $C(x)=200x-4{x}^{3}+0.3{x}^{4},$ where $x$ is the number of participants. What is the maximum profit that the computer science center can make?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates' test.\n\nFirst, note that for $x$ participants, the computer science center has a revenue (income) of $R(x)=200\\cdot x=200x$ dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =200x-(200x-4{x}^{3}+0.3{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}-0.3{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $3\\le x\\le 13.$ Therefore, $x=3$ and $x=13$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $3\\ge x\\ge 13.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=12{x}^{2}-1.2{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 12{x}^{2}-1.2{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 1.2{x}^{2}(10-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0$ and $x=10.$ However, the values $x=0$ is not in the permitted domain, so the only possible new candidate is $x=10.$\n\nLet's check if $x=10$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(x) & =24x-3.6{x}^{2} \\\\ {P}^{″}(10) & =240-360=-120<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative at  $x=10$ is negative, the point $x=10$ is a maximum. \n\nOur candidates are $x=3,x=10$ and $x=13.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =4(3)^{3}-0.3(3)^{4}=83.7 \\\\ P(10) & =4(10)^{3}-0.3(10)^{4}=1\\,000\\,✓ \\\\ P(13) & =4(13)^{3}-0.3(13)^{4}=219.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the maximum profit is $$1\\,000.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100545 :answer-field/correct "answer-5dffbfce-f42c-5aa6-9041-b83e50fdad63" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5dffbfce-f42c-5aa6-9041-b83e50fdad63"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5dffbfce-f42c-5aa6-9041-b83e50fdad63" :answer/id #uuid "5dffbfce-f42c-5aa6-9041-b83e50fdad63" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$1\\,000$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101504 :question/problem "A factory spends $C(x)={x}^{4}+13x$ thousand dollars to manufacture $x$ units of a certain product. The factory sells the product for $$121\\,000$ per unit. What is the maximum monthly profit that the factory can make given that it can produce at most $4$ items per month?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per month, the factory has a monthly revenue (income) of $R(x)=121\\cdot x=121x$ thousand dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function (in thousands of dollars) is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =121x-({x}^{4}+13x) \\\\  & =108x-{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 4.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=4$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<4.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=108-4{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 108-4{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =27 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $x=3$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-12{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating ${P}^{″}(x)$ at the critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(3)=-12(3)^{2}=-108<0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that $x=3$ is a maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nOur candidates are $x=0,$ $x=3$ and $x=4.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =108(0)-{0}^{4}=0 \\\\ P(3) & =108(3)-{3}^{4}=243\\,✓ \\\\ P(4) & =108(4)-{4}^{4}=176\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum monthly profit is $$243\\,000.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100185 :answer-field/correct "answer-17aa9f4f-ce84-554a-bc96-221077334d69" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d8c2b6a7-709f-5948-a5f8-ebe843858f20" "answer-933d6611-667a-577f-a7e0-6fa58e2da1ac" "answer-ddbf2bc7-cb32-5dcc-bad6-c8c2e13465b7" "answer-17aa9f4f-ce84-554a-bc96-221077334d69" "answer-9974f26a-f280-5eb2-933d-048ae35c522b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8c2b6a7-709f-5948-a5f8-ebe843858f20" :answer/id #uuid "d8c2b6a7-709f-5948-a5f8-ebe843858f20" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$107\\,000.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-933d6611-667a-577f-a7e0-6fa58e2da1ac" :answer/id #uuid "933d6611-667a-577f-a7e0-6fa58e2da1ac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$176\\,000.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ddbf2bc7-cb32-5dcc-bad6-c8c2e13465b7" :answer/id #uuid "ddbf2bc7-cb32-5dcc-bad6-c8c2e13465b7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$200\\,000.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17aa9f4f-ce84-554a-bc96-221077334d69" :answer/id #uuid "17aa9f4f-ce84-554a-bc96-221077334d69" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$243\\,000.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9974f26a-f280-5eb2-933d-048ae35c522b" :answer/id #uuid "9974f26a-f280-5eb2-933d-048ae35c522b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$310\\,000.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a7b5b31e-6d04-57eb-9051-cd4496c07c09" :question/id #uuid "a7b5b31e-6d04-57eb-9051-cd4496c07c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-347264" :question/problem "A factory spends  $C(x)=0.002375{x}^{2}+0.3x+1\\,000$ dollars to manufacture $x$ units of a certain product. The factory sells the product for $30-\\frac{x}{100}$ dollars per unit. Given that the factory can produce at most $2\\,000$ units per year, how many units should the factory produce per year to maximize its annual profit?\n\nThe optimal number of production is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per year, the factory has an annual revenue (income) of $R(x)=(30-\\frac{x}{100})\\cdot x=30x-0.01{x}^{2}$ dollars.\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =30x-0.01{x}^{2}-(0.002375{x}^{2}+0.3x+1\\,000) \\\\  & =-1\\,000+29.7x-0.012375{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 2\\,000.$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<2\\,000.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=29.7-0.02475x.\n$$\n\nSolving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 29.7-0.02475x & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\,200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=1\\,200$ lies in the interval $0<x<2\\,000.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=1\\,200$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:   \n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-0.02475<0.\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=1\\,200$ is indeed a  maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nOur candidates are $x=0,$ $x=1\\,200,$ and $x=2\\,000.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =-1\\,000 \\\\ P(1\\,200) & =-1\\,000+29.7(1\\,200)-0.012375(1\\,200)^{2}=16\\,820\\,✓ \\\\ P(2\\,000) & =-1\\,000+29.7(2\\,000)-0.012375(2\\,000)^{2}=9\\,500\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the factory will have a maximum annual profit when it produces $1\\,200$ units per year." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b3b3f22b-6d4c-5cc5-9130-cf5e72b4c48c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b3b3f22b-6d4c-5cc5-9130-cf5e72b4c48c" :answer-field/id #uuid "b3b3f22b-6d4c-5cc5-9130-cf5e72b4c48c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5dedbf55-c0ea-588a-a30e-090c0bd91a10" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5dedbf55-c0ea-588a-a30e-090c0bd91a10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5dedbf55-c0ea-588a-a30e-090c0bd91a10" :answer/id #uuid "5dedbf55-c0ea-588a-a30e-090c0bd91a10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1200" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2888ed58-7e1b-57a5-91ea-2124b7fe040e" :question/id #uuid "2888ed58-7e1b-57a5-91ea-2124b7fe040e" :question/math-academy-id "q-18671" :question/problem "A hotel has $71$ rooms. The manager has observed that when the room rate is $$50$ per night, all rooms are occupied and, for every $$2$ increase in the rate, a single room is vacated. If the maintenance per rented room is $$4$ per night, what rate per room should the manager set to obtain the maximum profit?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that if there are $x$ *vacant* rooms, the hotel has $(71-x)$ occupied rooms, and the rate per room is $(50+2x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in dollars) is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(71-x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{occupied rooms}}\\times \\,\\,\\,{{(50+2x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{rate per room}} \\\\  & =3\\,550+142x-50x-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\,550+92x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for having $(71-x)$ rooms rented out, the hotel has a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =4(71-x) \\\\  & =284-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\ P(x) & =3\\,550+92x-2{x}^{2}-(284-4x) \\\\  & =3\\,266+96x-2{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 71.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<71$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=96-4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 96-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =96 \\\\ x & =24\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=24$ lies in the interval $0<x<71.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=24$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-4<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=24$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=24$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the hotel will have a maximum profit level when there are $x=24$ vacant rooms. \n\nWe know that the rate per room is $(50+2x)$ dollars. If there are $24$ vacant rooms, then the rate per room is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50+2(24) & =$98.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the manager should charge a room rate of $$98$ per night." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-97e14268-ee5f-5656-86a1-d067419325eb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-97e14268-ee5f-5656-86a1-d067419325eb" :answer-field/id #uuid "97e14268-ee5f-5656-86a1-d067419325eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a6a572cf-f451-578e-b062-948286b9f2a3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e464d60b-ec47-553e-aef7-bedba5f73485" "answer-d26f525d-acc9-5080-9da8-6d1c7218b145" "answer-435b2373-d036-5a72-a5b3-9b22809d8383" "answer-67c15ab1-b48d-50a4-92c6-9e51aa87b1fd" "answer-a6a572cf-f451-578e-b062-948286b9f2a3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e464d60b-ec47-553e-aef7-bedba5f73485" :answer/id #uuid "e464d60b-ec47-553e-aef7-bedba5f73485" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$106$ per night" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d26f525d-acc9-5080-9da8-6d1c7218b145" :answer/id #uuid "d26f525d-acc9-5080-9da8-6d1c7218b145" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$120$ per night" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-435b2373-d036-5a72-a5b3-9b22809d8383" :answer/id #uuid "435b2373-d036-5a72-a5b3-9b22809d8383" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$50$ per night" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67c15ab1-b48d-50a4-92c6-9e51aa87b1fd" :answer/id #uuid "67c15ab1-b48d-50a4-92c6-9e51aa87b1fd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$84$ per night" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6a572cf-f451-578e-b062-948286b9f2a3" :answer/id #uuid "a6a572cf-f451-578e-b062-948286b9f2a3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$98$ per night" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8babed00-8ea0-55fe-9919-358ba07b0746" :question/id #uuid "8babed00-8ea0-55fe-9919-358ba07b0746" :question/math-academy-id "q-119098" :question/problem "A coffee shop can produce up to $500$ cups of coffee per day. When the price per cup of coffee is $$3,$ the coffee shop sells all of them, and, for each dollar increase in the price, $50$ cups remain unsold. If the cost for producing a cup of coffee is $$0.40,$ what price per cup should be set to obtain the maximum profit?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that if there are $x$ hundreds of *unsold* cups of coffee, the coffee shop sells $(5-x)$ hundreds of cups of coffee, and the price per cup is $(3+2x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in hundreds of dollars) is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(5-x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{sold cups of coffee}}\\times \\,\\,\\,{{(3+2x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{price per cup}} \\\\  & =15+10x-3x-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =15+7x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for selling $(5-x)$ hundreds of cups of coffee, the coffee shop has a cost (in hundreds of dollars) of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =0.4(5-x) \\\\  & =2-0.4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\ P(x) & =(15+7x-2{x}^{2})-(2-0.4x) \\\\  & =13+7.4x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 5.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<5.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=7.4-4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 7.4-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =7.4 \\\\ x & =1.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=1.85$ lies in the interval $0<x<5.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=1.85$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-4<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=1.85$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=1.85$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the coffee shop will have a maximum profit level when there are $185$ unsold cups of coffee.  \n\nWe know that the price per cup is $(3+2x)$ dollars. If there are $1.85$ hundreds of unsold cups of coffee, then the price per cup is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+2(1.85) & =$6.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coffee shop should sell each cup of coffee for $$6.7.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-15bd47fc-61d9-56ee-8807-cdce49006fe0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-15bd47fc-61d9-56ee-8807-cdce49006fe0" :answer-field/id #uuid "15bd47fc-61d9-56ee-8807-cdce49006fe0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5e2d9015-7c6e-5b67-9a9c-095a3636dc1a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5fcc83c3-0427-5087-ba48-9b12c96afdf6" "answer-c7738231-00cd-5bfb-9cbd-70ba13d1a6eb" "answer-fb06f779-6b7c-57da-960c-d293832340e6" "answer-795b1cfc-a40e-577c-9727-d8a557ce295a" "answer-5e2d9015-7c6e-5b67-9a9c-095a3636dc1a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fcc83c3-0427-5087-ba48-9b12c96afdf6" :answer/id #uuid "5fcc83c3-0427-5087-ba48-9b12c96afdf6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$1.65$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7738231-00cd-5bfb-9cbd-70ba13d1a6eb" :answer/id #uuid "c7738231-00cd-5bfb-9cbd-70ba13d1a6eb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$1.85$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb06f779-6b7c-57da-960c-d293832340e6" :answer/id #uuid "fb06f779-6b7c-57da-960c-d293832340e6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-795b1cfc-a40e-577c-9727-d8a557ce295a" :answer/id #uuid "795b1cfc-a40e-577c-9727-d8a557ce295a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$6.3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e2d9015-7c6e-5b67-9a9c-095a3636dc1a" :answer/id #uuid "5e2d9015-7c6e-5b67-9a9c-095a3636dc1a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$6.7$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-eefd8d60-720e-5861-a3ae-27d7c6377bff" :question/id #uuid "eefd8d60-720e-5861-a3ae-27d7c6377bff" :question/math-academy-id "q-316188" :question/problem "A theme park sells ride passes each day. The price of each ride pass is $$15.$ The cost of operating the park includes a fixed daily cost of $$700$ and a variable cost of $$2$ per ride pass sold. If the park can sell at most $500$ ride passes per day, what is the number of ride passes that maximizes the park's profit?\n\nThe number of ride passes that maximizes the daily profit is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ ride passes per day, the park has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=15x.\n$$\n\nThe cost (in dollars) of operating the park is made up of a fixed cost and a variable cost:\n\n$$\nC(x)={{700}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}+{{2x}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =15x-(700+2x) \\\\  & =13x-700.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 500.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=500$ are both candidates for the global maximum.\n\nNext, we take the derivative of the profit function:\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=13,\n$$\n\nwhich is constant and positive. Since ${P}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x,$ the profit function is increasing throughout the domain.\n\nThus, the maximum profit occurs at the endpoint $x=500.$ Let’s confirm by computing values at both endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =13(0)-700=-700, \\\\ P(500) & =13(500)-700=6500-700=5800\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of ride passes that maximizes the park’s profit is $500.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-baf6f008-070c-539f-b70e-a2e12580def6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-baf6f008-070c-539f-b70e-a2e12580def6" :answer-field/id #uuid "baf6f008-070c-539f-b70e-a2e12580def6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ca141565-3816-5092-880f-28f10de87eca" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ca141565-3816-5092-880f-28f10de87eca"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca141565-3816-5092-880f-28f10de87eca" :answer/id #uuid "ca141565-3816-5092-880f-28f10de87eca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "500" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101113 :question/problem "A summer camp has capacity for $200$ kids. The price for each kid attending the camp is $$300.$ The cost of setting up the camp is made up of a fixed cost of $$2\\,000$ plus a cost of $$100$ per kid. How many kids should attend the summer camp to maximize its profit?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for $x$ kids attending, the summer camp has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=300\\cdot x=300x\n$$\n\nand a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nC(x)={{2\\,000}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}\\,\\,+{{100x.}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =300x-(2\\,000+100x) \\\\  & =200x-2\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 200.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=200$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<200.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=200.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ has no solutions.\n\nThus, our candidates are $x=0$ and $x=200.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =200(0)-2\\,000=-2\\,000 \\\\ P(200) & =200(200)-2\\,000=38\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the summer camp will have a maximum profit level when $200$ kids attend." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186098013 :answer-field/correct 17592186098016 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-244652f5-cacd-5369-8e7e-0fc003669027" :question/id #uuid "244652f5-cacd-5369-8e7e-0fc003669027" :question/math-academy-id "q-347272" :question/problem "A museum sells daily entry passes. The price of each pass is $$20.$ The cost of operating the museum includes a fixed daily cost of $$1,100$ and a variable cost of $$5$ per pass sold. If the museum can sell at most $300$ passes per day, what is the number of passes that maximizes the museum's profit?\n\nThe number of passes that maximizes the daily profit is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ passes per day, the museum has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=20x.\n$$\n\nThe cost (in dollars) of operating the museum is made up of a fixed cost and a variable cost:\n\n$$\nC(x)={{1100}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}+{{5x}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =20x-(1100+5x) \\\\  & =15x-1100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 300.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=300$ are both candidates for the global maximum.\n\nNext, we take the derivative of the profit function:\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=15,\n$$\n\nwhich is constant and positive. Since ${P}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x,$ the profit function is increasing throughout the domain.\n\nThus, the maximum profit occurs at the endpoint $x=300.$ Let’s confirm by computing values at both endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =-1100, \\\\ P(300) & =15(300)-1100=4500-1100=3400\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of passes that maximizes the museum’s profit is $300.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-10db67e6-511a-52b3-adcc-8dc9f9185159"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-10db67e6-511a-52b3-adcc-8dc9f9185159" :answer-field/id #uuid "10db67e6-511a-52b3-adcc-8dc9f9185159" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b400b302-93f5-5cab-a964-15b179561edb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b400b302-93f5-5cab-a964-15b179561edb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b400b302-93f5-5cab-a964-15b179561edb" :answer/id #uuid "b400b302-93f5-5cab-a964-15b179561edb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "300" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186102014 :tutorial/content "There are several quantities that businesses need to keep track of to ensure that the business remains healthy:\n\n- Revenue: This is the total amount of money (in dollars) that a company gains after selling $x$ units of product.\n\n- Cost: This is the total amount of money (in dollars) that a company spends to create $x$ units of product.\n\n- Profit. The difference between revenue and cost. It represents the amount of *additional* money that the business has earned after paying its costs.\n\nBusinesses often strive to maximize revenue and profit. They also often seek to minimize cost. We can solve these problems using the second derivative or the candidates test. Let's find out how." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101994 :tutorial/content "Recall that the price function $p(x)$ gives the *price per unit* at which a company sells $x$ units of a good. \n\nThe revenue function $R(x)$ associated with a price function $p(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =(\\text{number of units})\\cdot (\\text{price per unit}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot p(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that the cost function $C(x)$ associated with producing $x$ units of a good can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =(\\text{fixed cost})+(\\text{number of units})\\cdot (\\text{cost per unit}) \\\\  & =(\\text{fixed cost})+x\\cdot (\\text{cost per unit})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quantity $x\\cdot (\\textrm{cost per unit})$ is sometimes called the **variable cost** since it is the part of the cost function $C(x)$ that is dependent on the number of units $x$ produced by the company. \n\nFinally, if we know the revenue function $R(x)$ and the cost function $C(x),$ then the profit function $P(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(x) = R(x) - C(x).\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101072 :activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101902 :step/math-academy-id 644177 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101903 :step/math-academy-id 644178 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101904 :step/math-academy-id 644181 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101905 :step/math-academy-id 644182 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101906 :step/math-academy-id 644186 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101907 :step/math-academy-id 644190 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "activity-040c5c5d-5c4b-5a6f-85e4-62f2dfb193f2" :activity/id #uuid "040c5c5d-5c4b-5a6f-85e4-62f2dfb193f2" :activity/title "Differentiating an Inverse Function at a Point" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048441 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-9a824594-37c3-5545-a8e3-787961ef9113" "step-39a62d29-467c-57d8-a4cf-80c34a617db1" "step-ce43bfd8-8a06-5e8e-8778-7c099cd04a7e" "step-f5621451-68ba-582d-9965-0dfcb4b47ee0" "step-249404ae-dfda-550a-932f-fa498ca16ce4"] :activity/first-step "step-9a824594-37c3-5545-a8e3-787961ef9113" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-9a824594-37c3-5545-a8e3-787961ef9113" :step/id #uuid "9a824594-37c3-5545-a8e3-787961ef9113" :step/content 17592186345131 :step/next "step-39a62d29-467c-57d8-a4cf-80c34a617db1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-39a62d29-467c-57d8-a4cf-80c34a617db1" :step/id #uuid "39a62d29-467c-57d8-a4cf-80c34a617db1" :step/content 17592186345181 :step/next "step-ce43bfd8-8a06-5e8e-8778-7c099cd04a7e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ce43bfd8-8a06-5e8e-8778-7c099cd04a7e" :step/id #uuid "ce43bfd8-8a06-5e8e-8778-7c099cd04a7e" :step/content 17592186247111 :step/next "step-f5621451-68ba-582d-9965-0dfcb4b47ee0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f5621451-68ba-582d-9965-0dfcb4b47ee0" :step/id #uuid "f5621451-68ba-582d-9965-0dfcb4b47ee0" :step/content 17592186297354 :step/next "step-249404ae-dfda-550a-932f-fa498ca16ce4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-249404ae-dfda-550a-932f-fa498ca16ce4" :step/id #uuid "249404ae-dfda-550a-932f-fa498ca16ce4" :step/content 17592186317283 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162015 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-524b5d0d-afb1-56e2-9f18-7dc1697e2495"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-524b5d0d-afb1-56e2-9f18-7dc1697e2495" :question/id #uuid "524b5d0d-afb1-56e2-9f18-7dc1697e2495" :question/math-academy-id "q-95728" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((3)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}10 & -10 \\\\ -10 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b4cd93bf-3a8c-5f18-b352-8eff8b26fc90"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b4cd93bf-3a8c-5f18-b352-8eff8b26fc90" :answer-field/id #uuid "b4cd93bf-3a8c-5f18-b352-8eff8b26fc90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ea35d22f-50f1-5cf4-aa38-4adac074dc4b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4986bc0c-e982-51ac-aecd-3824f87d8c94" "answer-ea35d22f-50f1-5cf4-aa38-4adac074dc4b" "answer-253fb856-2658-5841-900d-3d095bc94cf9" "answer-631ec9ac-1a1a-52f7-bf42-8397061beb8c" "answer-0a9ba5aa-7ff7-5a13-8435-4ae5f003f1ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4986bc0c-e982-51ac-aecd-3824f87d8c94" :answer/id #uuid "4986bc0c-e982-51ac-aecd-3824f87d8c94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea35d22f-50f1-5cf4-aa38-4adac074dc4b" :answer/id #uuid "ea35d22f-50f1-5cf4-aa38-4adac074dc4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-253fb856-2658-5841-900d-3d095bc94cf9" :answer/id #uuid "253fb856-2658-5841-900d-3d095bc94cf9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-631ec9ac-1a1a-52f7-bf42-8397061beb8c" :answer/id #uuid "631ec9ac-1a1a-52f7-bf42-8397061beb8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a9ba5aa-7ff7-5a13-8435-4ae5f003f1ed" :answer/id #uuid "0a9ba5aa-7ff7-5a13-8435-4ae5f003f1ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162014 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4d6df4b5-e301-58fd-845a-847c7a249d39"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4d6df4b5-e301-58fd-845a-847c7a249d39" :question/id #uuid "4d6df4b5-e301-58fd-845a-847c7a249d39" :question/math-academy-id "q-140849" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-29cd7cba-3362-549f-8e3e-97da0f1c1325"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-29cd7cba-3362-549f-8e3e-97da0f1c1325" :answer-field/id #uuid "29cd7cba-3362-549f-8e3e-97da0f1c1325" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3a0018a6-7dae-54cf-88b4-02338b8fff5b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a24957bf-c3bd-5b14-b0b3-55b5967d855a" "answer-e533b56d-0765-5385-b5e3-a0e0dfcc5a8e" "answer-3a0018a6-7dae-54cf-88b4-02338b8fff5b" "answer-a218fed0-75a7-50d9-8f60-c86aeaebfd23" "answer-bd5b03f7-2a8e-557c-abde-00dafe7a50cb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a24957bf-c3bd-5b14-b0b3-55b5967d855a" :answer/id #uuid "a24957bf-c3bd-5b14-b0b3-55b5967d855a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e533b56d-0765-5385-b5e3-a0e0dfcc5a8e" :answer/id #uuid "e533b56d-0765-5385-b5e3-a0e0dfcc5a8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a0018a6-7dae-54cf-88b4-02338b8fff5b" :answer/id #uuid "3a0018a6-7dae-54cf-88b4-02338b8fff5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a218fed0-75a7-50d9-8f60-c86aeaebfd23" :answer/id #uuid "a218fed0-75a7-50d9-8f60-c86aeaebfd23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd5b03f7-2a8e-557c-abde-00dafe7a50cb" :answer/id #uuid "bd5b03f7-2a8e-557c-abde-00dafe7a50cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-b98685f7-3920-523a-8d3c-632e830a9d7d" :activity/id #uuid "b98685f7-3920-523a-8d3c-632e830a9d7d" :activity/title "The Spectral Theorem" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049849 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-7f7d44ce-dbe3-5404-92b7-3a9a5ceebe0e" "step-3dcce81f-cc42-5948-b429-8ba4a11557f3" "step-55f4143a-4a93-5e8d-b696-93cfb4a2e6c1" "step-d68ef3a3-b3f5-5888-8b3c-31c4716078a7" "step-f8df9c63-69f3-5acb-8e84-5b34d208def1"] :activity/first-step "step-7f7d44ce-dbe3-5404-92b7-3a9a5ceebe0e" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-7f7d44ce-dbe3-5404-92b7-3a9a5ceebe0e" :step/id #uuid "7f7d44ce-dbe3-5404-92b7-3a9a5ceebe0e" :step/content 17592186162646 :step/next "step-3dcce81f-cc42-5948-b429-8ba4a11557f3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3dcce81f-cc42-5948-b429-8ba4a11557f3" :step/id #uuid "3dcce81f-cc42-5948-b429-8ba4a11557f3" :step/content 17592186407553 :step/next "step-55f4143a-4a93-5e8d-b696-93cfb4a2e6c1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-55f4143a-4a93-5e8d-b696-93cfb4a2e6c1" :step/id #uuid "55f4143a-4a93-5e8d-b696-93cfb4a2e6c1" :step/content 17592186407551 :step/next "step-d68ef3a3-b3f5-5888-8b3c-31c4716078a7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d68ef3a3-b3f5-5888-8b3c-31c4716078a7" :step/id #uuid "d68ef3a3-b3f5-5888-8b3c-31c4716078a7" :step/content "question-524b5d0d-afb1-56e2-9f18-7dc1697e2495" :step/next "step-f8df9c63-69f3-5acb-8e84-5b34d208def1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f8df9c63-69f3-5acb-8e84-5b34d208def1" :step/id #uuid "f8df9c63-69f3-5acb-8e84-5b34d208def1" :step/content "question-4d6df4b5-e301-58fd-845a-847c7a249d39" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162089 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-80fca04e-db43-5719-819a-d2aec209a00e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-80fca04e-db43-5719-819a-d2aec209a00e" :question/id #uuid "80fca04e-db43-5719-819a-d2aec209a00e" :question/math-academy-id "q-138536" :question/problem "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-29dc167c-c1ad-5d54-8910-071c51c74e87"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-29dc167c-c1ad-5d54-8910-071c51c74e87" :answer-field/id #uuid "29dc167c-c1ad-5d54-8910-071c51c74e87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ad9aa926-113d-59e6-8eab-0e2641ef958e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-caa6eda2-e6f0-5988-9e38-f7cffa0af3f4" "answer-503b74e3-9bce-5949-8ad7-9b878630cf87" "answer-ad9aa926-113d-59e6-8eab-0e2641ef958e" "answer-03c592a0-a31a-5768-80a4-19414e8114d1" "answer-3ca7b07c-b688-5430-bb51-a1a02662d23f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-caa6eda2-e6f0-5988-9e38-f7cffa0af3f4" :answer/id #uuid "caa6eda2-e6f0-5988-9e38-f7cffa0af3f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-503b74e3-9bce-5949-8ad7-9b878630cf87" :answer/id #uuid "503b74e3-9bce-5949-8ad7-9b878630cf87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad9aa926-113d-59e6-8eab-0e2641ef958e" :answer/id #uuid "ad9aa926-113d-59e6-8eab-0e2641ef958e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03c592a0-a31a-5768-80a4-19414e8114d1" :answer/id #uuid "03c592a0-a31a-5768-80a4-19414e8114d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ca7b07c-b688-5430-bb51-a1a02662d23f" :answer/id #uuid "3ca7b07c-b688-5430-bb51-a1a02662d23f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162091 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e15227a9-4593-50e7-a6e9-0ebad0f88648"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e15227a9-4593-50e7-a6e9-0ebad0f88648" :question/id #uuid "e15227a9-4593-50e7-a6e9-0ebad0f88648" :question/math-academy-id "q-138617" :question/problem "$$\n{U}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{V}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-06233fbd-739a-5715-82d1-f750ac787a62"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-06233fbd-739a-5715-82d1-f750ac787a62" :answer-field/id #uuid "06233fbd-739a-5715-82d1-f750ac787a62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5ae11b85-45e6-5dd7-8b03-2d6388de4723" :answer-field/choices ["answer-673fceb3-6f45-5623-8b71-9c0dfa70f9da" "answer-97c1d397-ce2a-566d-87b1-9841a83b5ce6" "answer-5ae11b85-45e6-5dd7-8b03-2d6388de4723" "answer-682473a8-ddf4-51d7-92e8-835c0c7d6bf1" "answer-e27eae27-fef7-5f3f-9694-1cc984a96420"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-673fceb3-6f45-5623-8b71-9c0dfa70f9da" :answer/id #uuid "673fceb3-6f45-5623-8b71-9c0dfa70f9da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97c1d397-ce2a-566d-87b1-9841a83b5ce6" :answer/id #uuid "97c1d397-ce2a-566d-87b1-9841a83b5ce6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ae11b85-45e6-5dd7-8b03-2d6388de4723" :answer/id #uuid "5ae11b85-45e6-5dd7-8b03-2d6388de4723" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-682473a8-ddf4-51d7-92e8-835c0c7d6bf1" :answer/id #uuid "682473a8-ddf4-51d7-92e8-835c0c7d6bf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e27eae27-fef7-5f3f-9694-1cc984a96420" :answer/id #uuid "e27eae27-fef7-5f3f-9694-1cc984a96420" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-b5254e07-5277-5044-88b8-8db457aafdb3" :activity/id #uuid "b5254e07-5277-5044-88b8-8db457aafdb3" :activity/title "Singular Value Decomposition and the Pseudoinverse Matrix" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049503 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-cdba1b8c-84f0-595a-bbc8-675a1ff04aff" "step-1e7c14bb-006e-5e34-b55c-ef232ee8999e" "step-16a6c242-af22-5cb6-bbb9-21913b53eaae" "step-d166b145-57b4-5cbe-9caf-70939c1de8e3" "step-1ad8e3aa-2544-535a-b8c8-09ae28fba811"] :activity/first-step "step-cdba1b8c-84f0-595a-bbc8-675a1ff04aff" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-cdba1b8c-84f0-595a-bbc8-675a1ff04aff" :step/id #uuid "cdba1b8c-84f0-595a-bbc8-675a1ff04aff" :step/content 17592186371790 :step/next "step-1e7c14bb-006e-5e34-b55c-ef232ee8999e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1e7c14bb-006e-5e34-b55c-ef232ee8999e" :step/id #uuid "1e7c14bb-006e-5e34-b55c-ef232ee8999e" :step/content 17592186388545 :step/next "step-16a6c242-af22-5cb6-bbb9-21913b53eaae" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-16a6c242-af22-5cb6-bbb9-21913b53eaae" :step/id #uuid "16a6c242-af22-5cb6-bbb9-21913b53eaae" :step/content "question-80fca04e-db43-5719-819a-d2aec209a00e" :step/next "step-d166b145-57b4-5cbe-9caf-70939c1de8e3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d166b145-57b4-5cbe-9caf-70939c1de8e3" :step/id #uuid "d166b145-57b4-5cbe-9caf-70939c1de8e3" :step/content "question-e15227a9-4593-50e7-a6e9-0ebad0f88648" :step/next "step-1ad8e3aa-2544-535a-b8c8-09ae28fba811" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1ad8e3aa-2544-535a-b8c8-09ae28fba811" :step/id #uuid "1ad8e3aa-2544-535a-b8c8-09ae28fba811" :step/content 17592186388559 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229174 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-270a37f5-30dc-586c-8bce-5a858bce0aaa" "question-f0bd5207-abfa-5ab3-9c43-fac18e98df50"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-270a37f5-30dc-586c-8bce-5a858bce0aaa" :question/id #uuid "270a37f5-30dc-586c-8bce-5a858bce0aaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-340776" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of a function from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the constant Fourier coefficient of  $f(x)+4?$" :question/worked-solution "The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nRecall that if $C$ is a constant, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $f(x)+C$ are\n\n- ${a}_{0}+2C$  for the constant coefficient,\n\n- ${a}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- ${b}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nIn our case, $C=4.$ Therefore, the constant Fourier coefficient of  $f(x)+4$ is ${a}_{0}+8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aa9d73a9-94dc-5fe0-87cb-3e57fcd56b5a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aa9d73a9-94dc-5fe0-87cb-3e57fcd56b5a" :answer-field/id #uuid "aa9d73a9-94dc-5fe0-87cb-3e57fcd56b5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cee4def6-0cfa-56ad-99c4-7b9846ee4316" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d7e53dcb-9b26-58de-96f2-fc3f376f55dc" "answer-79b9b20a-a095-5016-8fe7-3f0217a39664" "answer-cee4def6-0cfa-56ad-99c4-7b9846ee4316" "answer-0e664224-e5b9-5dea-89e9-5258fe549e91" "answer-3003975d-8590-52c8-854f-7b40173950e1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7e53dcb-9b26-58de-96f2-fc3f376f55dc" :answer/id #uuid "d7e53dcb-9b26-58de-96f2-fc3f376f55dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79b9b20a-a095-5016-8fe7-3f0217a39664" :answer/id #uuid "79b9b20a-a095-5016-8fe7-3f0217a39664" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{0}+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cee4def6-0cfa-56ad-99c4-7b9846ee4316" :answer/id #uuid "cee4def6-0cfa-56ad-99c4-7b9846ee4316" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{0}+8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e664224-e5b9-5dea-89e9-5258fe549e91" :answer/id #uuid "0e664224-e5b9-5dea-89e9-5258fe549e91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{0}-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3003975d-8590-52c8-854f-7b40173950e1" :answer/id #uuid "3003975d-8590-52c8-854f-7b40173950e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{0}-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f0bd5207-abfa-5ab3-9c43-fac18e98df50" :question/id #uuid "f0bd5207-abfa-5ab3-9c43-fac18e98df50" :question/math-academy-id "q-340784" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of a function from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the $5$th cosine Fourier coefficient of $f(-x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nRecall that the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $f(-x)$ are\n\n- ${a}_{0}$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- ${a}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- $-{b}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore, the $5$th cosine coefficient of $f(-x)$ is ${a}_{5}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0cd3b6c4-428e-5510-88f8-cbc092e81e64"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0cd3b6c4-428e-5510-88f8-cbc092e81e64" :answer-field/id #uuid "0cd3b6c4-428e-5510-88f8-cbc092e81e64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fffdf104-47aa-5883-820c-6129aeff7de3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-31897f5a-6e6b-5449-8ab3-590db3d42de2" "answer-919e0113-edd3-5841-879e-86aa411c1a17" "answer-fffdf104-47aa-5883-820c-6129aeff7de3" "answer-ae7aa80e-855d-54f5-a677-6ef4cbb64763" "answer-dc0e1606-796a-518e-aacf-18478af80e60"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31897f5a-6e6b-5449-8ab3-590db3d42de2" :answer/id #uuid "31897f5a-6e6b-5449-8ab3-590db3d42de2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{a}_{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-919e0113-edd3-5841-879e-86aa411c1a17" :answer/id #uuid "919e0113-edd3-5841-879e-86aa411c1a17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{b}_{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fffdf104-47aa-5883-820c-6129aeff7de3" :answer/id #uuid "fffdf104-47aa-5883-820c-6129aeff7de3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae7aa80e-855d-54f5-a677-6ef4cbb64763" :answer/id #uuid "ae7aa80e-855d-54f5-a677-6ef4cbb64763" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{5}+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc0e1606-796a-518e-aacf-18478af80e60" :answer/id #uuid "dc0e1606-796a-518e-aacf-18478af80e60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{b}_{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229175 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8c279eae-dd78-5b67-912d-9a38a48eda28" "question-cd373585-64b2-5a1f-ad55-f3dee081e651"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-8c279eae-dd78-5b67-912d-9a38a48eda28" :question/id #uuid "8c279eae-dd78-5b67-912d-9a38a48eda28" :question/math-academy-id "q-340800" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=7-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the {{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." :question/worked-solution "To check whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =7-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(-x)) \\\\  & =7-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x) \\\\  & =7-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=f(x),$ we have that $f$ is even.\n\nFor the Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is even and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd, so $f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${b}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{even}$ and the $\\text{sine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9b911936-49df-53cf-9f01-74e0cebc1ad3" "answer-field-3130d5c0-7c5e-597f-aea9-6bb1181000b3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9b911936-49df-53cf-9f01-74e0cebc1ad3" :answer-field/id #uuid "9b911936-49df-53cf-9f01-74e0cebc1ad3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-b69ee136-7e7e-535f-917e-01a32e6e673d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b69ee136-7e7e-535f-917e-01a32e6e673d" "answer-7b810400-5899-5b7e-9373-4174747a47a7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b69ee136-7e7e-535f-917e-01a32e6e673d" :answer/id #uuid "b69ee136-7e7e-535f-917e-01a32e6e673d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "even" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b810400-5899-5b7e-9373-4174747a47a7" :answer/id #uuid "7b810400-5899-5b7e-9373-4174747a47a7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "odd" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3130d5c0-7c5e-597f-aea9-6bb1181000b3" :answer-field/id #uuid "3130d5c0-7c5e-597f-aea9-6bb1181000b3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-6e4b8479-723c-55f8-8a13-809141b3b771" :answer-field/choices ["answer-151c2adc-f498-5355-bc1b-efa89a51b45a" "answer-6e4b8479-723c-55f8-8a13-809141b3b771"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-151c2adc-f498-5355-bc1b-efa89a51b45a" :answer/id #uuid "151c2adc-f498-5355-bc1b-efa89a51b45a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "cosine terms" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e4b8479-723c-55f8-8a13-809141b3b771" :answer/id #uuid "6e4b8479-723c-55f8-8a13-809141b3b771" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "sine terms" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cd373585-64b2-5a1f-ad55-f3dee081e651" :question/id #uuid "cd373585-64b2-5a1f-ad55-f3dee081e651" :question/math-academy-id "q-340808" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the \n{{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ we have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${a}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{odd}$ and the $\\text{cosine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-19bcbcdc-3036-501a-8695-55f2eed0f886" "answer-field-a035b7e1-0b84-5941-bd5f-075b05041e0e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-19bcbcdc-3036-501a-8695-55f2eed0f886" :answer-field/id #uuid "19bcbcdc-3036-501a-8695-55f2eed0f886" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-f5668d0f-beb2-5460-abcc-92d140689ec1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-72a0583c-ea43-573f-b06b-b4a00d3b2c56" "answer-f5668d0f-beb2-5460-abcc-92d140689ec1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72a0583c-ea43-573f-b06b-b4a00d3b2c56" :answer/id #uuid "72a0583c-ea43-573f-b06b-b4a00d3b2c56" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "even" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5668d0f-beb2-5460-abcc-92d140689ec1" :answer/id #uuid "f5668d0f-beb2-5460-abcc-92d140689ec1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "odd" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a035b7e1-0b84-5941-bd5f-075b05041e0e" :answer-field/id #uuid "a035b7e1-0b84-5941-bd5f-075b05041e0e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-8459b090-9382-5cc1-a414-5ca52e5a7ae5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8459b090-9382-5cc1-a414-5ca52e5a7ae5" "answer-d9aad8a1-e2f8-56c6-bcac-8497da0fc04a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8459b090-9382-5cc1-a414-5ca52e5a7ae5" :answer/id #uuid "8459b090-9382-5cc1-a414-5ca52e5a7ae5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "cosine terms" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9aad8a1-e2f8-56c6-bcac-8497da0fc04a" :answer/id #uuid "d9aad8a1-e2f8-56c6-bcac-8497da0fc04a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "sine terms" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-555fcac9-8713-58b2-9317-3fe48ec69606" :activity/id #uuid "555fcac9-8713-58b2-9317-3fe48ec69606" :activity/title "Properties of Fourier Series" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050730 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-9c211d01-9532-5ebc-88b5-b6935d393a65" "step-b2b11a51-d093-57da-a2af-6ee6f4f4690b" "step-8c2976be-ba7a-5dd8-a390-f8f1e5aa8929" "step-59e44bcc-0c2e-57a7-8e71-45cfd8dd1e87" "step-e2232810-d75c-5b33-8c6d-124abfa15358"] :activity/first-step "step-9c211d01-9532-5ebc-88b5-b6935d393a65" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-9c211d01-9532-5ebc-88b5-b6935d393a65" :step/id #uuid "9c211d01-9532-5ebc-88b5-b6935d393a65" :step/content "question-270a37f5-30dc-586c-8bce-5a858bce0aaa" :step/next "step-b2b11a51-d093-57da-a2af-6ee6f4f4690b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b2b11a51-d093-57da-a2af-6ee6f4f4690b" :step/id #uuid "b2b11a51-d093-57da-a2af-6ee6f4f4690b" :step/content 17592186315020 :step/next "step-8c2976be-ba7a-5dd8-a390-f8f1e5aa8929" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8c2976be-ba7a-5dd8-a390-f8f1e5aa8929" :step/id #uuid "8c2976be-ba7a-5dd8-a390-f8f1e5aa8929" :step/content "question-f0bd5207-abfa-5ab3-9c43-fac18e98df50" :step/next "step-59e44bcc-0c2e-57a7-8e71-45cfd8dd1e87" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-59e44bcc-0c2e-57a7-8e71-45cfd8dd1e87" :step/id #uuid "59e44bcc-0c2e-57a7-8e71-45cfd8dd1e87" :step/content "question-8c279eae-dd78-5b67-912d-9a38a48eda28" :step/next "step-e2232810-d75c-5b33-8c6d-124abfa15358" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e2232810-d75c-5b33-8c6d-124abfa15358" :step/id #uuid "e2232810-d75c-5b33-8c6d-124abfa15358" :step/content "question-cd373585-64b2-5a1f-ad55-f3dee081e651" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092388 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-61246f8d-17fc-5cf4-84dc-d3e84d220ded"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-61246f8d-17fc-5cf4-84dc-d3e84d220ded" :question/id #uuid "61246f8d-17fc-5cf4-84dc-d3e84d220ded" :question/math-academy-id "q-166362" :question/problem "From the given data, the best forward difference approximation for the rate at which the number of customers was changing on June $1$st is{{field-1}}\ncustomers per month." :question/worked-solution "First, we note that June $1$st corresponds to $t=1.$\n\nWe use the forward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{q(t+h)-q(t)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $t=1.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible. In this case, we choose $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{q(1+1)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(2)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{120-59}{1} \\\\  & =61.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using a forward difference approximation, the rate of change in the number of customers on June $1$st is approximately $61$ customers per month." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cf8bd0d4-e2df-5b96-ba24-b2e56674f0f8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cf8bd0d4-e2df-5b96-ba24-b2e56674f0f8" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf8bd0d4-e2df-5b96-ba24-b2e56674f0f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-876c3c8e-19f9-56b2-a451-3c2578ba7af7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-876c3c8e-19f9-56b2-a451-3c2578ba7af7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-876c3c8e-19f9-56b2-a451-3c2578ba7af7" :answer/id #uuid "876c3c8e-19f9-56b2-a451-3c2578ba7af7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "61" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092391 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7a6777e5-075d-540c-87f4-265fdb022242"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7a6777e5-075d-540c-87f4-265fdb022242" :question/id #uuid "7a6777e5-075d-540c-87f4-265fdb022242" :question/math-academy-id "q-166363" :question/problem "From the given data, the best backward difference approximation for the rate at which the number of customers was changing on June $1$st is{{field-1}}\ncustomers per month." :question/worked-solution "Again, we use the fact that June $1$st corresponds to $t=1.$\n\nWe use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{q(t)-q(t-h)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $t=1.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible. In this case, we choose $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{q(1)-q(1-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(1)-q(0)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{59-0}{1} \\\\  & =59.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using a backward difference approximation, the rate of change in the number of customers on June $1$st is approximately $59$ customers per month." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-74caf0bf-cacd-5f28-aa72-d801b776ea5f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-74caf0bf-cacd-5f28-aa72-d801b776ea5f" :answer-field/id #uuid "74caf0bf-cacd-5f28-aa72-d801b776ea5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9593a22e-5aa8-5e2e-b0d9-870be17ad1c0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9593a22e-5aa8-5e2e-b0d9-870be17ad1c0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9593a22e-5aa8-5e2e-b0d9-870be17ad1c0" :answer/id #uuid "9593a22e-5aa8-5e2e-b0d9-870be17ad1c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "59" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092181 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ae9e49ed-eef5-5e75-8ccc-b6af327fb845"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ae9e49ed-eef5-5e75-8ccc-b6af327fb845" :question/id #uuid "ae9e49ed-eef5-5e75-8ccc-b6af327fb845" :question/math-academy-id "q-166364" :question/problem "Using a trapezoidal sum with two equal subintervals, ${\\int }_{0}^{4}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t≈${{field-1}}\n." :question/worked-solution "The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t≈\\frac{1}{2}[q(a)+2(q({t}_{1})+q({t}_{2})+…+q({t}_{n-1}))+q(b)]Δt,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δt=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n* The number of trapezoids is $n=2.$\n\n* The corresponding interval is $[0,4],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=4.$\n\n* The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{4-0}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t≈\\frac{1}{2}[q(0)+2q(2)+q(4)]\\cdot 2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ≈\\frac{1}{2}[0+2(120)+108]\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =240+108 \\\\  & =348.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dff10b37-1425-5f08-9969-ac5f0106b371"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dff10b37-1425-5f08-9969-ac5f0106b371" :answer-field/id #uuid "dff10b37-1425-5f08-9969-ac5f0106b371" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-32acdae7-2eaa-5d7b-bea5-1d4a19711cdc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-32acdae7-2eaa-5d7b-bea5-1d4a19711cdc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32acdae7-2eaa-5d7b-bea5-1d4a19711cdc" :answer/id #uuid "32acdae7-2eaa-5d7b-bea5-1d4a19711cdc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "348" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064267 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6b1c7ad5-e07c-5b25-9e08-a2e2aa0ac237"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-6b1c7ad5-e07c-5b25-9e08-a2e2aa0ac237" :question/id #uuid "6b1c7ad5-e07c-5b25-9e08-a2e2aa0ac237" :question/math-academy-id "q-166365" :question/problem "Use your answer to part 3 to approximate the average number of customers at the campsite during the first $4$ months.\n\nThe average number of customers at the campsite over the first four months was\n{{field-1}}\n." :question/worked-solution "The average value of a continuous function $q(t)$ on a closed interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\n{q}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}q(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ≈348.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{q}_{\\text{ave}}≈\\frac{348}{4-0}=87.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5847b751-f98d-563e-aaf4-cc2c04b1208d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5847b751-f98d-563e-aaf4-cc2c04b1208d" :answer-field/id #uuid "5847b751-f98d-563e-aaf4-cc2c04b1208d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a73ce7dc-ebea-528a-9250-92701e8d78cb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a73ce7dc-ebea-528a-9250-92701e8d78cb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a73ce7dc-ebea-528a-9250-92701e8d78cb" :answer/id #uuid "a73ce7dc-ebea-528a-9250-92701e8d78cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "87" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064273 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9de46d9e-0321-5243-8082-f7e3f4b58ad3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9de46d9e-0321-5243-8082-f7e3f4b58ad3" :question/id #uuid "9de46d9e-0321-5243-8082-f7e3f4b58ad3" :question/math-academy-id "q-166366" :question/problem "For $0\\le t\\le 8,$ the equation ${q}^{′}(t)=0$ has at least{{field-1}}\nsolution(s)." :question/worked-solution "The solutions to ${q}^{′}(t)=0$ correspond to the stationary points of the graph of $q(t).$\n\nSketching a possible graph of $q(t),$ we get the following:\n\n![](images/2f/2fd9e08520f4a671a255f360e583140175650de856a12233962c99a064c4c582.png)\n\nFrom our sketch, we notice three stationary points located near $t=2,$ $t=3,$ and $t=4.$\n\nTherefore, the equation ${q}^{′}(t)=0$ has at least three solutions." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-129d9d3d-517b-56d8-9503-4a5fa301d52f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-129d9d3d-517b-56d8-9503-4a5fa301d52f" :answer-field/id #uuid "129d9d3d-517b-56d8-9503-4a5fa301d52f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2adf1cf9-8c4d-5665-8948-53a09c6c23e5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2adf1cf9-8c4d-5665-8948-53a09c6c23e5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2adf1cf9-8c4d-5665-8948-53a09c6c23e5" :answer/id #uuid "2adf1cf9-8c4d-5665-8948-53a09c6c23e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "multistep-1f7f0079-ead3-5b48-9ed7-038c428c6b95" :multistep/id #uuid "1f7f0079-ead3-5b48-9ed7-038c428c6b95" :multistep/context "A campsite opens on the $1$st of May $(t=0)$ and closes after $8$ months. The number of customers in the campsite after $t$ months is modeled by a differentiable function $q$ for $0\\le t\\le 8.$ Some values for $q(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $t$ (months) | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $q(t)$ (customers) | $0$ | $59$ | $120$ | $92$ | $108$ | $85$ | $47$ | $18$ | $0$ |" :multistep/steps ["step-14dc92fe-b926-56a3-9447-0731e156f799" "step-65e9a74f-d4af-5e85-9717-677667dca31c" "step-6719078a-ae01-5b15-a69d-2c93b4d15fad" "step-766b9e6a-e65b-53da-b6e9-5f712795eacf" "step-c355678b-004a-527a-95e3-0498f895348c"] :multistep/first-step "step-14dc92fe-b926-56a3-9447-0731e156f799" :db/ensure [:multistep/validate]} {:db/id "step-14dc92fe-b926-56a3-9447-0731e156f799" :step/id #uuid "14dc92fe-b926-56a3-9447-0731e156f799" :step/content "question-61246f8d-17fc-5cf4-84dc-d3e84d220ded" :step/next "step-65e9a74f-d4af-5e85-9717-677667dca31c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-65e9a74f-d4af-5e85-9717-677667dca31c" :step/id #uuid "65e9a74f-d4af-5e85-9717-677667dca31c" :step/content "question-7a6777e5-075d-540c-87f4-265fdb022242" :step/next "step-6719078a-ae01-5b15-a69d-2c93b4d15fad" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6719078a-ae01-5b15-a69d-2c93b4d15fad" :step/id #uuid "6719078a-ae01-5b15-a69d-2c93b4d15fad" :step/content "question-ae9e49ed-eef5-5e75-8ccc-b6af327fb845" :step/next "step-766b9e6a-e65b-53da-b6e9-5f712795eacf" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-766b9e6a-e65b-53da-b6e9-5f712795eacf" :step/id #uuid "766b9e6a-e65b-53da-b6e9-5f712795eacf" :step/content "question-6b1c7ad5-e07c-5b25-9e08-a2e2aa0ac237" :step/next "step-c355678b-004a-527a-95e3-0498f895348c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c355678b-004a-527a-95e3-0498f895348c" :step/id #uuid "c355678b-004a-527a-95e3-0498f895348c" :step/content "question-9de46d9e-0321-5243-8082-f7e3f4b58ad3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "assigned-problem-a0690a2f-978b-5af9-8216-9a182879d18d" :assigned-problem/id #uuid "a0690a2f-978b-5af9-8216-9a182879d18d" :assigned-problem/content "multistep-1f7f0079-ead3-5b48-9ed7-038c428c6b95" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [17592186049934 17592186049236 17592186048154 17592186050572 17592186048123] :db/ensure [:assigned-problem/validate]} {:db/id "activity-bedb83e1-4c37-5446-a72c-fd137b4418d6" :activity/id #uuid "bedb83e1-4c37-5446-a72c-fd137b4418d6" :activity/title "Integrating Rates of Change From Tabular Data" :activity/type :activity.type/assignment :activity/steps ["step-367ea342-8ab9-51eb-810a-0e21d53f53dc"] :activity/first-step "step-367ea342-8ab9-51eb-810a-0e21d53f53dc" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-367ea342-8ab9-51eb-810a-0e21d53f53dc" :step/id #uuid "367ea342-8ab9-51eb-810a-0e21d53f53dc" :step/content "assigned-problem-a0690a2f-978b-5af9-8216-9a182879d18d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186133284 :answer-field/choices 17592186133285] [:db/retract 17592186133284 :answer-field/choices 17592186133286] [:db/retract 17592186133284 :answer-field/choices 17592186133287] [:db/retract 17592186133284 :answer-field/choices 17592186133288] [:db/retract 17592186133284 :answer-field/choices 17592186133289] {:db/id 17592186134541 :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-|x/2|}}{4},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x/2}}{2},\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x/2}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-t/2}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x/2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-x/2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x/2}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/2}}{2}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133284 :answer-field/correct "answer-b905210f-a059-572c-8e25-2b36e139ca53" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b905210f-a059-572c-8e25-2b36e139ca53" "answer-128e3b85-4be0-58d5-9120-cf5364e7369e" "answer-82c39e58-8d46-563b-b847-882b5527d6e1" "answer-c09d67cf-c692-53b9-9009-9755c3847a2d" "answer-9b72f51e-b7fa-5eaa-b4a9-881ec5d3b1ce"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b905210f-a059-572c-8e25-2b36e139ca53" :answer/id #uuid "b905210f-a059-572c-8e25-2b36e139ca53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-128e3b85-4be0-58d5-9120-cf5364e7369e" :answer/id #uuid "128e3b85-4be0-58d5-9120-cf5364e7369e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-x/2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82c39e58-8d46-563b-b847-882b5527d6e1" :answer/id #uuid "82c39e58-8d46-563b-b847-882b5527d6e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x/2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c09d67cf-c692-53b9-9009-9755c3847a2d" :answer/id #uuid "c09d67cf-c692-53b9-9009-9755c3847a2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x/2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b72f51e-b7fa-5eaa-b4a9-881ec5d3b1ce" :answer/id #uuid "9b72f51e-b7fa-5eaa-b4a9-881ec5d3b1ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x/2}}{2}-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129173 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-62a31be5-1f88-5fce-bc3d-c7a604a6e638"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-62a31be5-1f88-5fce-bc3d-c7a604a6e638" :question/id #uuid "62a31be5-1f88-5fce-bc3d-c7a604a6e638" :question/math-academy-id "q-72402" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}({i}^{2}-{j}^{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write out the inner summation by summing over the index $j:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}({i}^{2}-{j}^{2}) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}[{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}({i}^{2}-{j}^{2})] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}(({i}^{2}-{1}^{2})+({i}^{2}-{2}^{2})) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}(({i}^{2}-1)+({i}^{2}-4)) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}(2{i}^{2}-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we sum over the index $i:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}(2{i}^{2}-5) & =(2(1)^{2}-5)+(2(2)^{2}-5)+(2(3)^{2}-5) \\\\  & =-3+3+13 \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cb74bb6d-eb9e-5168-b33b-f718103e0618"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cb74bb6d-eb9e-5168-b33b-f718103e0618" :answer-field/id #uuid "cb74bb6d-eb9e-5168-b33b-f718103e0618" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-135c1c58-72fd-5e33-bb89-e656d31fd58a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f93f673f-1cdf-5fcb-8a52-d4d7a60f012f" "answer-135c1c58-72fd-5e33-bb89-e656d31fd58a" "answer-8f64f0ed-c125-50ec-80b4-0baad2e5331e" "answer-8fdbc633-5f94-527c-beb7-ec30a2b7cea8" "answer-8b3283bc-9a97-5ab1-beab-d6945922e38d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f93f673f-1cdf-5fcb-8a52-d4d7a60f012f" :answer/id #uuid "f93f673f-1cdf-5fcb-8a52-d4d7a60f012f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-135c1c58-72fd-5e33-bb89-e656d31fd58a" :answer/id #uuid "135c1c58-72fd-5e33-bb89-e656d31fd58a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f64f0ed-c125-50ec-80b4-0baad2e5331e" :answer/id #uuid "8f64f0ed-c125-50ec-80b4-0baad2e5331e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "19" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8fdbc633-5f94-527c-beb7-ec30a2b7cea8" :answer/id #uuid "8fdbc633-5f94-527c-beb7-ec30a2b7cea8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b3283bc-9a97-5ab1-beab-d6945922e38d" :answer/id #uuid "8b3283bc-9a97-5ab1-beab-d6945922e38d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6d3ae80f-587f-5674-9679-5efb33bf0d2c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-6d3ae80f-587f-5674-9679-5efb33bf0d2c" :question/id #uuid "6d3ae80f-587f-5674-9679-5efb33bf0d2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-157227" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+2{a}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+2{a}_{3} & =-3[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12+0+2 \\\\ 3+6+4 \\\\ 0+0+10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-993bd62a-640e-5656-8035-d20d8bad796e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-993bd62a-640e-5656-8035-d20d8bad796e" :answer-field/id #uuid "993bd62a-640e-5656-8035-d20d8bad796e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b1e669dd-ebc9-5d9b-a6c3-bf1f63c51806" :answer-field/choices ["answer-14d3b9a6-b42e-5d81-b5e8-d7e65c357f71" "answer-d63d1670-ebb4-54f4-afea-a48835538de1" "answer-b1e669dd-ebc9-5d9b-a6c3-bf1f63c51806" "answer-5a089876-2b6c-5a99-9e81-d189370977ea" "answer-6e02d051-6b0a-5a1c-8d4f-65d665e53a26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14d3b9a6-b42e-5d81-b5e8-d7e65c357f71" :answer/id #uuid "14d3b9a6-b42e-5d81-b5e8-d7e65c357f71" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d63d1670-ebb4-54f4-afea-a48835538de1" :answer/id #uuid "d63d1670-ebb4-54f4-afea-a48835538de1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1e669dd-ebc9-5d9b-a6c3-bf1f63c51806" :answer/id #uuid "b1e669dd-ebc9-5d9b-a6c3-bf1f63c51806" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a089876-2b6c-5a99-9e81-d189370977ea" :answer/id #uuid "5a089876-2b6c-5a99-9e81-d189370977ea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e02d051-6b0a-5a1c-8d4f-65d665e53a26" :answer/id #uuid "6e02d051-6b0a-5a1c-8d4f-65d665e53a26" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157394 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8da6073f-aed1-5284-a635-95231dda9375"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-8da6073f-aed1-5284-a635-95231dda9375" :question/id #uuid "8da6073f-aed1-5284-a635-95231dda9375" :question/math-academy-id "q-152896" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=3{x}^{2},\\,0\\le x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the expected value of ${X}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe compute the expected value of ${X}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,{x}^{5}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e4c5e0e7-c0ac-54ef-a1ca-a2167b6452cc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e4c5e0e7-c0ac-54ef-a1ca-a2167b6452cc" :answer-field/id #uuid "e4c5e0e7-c0ac-54ef-a1ca-a2167b6452cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-155ac80b-d257-59db-8d11-526c13b72b8a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-10f69397-caa8-5d90-9585-243065e0aedd" "answer-d870d643-7767-5376-bad6-e82c7fce52c8" "answer-155ac80b-d257-59db-8d11-526c13b72b8a" "answer-999ce95f-9709-5859-80e2-0586b0162a35" "answer-1ab35478-39a1-5c45-b7c4-35b9df820448"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10f69397-ca