ld/choices 17592186099805] [:db/retract 17592186099801 :answer-field/choices 17592186099806] {:db/id 17592186100803 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-99920a9c-2e7f-5c68-986e-e57bd85cf9e2" "question-4d55624d-78c7-5fb3-b79b-1987319cc6a3" "question-89569c74-5681-5d64-867b-d3414ad5af76" "question-2e2710e1-9e72-5ace-ad58-3320fc10bae0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100618 :question/problem "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what value(s) of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=-1?$\n![](images/ed/ed102b504e05beeadc9c50229066f14d39ff9f39730f122a40d3597861755486.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{kx+k} & =\\frac{(x+1)({x}^{2}-x+1)}{k(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x+1)}({x}^{2}-x+1)}{k\\cancel{(x+1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x+1}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can compute the limit at $x=-1,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -1} g(x) = \\lim_\\limits{x\\to -1} \\left(\\dfrac{x^2-x+1}{k}\\right) = \\dfrac{3}{k}.\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}g(x) & =g(-1) \\\\ \\frac{3}{k} & =\\frac{k}{3} \\\\ 9 & ={k}^{2} \\\\ \\pm 3 & =k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ the value of $k$ must be either $-3$ or $3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100804 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-791927d1-0973-588f-903c-f13b3a6de1fa" "question-8cbd258b-287d-5f95-b759-449d64d2387b" "question-7217df53-39b8-5ccc-a849-6b2d9370fbc6" "question-0a4ad86f-33ac-54b8-ac3c-2f4008b70bee" "question-74f22eb1-bd58-519a-94ee-cc4da7917518"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100619 :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. Find the values of $k$ for which $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$\n![](images/ad/ad328ab7996bbdacac0284ecf282c610bebc3ba7959ddb25dcc78123a5d91384.svg)" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x)$, the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x-12}{{x}^{2}-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)(x+6)}{(x-2)(x+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+6)}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{x+6}{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+6}{2+2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{2}^{kx} \\\\  & ={2}^{2k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $f(2) = 2^{2k}.$\n\nSo, for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ {2}^{2k} & =2 \\\\ {2}^{2k} & ={2}^{1} \\\\ 2k & =1 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\dfrac12$ removes the discontinuity at $x=2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-99920a9c-2e7f-5c68-986e-e57bd85cf9e2" :question/id #uuid "99920a9c-2e7f-5c68-986e-e57bd85cf9e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-48328" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\nh(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{k}^{2}}{x-k}, & x\\ne k \\\\ -4, & x=k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis continuous at $x=k$, find the value of the constant $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator and cancel any common factors: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{k}^{2}}{x-k} & =\\frac{(x+k)(x-k)}{(x-k)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+k)\\cancel{(x-k)}}{\\cancel{x-k}} \\\\  & =x+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then can compute the limit at $x=k,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to k}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to k}(x+k)=k+k=2k.\n$$\n\nFor $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=k,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(k) & ={lim}_{x\\to k}h(x) \\\\ -4 & =2k \\\\ k & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=k,$ we need $k=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3590515a-1849-55be-8356-2b733c344142"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3590515a-1849-55be-8356-2b733c344142" :answer-field/id #uuid "3590515a-1849-55be-8356-2b733c344142" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-11fcb03c-b0dc-571b-9c06-f1f1d60827c2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-34a4d18e-1792-5d9d-ae58-9c0973e08247" "answer-11fcb03c-b0dc-571b-9c06-f1f1d60827c2" "answer-77aa8fdd-48a9-5ace-b8c6-8519620e5478" "answer-8be42b75-aaaf-5b21-be6b-5588ca260123" "answer-02c13fe1-34eb-5051-86f7-23db73dc3195"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34a4d18e-1792-5d9d-ae58-9c0973e08247" :answer/id #uuid "34a4d18e-1792-5d9d-ae58-9c0973e08247" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11fcb03c-b0dc-571b-9c06-f1f1d60827c2" :answer/id #uuid "11fcb03c-b0dc-571b-9c06-f1f1d60827c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77aa8fdd-48a9-5ace-b8c6-8519620e5478" :answer/id #uuid "77aa8fdd-48a9-5ace-b8c6-8519620e5478" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8be42b75-aaaf-5b21-be6b-5588ca260123" :answer/id #uuid "8be42b75-aaaf-5b21-be6b-5588ca260123" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02c13fe1-34eb-5051-86f7-23db73dc3195" :answer/id #uuid "02c13fe1-34eb-5051-86f7-23db73dc3195" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4d55624d-78c7-5fb3-b79b-1987319cc6a3" :question/id #uuid "4d55624d-78c7-5fb3-b79b-1987319cc6a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-48747" :question/problem "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=2?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}, & x\\ne 2 \\\\ {k}^{2}-3k-12, & x=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-6x+8} & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-2)}{(x-4)(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)\\cancel{(x-2)}}{(x-4)\\cancel{(x-2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then can compute the limit at $x=2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x+2}{x-4}=\\frac{2+2}{2-4}=-2.\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x) \\\\ {k}^{2}-3k-12 & =-2 \\\\ {k}^{2}-3k-10 & =0 \\\\ (k+2)(k-5) & =0 \\\\ k & =-2,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=2,$ we need $k=-2$ or $k=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a9472cfa-409a-5fa2-b496-e7f99f19db09"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a9472cfa-409a-5fa2-b496-e7f99f19db09" :answer-field/id #uuid "a9472cfa-409a-5fa2-b496-e7f99f19db09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-77ea4d29-9341-5395-8064-d4d2e262dd6c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-81870a9d-5407-5ab4-b674-61633547aec5" "answer-77ea4d29-9341-5395-8064-d4d2e262dd6c" "answer-ad71f82c-bec2-5150-8281-5e8f0c047fd7" "answer-6379ff3d-9103-53e6-bcef-4d40c4bdcece" "answer-08c74c4e-8b61-5db7-8ead-406f11f4ccb0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81870a9d-5407-5ab4-b674-61633547aec5" :answer/id #uuid "81870a9d-5407-5ab4-b674-61633547aec5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77ea4d29-9341-5395-8064-d4d2e262dd6c" :answer/id #uuid "77ea4d29-9341-5395-8064-d4d2e262dd6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=-2,\\,5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad71f82c-bec2-5150-8281-5e8f0c047fd7" :answer/id #uuid "ad71f82c-bec2-5150-8281-5e8f0c047fd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6379ff3d-9103-53e6-bcef-4d40c4bdcece" :answer/id #uuid "6379ff3d-9103-53e6-bcef-4d40c4bdcece" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=3,\\,5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08c74c4e-8b61-5db7-8ead-406f11f4ccb0" :answer/id #uuid "08c74c4e-8b61-5db7-8ead-406f11f4ccb0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No such $k$ exists" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-89569c74-5681-5d64-867b-d3414ad5af76" :question/id #uuid "89569c74-5681-5d64-867b-d3414ad5af76" :question/math-academy-id "q-48431" :question/problem "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what value of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=1?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-3}{kx-k}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ k, & x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-3}{kx-k} & =\\frac{(x+3)(x-1)}{k(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)\\cancel{(x-1)}}{k\\cancel{(x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then can compute the limit at $x=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x+3}{k}=\\frac{4}{k}.\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x) \\\\ k & =\\frac{4}{k} \\\\ {k}^{2} & =4 \\\\ k & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ we need $k=\\pm 2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1c19081a-fb06-5be4-b08f-6665e6611016"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1c19081a-fb06-5be4-b08f-6665e6611016" :answer-field/id #uuid "1c19081a-fb06-5be4-b08f-6665e6611016" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-abff3922-0963-57a5-a0b4-d3c74b7e0c1e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-38cab349-df9b-52ec-bc21-60fef35eba02" "answer-abff3922-0963-57a5-a0b4-d3c74b7e0c1e" "answer-a91c2bb1-9c60-5fec-9eec-8ac3b0f23491" "answer-3fd2d0dc-499c-5f6b-a3b3-eabdda3e2c67" "answer-33a9709b-4752-524b-b8cc-b029ad81aa16"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38cab349-df9b-52ec-bc21-60fef35eba02" :answer/id #uuid "38cab349-df9b-52ec-bc21-60fef35eba02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-abff3922-0963-57a5-a0b4-d3c74b7e0c1e" :answer/id #uuid "abff3922-0963-57a5-a0b4-d3c74b7e0c1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm 2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a91c2bb1-9c60-5fec-9eec-8ac3b0f23491" :answer/id #uuid "a91c2bb1-9c60-5fec-9eec-8ac3b0f23491" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm 3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3fd2d0dc-499c-5f6b-a3b3-eabdda3e2c67" :answer/id #uuid "3fd2d0dc-499c-5f6b-a3b3-eabdda3e2c67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm 4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33a9709b-4752-524b-b8cc-b029ad81aa16" :answer/id #uuid "33a9709b-4752-524b-b8cc-b029ad81aa16" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No such $k$ exists" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2e2710e1-9e72-5ace-ad58-3320fc10bae0" :question/id #uuid "2e2710e1-9e72-5ace-ad58-3320fc10bae0" :question/math-academy-id "q-339609" :question/problem "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what value of $b$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=1?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-2}{{x}^{2}-1}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ b, & x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-2}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-1)}{(x+1)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)\\cancel{(x-1)}}{(x+1)\\cancel{(x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then can compute the limit at $x=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x+2}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x) \\\\ b & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ we need $b=\\frac{3}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9a0f8d27-8537-5244-ab23-ca98e0901930"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9a0f8d27-8537-5244-ab23-ca98e0901930" :answer-field/id #uuid "9a0f8d27-8537-5244-ab23-ca98e0901930" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ef8dc21b-1f1d-5862-a870-07149d4da58e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ef8dc21b-1f1d-5862-a870-07149d4da58e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef8dc21b-1f1d-5862-a870-07149d4da58e" :answer/id #uuid "ef8dc21b-1f1d-5862-a870-07149d4da58e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101440 :question/problem "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=-2?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+5x+6}{kx+2k}, & x\\ne -2 \\\\ k, & x=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+5x+6}{kx+2k} & =\\frac{(x+3)(x+2)}{k(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{k\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then can compute the limit at $x=-2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to -2}(\\frac{x+3}{k})=\\frac{1}{k}.\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-2) & ={lim}_{x\\to -2}g(x) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{k} \\\\ {k}^{2} & =1 \\\\ k & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=-2,$ we need $k=\\pm 1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186099801 :answer-field/correct "answer-e27fce12-ba0e-569d-9f95-ba338484dfd7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e27fce12-ba0e-569d-9f95-ba338484dfd7" "answer-a78a608b-de9f-5aa1-a04e-3fd289b0efef" "answer-c6e5d373-36bb-58f3-a752-14e6513fd5a3" "answer-0c60f8f4-7300-5616-8f6e-c3d7dff732be"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e27fce12-ba0e-569d-9f95-ba338484dfd7" :answer/id #uuid "e27fce12-ba0e-569d-9f95-ba338484dfd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a78a608b-de9f-5aa1-a04e-3fd289b0efef" :answer/id #uuid "a78a608b-de9f-5aa1-a04e-3fd289b0efef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm 2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6e5d373-36bb-58f3-a752-14e6513fd5a3" :answer/id #uuid "c6e5d373-36bb-58f3-a752-14e6513fd5a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm 3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c60f8f4-7300-5616-8f6e-c3d7dff732be" :answer/id #uuid "0c60f8f4-7300-5616-8f6e-c3d7dff732be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm 5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-791927d1-0973-588f-903c-f13b3a6de1fa" :question/id #uuid "791927d1-0973-588f-903c-f13b3a6de1fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-38218" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. Find the value of $a,$ if any, for which $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-2}, & x<2 \\\\ ax, & x\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$ the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{1}{x-2}=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}ax=2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-\\infty ,$ there is no value of $a$ that makes $f(x)$ continuous at $x=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d952cf65-eeb0-5f71-bdd7-74da23f0cdb0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d952cf65-eeb0-5f71-bdd7-74da23f0cdb0" :answer-field/id #uuid "d952cf65-eeb0-5f71-bdd7-74da23f0cdb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f9788b21-56ae-57c4-93e9-f119a266299c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d566f98a-89c0-58c8-8c69-7c676365310d" "answer-18ec3272-fdde-5438-a3f5-14bb77bfe0f1" "answer-116c5e43-e04a-55da-ac25-b50adf12ac90" "answer-9fd1633d-5ccc-5e39-88e8-48ddb0a2b021" "answer-f9788b21-56ae-57c4-93e9-f119a266299c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d566f98a-89c0-58c8-8c69-7c676365310d" :answer/id #uuid "d566f98a-89c0-58c8-8c69-7c676365310d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18ec3272-fdde-5438-a3f5-14bb77bfe0f1" :answer/id #uuid "18ec3272-fdde-5438-a3f5-14bb77bfe0f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-116c5e43-e04a-55da-ac25-b50adf12ac90" :answer/id #uuid "116c5e43-e04a-55da-ac25-b50adf12ac90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9fd1633d-5ccc-5e39-88e8-48ddb0a2b021" :answer/id #uuid "9fd1633d-5ccc-5e39-88e8-48ddb0a2b021" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9788b21-56ae-57c4-93e9-f119a266299c" :answer/id #uuid "f9788b21-56ae-57c4-93e9-f119a266299c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No such $a$ exists" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8cbd258b-287d-5f95-b759-449d64d2387b" :question/id #uuid "8cbd258b-287d-5f95-b759-449d64d2387b" :question/math-academy-id "q-303064" :question/problem "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=k?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}-8}{x-2}, & x<k \\\\ -2k-x, & x\\ge k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nArranged in **ascending order**,\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,k=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $g(x)$, the left-sided limit of $g(x)$ at $x=k$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{3}-8}{x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)({x}^{2}+2x+4)}{x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-2)}({x}^{2}+2x+4)}{\\cancel{x-2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}({x}^{2}+2x+4) \\\\  & ={k}^{2}+2k+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {k}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{+}}(-2k-x) \\\\  & =-2k-k \\\\  & =-3k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $g(k)=-3k.$ \n\nSo, for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=k,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ {k}^{2}+2k+4 & =-3k \\\\ {k}^{2}+5k+4 & =0 \\\\ (k+4)(k+1) & =0 \\\\ k & =-4,\\,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous at $x=k$ for the values\n\n$$\nk=-4\\,\\text{and}\\,k=-1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e5e3e126-997f-5916-8a3a-4894a4d9dc44" "answer-field-ec3eded7-8b33-51f5-aa61-b08150e3ba4f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e5e3e126-997f-5916-8a3a-4894a4d9dc44" :answer-field/id #uuid "e5e3e126-997f-5916-8a3a-4894a4d9dc44" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-bd57730f-adde-55d2-8ce1-828a6c17d39f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bd57730f-adde-55d2-8ce1-828a6c17d39f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd57730f-adde-55d2-8ce1-828a6c17d39f" :answer/id #uuid "bd57730f-adde-55d2-8ce1-828a6c17d39f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ec3eded7-8b33-51f5-aa61-b08150e3ba4f" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec3eded7-8b33-51f5-aa61-b08150e3ba4f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-cd55c584-98f3-5aaa-a34b-c5c74f5be225" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cd55c584-98f3-5aaa-a34b-c5c74f5be225"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd55c584-98f3-5aaa-a34b-c5c74f5be225" :answer/id #uuid "cd55c584-98f3-5aaa-a34b-c5c74f5be225" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7217df53-39b8-5ccc-a849-6b2d9370fbc6" :question/id #uuid "7217df53-39b8-5ccc-a849-6b2d9370fbc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-340826" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k>0$ is $f(x)$ continuous at $x=-2?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-14}{x+2}, & x<-2 \\\\ {k}^{1-x}-10, & x\\ge -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$ the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-14}{x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-7)(x+2)}{x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-7)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{\\cancel{x+2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}(x-7) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}({k}^{1-x}-10) \\\\  & ={k}^{1-(-2)}-10 \\\\  & ={k}^{3}-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $f(-2)={k}^{3}-10$ \n\nSo, for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ {k}^{3}-10 & =-9 \\\\ {k}^{3} & =1 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=-2,$ we need $k=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-be01bb43-e1fd-5d92-8d3e-37f18a32f3af"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-be01bb43-e1fd-5d92-8d3e-37f18a32f3af" :answer-field/id #uuid "be01bb43-e1fd-5d92-8d3e-37f18a32f3af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-25ccf6a7-e1da-5ef8-a030-22413513df5f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-25ccf6a7-e1da-5ef8-a030-22413513df5f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25ccf6a7-e1da-5ef8-a030-22413513df5f" :answer/id #uuid "25ccf6a7-e1da-5ef8-a030-22413513df5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0a4ad86f-33ac-54b8-ac3c-2f4008b70bee" :question/id #uuid "0a4ad86f-33ac-54b8-ac3c-2f4008b70bee" :question/math-academy-id "q-48751" :question/problem "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=-3?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+14x+33}{2kx+6k}, & x<-3 \\\\ -\\frac{kx}{3}, & x\\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $g(x)$, the left-sided limit of $g(x)$ at $x=-3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{2}+14x+33}{2kx+6k} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{(x+11)(x+3)}{2k(x+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{(x+11)\\cancel{(x+3)}}{2k\\cancel{(x+3)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{x+11}{2k} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}(-\\frac{kx}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{k(-3)}{3} \\\\  & =k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $g(-3)=k.$ \n\nSo, for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-3,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}g(x) \\\\ k & =\\frac{4}{k} \\\\ {k}^{2} & =4 \\\\ k & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=-2$ or $k=2$ remove the discontinuity at $x=-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ee0499b8-2a39-5262-89e9-bfc3b9bddd29"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ee0499b8-2a39-5262-89e9-bfc3b9bddd29" :answer-field/id #uuid "ee0499b8-2a39-5262-89e9-bfc3b9bddd29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-13a86203-0bc5-55e2-986d-c1c309e6ee38" :answer-field/choices ["answer-13a86203-0bc5-55e2-986d-c1c309e6ee38" "answer-17d0dd42-5a1b-52aa-becb-f58c46712b06" "answer-84972923-41cf-5a0d-9170-7092398d68bb" "answer-560d4297-737e-51ee-b485-c82f9de8306b" "answer-93fd1a29-955a-551f-9dee-8f81ad48e8f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13a86203-0bc5-55e2-986d-c1c309e6ee38" :answer/id #uuid "13a86203-0bc5-55e2-986d-c1c309e6ee38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm 2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17d0dd42-5a1b-52aa-becb-f58c46712b06" :answer/id #uuid "17d0dd42-5a1b-52aa-becb-f58c46712b06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm 4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84972923-41cf-5a0d-9170-7092398d68bb" :answer/id #uuid "84972923-41cf-5a0d-9170-7092398d68bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-560d4297-737e-51ee-b485-c82f9de8306b" :answer/id #uuid "560d4297-737e-51ee-b485-c82f9de8306b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93fd1a29-955a-551f-9dee-8f81ad48e8f1" :answer/id #uuid "93fd1a29-955a-551f-9dee-8f81ad48e8f1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No such $k$ exists" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-74f22eb1-bd58-519a-94ee-cc4da7917518" :question/id #uuid "74f22eb1-bd58-519a-94ee-cc4da7917518" :question/math-academy-id "q-339855" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $f(x)$ continuous at $x=2?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15}{x+3}, & x<2 \\\\ 2x+k, & x\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x)$, the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{15}{x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2+3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(2x+k) \\\\  & =2(2)+k \\\\  & =4+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $f(2)=4+k.$ \n\nSo, for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ 4+k & =3 \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous at $x=2$ for the value $k=-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9629635b-88c4-533d-9081-d8a1cc525da1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9629635b-88c4-533d-9081-d8a1cc525da1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9629635b-88c4-533d-9081-d8a1cc525da1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-60a7f976-f4de-5958-8041-40d291b11ea0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-60a7f976-f4de-5958-8041-40d291b11ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60a7f976-f4de-5958-8041-40d291b11ea0" :answer/id #uuid "60a7f976-f4de-5958-8041-40d291b11ea0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101995 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)$ below:\n![](images/cb/cbdbe44a94ff036c74957691e30ed3652d50d2a8dbaa50b29827408f242492bb.svg)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a point discontinuity at $x=1.$ However, by selecting a particular value for $k,$ we can remove the point discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $k$ by forcing the limit of the function to be equal to the value of the function at $x=1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x) = f(1)\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x)$ represents the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ gets closer and closer, but not equal, to $1.$ So, when evaluating $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x),$ we need to use the function definition for $x \\neq 1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{2}+x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{(x-1)(x+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x+1)}{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x+1}{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{1+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=1,$ the function is defined as $f(x)=k.$ So, $f(1)=k$, and for continuity we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & =k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=1,$ we need to choose $k=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101077 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133837 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-dcb73b7c-8cd5-5969-93ed-1bf898ab0795"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-dcb73b7c-8cd5-5969-93ed-1bf898ab0795" :question/id #uuid "dcb73b7c-8cd5-5969-93ed-1bf898ab0795" :question/math-academy-id "q-140491" :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|?$\n\n$$\nU=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{17}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\sqrt{17}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3},$ and from the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=3,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}}=\\sqrt{34}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|=|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}|+|\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{34}}|+|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}|=\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{34}}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4e24f321-343d-593f-9ede-112a987e4533"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4e24f321-343d-593f-9ede-112a987e4533" :answer-field/id #uuid "4e24f321-343d-593f-9ede-112a987e4533" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c5abdd46-3123-599e-ba75-96985fec9ea2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c5abdd46-3123-599e-ba75-96985fec9ea2" "answer-07549629-124a-5c26-94d8-775576771467" "answer-530f1d3c-21d1-551b-a2d2-11e6d377597a" "answer-09a831bd-413d-5031-9d8f-47f63a86dc4c" "answer-293f3ef5-7bd8-58b9-8d4d-52c8d95cc564"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5abdd46-3123-599e-ba75-96985fec9ea2" :answer/id #uuid "c5abdd46-3123-599e-ba75-96985fec9ea2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{34}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07549629-124a-5c26-94d8-775576771467" :answer/id #uuid "07549629-124a-5c26-94d8-775576771467" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{34}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-530f1d3c-21d1-551b-a2d2-11e6d377597a" :answer/id #uuid "530f1d3c-21d1-551b-a2d2-11e6d377597a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{34}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09a831bd-413d-5031-9d8f-47f63a86dc4c" :answer/id #uuid "09a831bd-413d-5031-9d8f-47f63a86dc4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{14}{\\sqrt{34}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-293f3ef5-7bd8-58b9-8d4d-52c8d95cc564" :answer/id #uuid "293f3ef5-7bd8-58b9-8d4d-52c8d95cc564" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{34}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-31a4950c-e57e-5388-9d71-5baa611ca7b2" :activity/id #uuid "31a4950c-e57e-5388-9d71-5baa611ca7b2" :activity/title "Orthogonal Sets in Euclidean Spaces" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049811 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-e74678fc-cdaa-5c80-82f2-06de36ea9535" "step-7a87ebea-2c02-5322-b156-afea02e5b0a3" "step-34d88b52-a0d9-5edb-9036-074e82704885" "step-44855349-ccd7-57b0-9849-4398509b96fd" "step-f7d71d9d-b1d2-5a1f-a4ea-4a18a90b1556"] :activity/first-step "step-e74678fc-cdaa-5c80-82f2-06de36ea9535" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-e74678fc-cdaa-5c80-82f2-06de36ea9535" :step/id #uuid "e74678fc-cdaa-5c80-82f2-06de36ea9535" :step/content 17592186134220 :step/next "step-7a87ebea-2c02-5322-b156-afea02e5b0a3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7a87ebea-2c02-5322-b156-afea02e5b0a3" :step/id #uuid "7a87ebea-2c02-5322-b156-afea02e5b0a3" :step/content 17592186134283 :step/next "step-34d88b52-a0d9-5edb-9036-074e82704885" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-34d88b52-a0d9-5edb-9036-074e82704885" :step/id #uuid "34d88b52-a0d9-5edb-9036-074e82704885" :step/content 17592186326836 :step/next "step-44855349-ccd7-57b0-9849-4398509b96fd" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-44855349-ccd7-57b0-9849-4398509b96fd" :step/id #uuid "44855349-ccd7-57b0-9849-4398509b96fd" :step/content 17592186272107 :step/next "step-f7d71d9d-b1d2-5a1f-a4ea-4a18a90b1556" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f7d71d9d-b1d2-5a1f-a4ea-4a18a90b1556" :step/id #uuid "f7d71d9d-b1d2-5a1f-a4ea-4a18a90b1556" :step/content "question-dcb73b7c-8cd5-5969-93ed-1bf898ab0795" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199502 :tutorial/content "It's possible to derive Taylor polynomials for functions of more than two variables. However, increasing the number of variables significantly increases the number of terms in the Taylor polynomial. \n\nIdeally, we'd like a compact expression for the quadratic Taylor polynomial that works for all twice-differentiable functions $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R.$ This can be achieved using matrix notation. \n\nTo see how, let's consider the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f(x,y)$ about $(a,b){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote ![](images/6f/6fe1832d2f4ae2b36446386e5d0ec7c41f898f4e575af9238695c37f7c427250.svg) and ![](images/49/496eacb98651e3fab4c0d0a7b44ab283fadad1c07243da485aeb0c4a059d5712.svg) then the total derivative of $f(x,y)=f(\\mathbf{x})$ at $(a,b)=\\mathbf{a}$ is given by\n\n![](images/d6/d696700e295d9f43111ac53602b936a991b7766af654cedaa5afdae21b088a59.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, we can rewrite the linear part of the approximation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Linear approximation} & =f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b) \\\\  & =f(a)+[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(a,b) & {f}_{y}(a,b)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x-a \\\\ y-b\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =f(a)+[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(a,b) & {f}_{y}(a,b)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot ([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is just our usual linear approximation of a function $f.$\n\nThe second derivative of $f$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n![](images/de/de2a75a6681e1e393b21b60de9d7aeee748a8e6d04010ab713b40f0f41f239cf.svg)\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $f_{xy} = f_{yx},$ we can rewrite the quadratic part of the approximation using matrix multiplication as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Quadratic part} & =\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}x-a & y-b\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(a) & {f}_{xy}(a) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(a) & {f}_{yy}(a)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x-a \\\\ y-b\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}([\\begin{aligned}x & y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}a & b\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(a) & {f}_{xy}(a) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(a) & {f}_{yy}(a)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot ([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our quadratic Taylor polynomial can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)}}+{{\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this formula is more compact and has clear similarity with the corresponding formula for functions with one variable:\n\n$$\nf(x) \\approx  \\overbrace{f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} + \\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(a)(x-a)^2}_{\\textrm{Quadratic part}}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100961 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f1f17c93-4d26-549b-8492-2ec95bf66e77" "question-47b1ac6a-9da0-5717-9e95-4b0413ae2c6c" "question-55621ada-4f38-5ee1-82ac-26e8704cbd38" "question-e22ec0ee-78ef-534d-a2b2-dbeef1b12436"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100581 :question/problem "![](images/3e/3e9a879522107eb2b2381a688d1173fdf6a93a8b81686fd2f21c815008ec35df.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,3].$ The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $10.$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,3]$ given that $f(-2)=3.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,3),$ and $f'(x)\\neq 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,\\,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =3 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3+(-10) \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{3,-7\\} = 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{max}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{max}=-2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100858 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-27335de7-ab94-5f77-a70c-95b3aeec92aa" "question-065ae9b3-7009-5705-869a-1bfc3920deab" "question-488dbbc3-e305-587b-8387-bc6f8dfc6d19" "question-3b3942d1-426d-544a-a802-5cef91893443"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100699 :question/problem "The function $f$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,6].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown below, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $6$ and $2$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,6]$ given that $f(0)=1.$\n\n![](images/7c/7ca8e07d3438890b4f1e220e491d4a20f35c7473a31e76426b9a1fa13ed6926c.png)" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,6),$ and $f'(x)=0$ at $x=4.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,4,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =1 \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+6 \\\\  & =7 \\\\ f(6) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+6-2 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{1, 7, 5\\} = 7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{max}}=7,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{max}=4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100857 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c4376358-d419-5ca5-9d5f-60ee48a180a3" "question-457b4315-a413-5f6e-81e5-9a7aa0226cf4" "question-a52ab505-f7f2-54ed-afb3-b43bed5cfef5" "question-907c4d01-952e-54f5-819a-03c35c5b1210" "question-de089661-beea-5ee0-bfa5-18ccd2aea3c7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100698 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $1 \\leq x \\leq 5.$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown below. Find a value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,5]$ given that $f(1)=-2.$\n\n![](images/e4/e4327bc76ae23907fb628cc9e9d5d045ab773070f9ce8fc2972dcf78e434b24d.png)" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,5),$ and $f'(x)=0$ at $x=2$ and $x=4.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,2,4,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $3$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/7f/7f129ef5fe5d219f1bf57425682cea13ff657b9a539dc7ae0b2d172bf37c2c28.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-2 \\\\ f(2) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(4) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{3}{2}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ f(5) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{3}{2}-1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} =  \\max\\left\\lbrace -2,-\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{3}{2},-1 \\right\\rbrace = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{max}}=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{max}=2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101160 :question/problem "![](images/06/0669cf5e31d559c70c22c5154c0e43638eb5df8a11bedab1e5eea4dc4d7ab90f.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $7.$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,2]$ given that $f(-2)=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =2 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+7 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,9\\}=9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=9,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186098295 :answer-field/correct 17592186098300 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-f1f17c93-4d26-549b-8492-2ec95bf66e77" :question/id #uuid "f1f17c93-4d26-549b-8492-2ec95bf66e77" :question/math-academy-id "q-141882" :question/problem "![](images/82/82a08c804381b11ae118691d82c4801b655cd8e0b131677f67ed6e63b8400d7c.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[2,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $13.$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[2,6]$ given that $f(2)=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[2,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (2,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are$x=2,\\,6.$ \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =-2 \\\\ f(6) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{6}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2+13 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-2,11\\}=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=11,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-80ff88f9-afef-5744-91de-630c6ef0b4b2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-80ff88f9-afef-5744-91de-630c6ef0b4b2" :answer-field/id #uuid "80ff88f9-afef-5744-91de-630c6ef0b4b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-71962e20-5aa7-5aa9-bce4-7e12d2356e64" :answer-field/choices ["answer-71962e20-5aa7-5aa9-bce4-7e12d2356e64" "answer-da3d597e-169f-5c12-981e-e45eb43612aa" "answer-b076a541-1d5b-5195-a894-5f6fb12c5287" "answer-1ba863d0-df64-50d8-8f3e-d48da3e80c4a" "answer-e8ce9f06-450e-5881-8fac-5218ef358d3e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71962e20-5aa7-5aa9-bce4-7e12d2356e64" :answer/id #uuid "71962e20-5aa7-5aa9-bce4-7e12d2356e64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da3d597e-169f-5c12-981e-e45eb43612aa" :answer/id #uuid "da3d597e-169f-5c12-981e-e45eb43612aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b076a541-1d5b-5195-a894-5f6fb12c5287" :answer/id #uuid "b076a541-1d5b-5195-a894-5f6fb12c5287" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "19" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ba863d0-df64-50d8-8f3e-d48da3e80c4a" :answer/id #uuid "1ba863d0-df64-50d8-8f3e-d48da3e80c4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8ce9f06-450e-5881-8fac-5218ef358d3e" :answer/id #uuid "e8ce9f06-450e-5881-8fac-5218ef358d3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-47b1ac6a-9da0-5717-9e95-4b0413ae2c6c" :question/id #uuid "47b1ac6a-9da0-5717-9e95-4b0413ae2c6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343665" :question/problem "![](images/d7/d7aa23647a61b4d13e9468eafc34947e8b01046a31d67da8ee997476e13dec95.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[1,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $2\\pi .$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,5]$ given that $f(1)=-3\\pi .$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-3\\pi  \\\\ f(5) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\pi -2\\pi  \\\\  & =-5\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-3\\pi ,-5\\pi \\}=-3\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-3\\pi ,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-33a1b22c-006e-5530-8085-81d9408aea6b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-33a1b22c-006e-5530-8085-81d9408aea6b" :answer-field/id #uuid "33a1b22c-006e-5530-8085-81d9408aea6b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-fc09cc98-aa3a-5fb7-8896-022fdc71a474" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fc09cc98-aa3a-5fb7-8896-022fdc71a474"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc09cc98-aa3a-5fb7-8896-022fdc71a474" :answer/id #uuid "fc09cc98-aa3a-5fb7-8896-022fdc71a474" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-55621ada-4f38-5ee1-82ac-26e8704cbd38" :question/id #uuid "55621ada-4f38-5ee1-82ac-26e8704cbd38" :question/math-academy-id "q-141619" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa70775eb1b2968406c61e1bebad52139f492890e71a9977582fc1898ba5ee8f.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $9.$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,2]$ given that $f(-2)=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\n We see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,\\,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =2 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-9 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,-7\\}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=2,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f5acdfa9-775d-577e-ae8c-9aadaf73c349"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f5acdfa9-775d-577e-ae8c-9aadaf73c349" :answer-field/id #uuid "f5acdfa9-775d-577e-ae8c-9aadaf73c349" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-36f1c22b-e984-57a9-89c4-5c0b33dfae7a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bd46d8fd-7d57-5880-a3f6-77bb4a90c0f5" "answer-238fef3c-4f22-5034-924b-0380723ee9db" "answer-36f1c22b-e984-57a9-89c4-5c0b33dfae7a" "answer-c6e926f2-c0b7-5989-b9fe-45190a83cbe6" "answer-295da83