l theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =3 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-6 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-3,3\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-99541a9b-c65b-5a54-8eab-cc4568e265c3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-99541a9b-c65b-5a54-8eab-cc4568e265c3" :answer-field/id #uuid "99541a9b-c65b-5a54-8eab-cc4568e265c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-da227ff9-882b-5bb9-b70a-72f338b132dd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-066d1a43-ed49-5af5-b3b0-3b9a16e1713f" "answer-4fcd42e4-12ae-595c-9c10-d746ae13d982" "answer-06649de1-9bda-5107-be59-afa45a2653cf" "answer-da227ff9-882b-5bb9-b70a-72f338b132dd" "answer-cf1432d6-a3c5-5ca4-b770-1eb65c25bbc8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-066d1a43-ed49-5af5-b3b0-3b9a16e1713f" :answer/id #uuid "066d1a43-ed49-5af5-b3b0-3b9a16e1713f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4fcd42e4-12ae-595c-9c10-d746ae13d982" :answer/id #uuid "4fcd42e4-12ae-595c-9c10-d746ae13d982" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06649de1-9bda-5107-be59-afa45a2653cf" :answer/id #uuid "06649de1-9bda-5107-be59-afa45a2653cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da227ff9-882b-5bb9-b70a-72f338b132dd" :answer/id #uuid "da227ff9-882b-5bb9-b70a-72f338b132dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf1432d6-a3c5-5ca4-b770-1eb65c25bbc8" :answer/id #uuid "cf1432d6-a3c5-5ca4-b770-1eb65c25bbc8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-27335de7-ab94-5f77-a70c-95b3aeec92aa" :question/id #uuid "27335de7-ab94-5f77-a70c-95b3aeec92aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-343704" :question/problem "![](images/a3/a3f57d0658f89f37df9a838b4a79cb9d4ed3d4183187013923c26fb3bd836e10.png)\n\nThe function $k(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,7].$ The graph of ${k}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $k(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4,$ $7,$ and $2$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $k(x)$ on the interval $[-2,7]$ given that $k(-2)=2.$\n\n${k}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $k(x)$ on $[-2,7]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${k}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,7),$ and ${k}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1,5.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,1,5,7.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $k$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(-2) & =2 \\\\ k(1) & =k(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{k}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-4 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ k(5) & =k(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{5}{k}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(-4+7) \\\\  & =5 \\\\ k(7) & =k(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{7}{k}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(-4+7-2) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $k$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $k$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{k}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,-2,5,3\\}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $k$ is ${k}_{\\text{max}}=5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $k(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4003c727-78b9-502a-bc48-15371f44dc21"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4003c727-78b9-502a-bc48-15371f44dc21" :answer-field/id #uuid "4003c727-78b9-502a-bc48-15371f44dc21" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-80f28cbc-9bc5-54e6-97e1-466a4f97e761" :answer-field/choices ["answer-80f28cbc-9bc5-54e6-97e1-466a4f97e761"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80f28cbc-9bc5-54e6-97e1-466a4f97e761" :answer/id #uuid "80f28cbc-9bc5-54e6-97e1-466a4f97e761" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101284 :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f2d06871f14bead2e13d9fcb373560dcd74b30f74c3d8f9a6319762130ec162.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4$ and $2$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,3]$ given that $f(-3)=-6.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =-6 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6-4 \\\\  & =-10 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6-4+2 \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-6,-10,-8\\}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-6,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186099025 :answer-field/correct 17592186099026 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-065ae9b3-7009-5705-869a-1bfc3920deab" :question/id #uuid "065ae9b3-7009-5705-869a-1bfc3920deab" :question/math-academy-id "q-343672" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5f0d970e95b7e2e2656ef579f9d5be49eb9eddb433a5921149347ef349139c5.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,5].$ The graph of ${g}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $g(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4$ and $6$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $g(x)$ on the interval $[-3,5]$ given that $g(-3)=2.$\n\n${g}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $g(x)$ on $[-3,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${g}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,5),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,0,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $g$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-3) & =2 \\\\ g(0) & =g(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+4 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ g(5) & =g(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{5}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(4-6) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $g$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $g$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,6,0\\}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $g$ is ${g}_{\\text{max}}=6,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $g(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b71666a0-c57b-5c11-820a-d694a30bc7a9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b71666a0-c57b-5c11-820a-d694a30bc7a9" :answer-field/id #uuid "b71666a0-c57b-5c11-820a-d694a30bc7a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f93c0d67-7bd9-5f1c-85c2-95df089580db" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f93c0d67-7bd9-5f1c-85c2-95df089580db"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f93c0d67-7bd9-5f1c-85c2-95df089580db" :answer/id #uuid "f93c0d67-7bd9-5f1c-85c2-95df089580db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-488dbbc3-e305-587b-8387-bc6f8dfc6d19" :question/id #uuid "488dbbc3-e305-587b-8387-bc6f8dfc6d19" :question/math-academy-id "q-15742" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d8500caba027f22623c3e20287f92f12e56aad667788708189f68205f4122195.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,9].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4,$ $8,$ and $2$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,9]$ given that $f(0)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,9]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,9),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=3,7.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,3,7,9.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =1 \\\\ f(3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(7) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{7}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+4-8 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ f(9) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{9}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+4-8+2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{1,5,-3,-1\\}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-878756a2-046d-51e8-8047-b9ae0e732f64"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-878756a2-046d-51e8-8047-b9ae0e732f64" :answer-field/id #uuid "878756a2-046d-51e8-8047-b9ae0e732f64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0b09df16-6636-5d5a-a07e-b977e5c99a48" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5670001a-de2c-5e94-bfc2-8dd60d548162" "answer-809430b8-a1ca-5594-b25f-9c41dda2a948" "answer-0b09df16-6636-5d5a-a07e-b977e5c99a48" "answer-2ead93a4-4662-5088-8f13-942128d4fcae" "answer-4291ba92-5f2c-5dc3-a5db-2d7ca1b77cfb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5670001a-de2c-5e94-bfc2-8dd60d548162" :answer/id #uuid "5670001a-de2c-5e94-bfc2-8dd60d548162" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-809430b8-a1ca-5594-b25f-9c41dda2a948" :answer/id #uuid "809430b8-a1ca-5594-b25f-9c41dda2a948" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b09df16-6636-5d5a-a07e-b977e5c99a48" :answer/id #uuid "0b09df16-6636-5d5a-a07e-b977e5c99a48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ead93a4-4662-5088-8f13-942128d4fcae" :answer/id #uuid "2ead93a4-4662-5088-8f13-942128d4fcae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4291ba92-5f2c-5dc3-a5db-2d7ca1b77cfb" :answer/id #uuid "4291ba92-5f2c-5dc3-a5db-2d7ca1b77cfb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3b3942d1-426d-544a-a802-5cef91893443" :question/id #uuid "3b3942d1-426d-544a-a802-5cef91893443" :question/math-academy-id "q-343680" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d4b4735b3154603284ed6f232ae640c5f47a536a5c26b75bb99970c8d0cb2c08.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,2].$ The graph of ${h}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $h(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $5$ and $3$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $h(x)$ on the interval $[-4,2]$ given that $h(-4)=-6.$\n\n${h}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $h(x)$ on $[-4,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${h}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,2),$ and ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $h$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-4) & =-6 \\\\ h(-1) & =h(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6+5 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ h(2) & =h(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{2}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6+(5-3) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $h$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $h$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{h}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-6,-1,-4\\}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $h$ is ${h}_{\\text{max}}=-1,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $h(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8811494e-64ac-563f-b9e3-157298f88e64"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8811494e-64ac-563f-b9e3-157298f88e64" :answer-field/id #uuid "8811494e-64ac-563f-b9e3-157298f88e64" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-97ad1452-16bf-5aaa-b266-a0c4eefba9e3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-97ad1452-16bf-5aaa-b266-a0c4eefba9e3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97ad1452-16bf-5aaa-b266-a0c4eefba9e3" :answer/id #uuid "97ad1452-16bf-5aaa-b266-a0c4eefba9e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c4376358-d419-5ca5-9d5f-60ee48a180a3" :question/id #uuid "c4376358-d419-5ca5-9d5f-60ee48a180a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-343902" :question/problem "![](images/86/8678ae0607275abf4732943add33116314569efc3afb44e92a15d6e78eeacd64.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[-4,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ if $f(-4)=-5.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,1,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of one square, two trapezoids, and one triangle. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/7f/7fe0ee64fd29812d2ee536d7285f5e4674d8985a2dc1e27bf230dafb0159046e.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =-5 \\\\ f(1) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5+(1+5+4) \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5-3 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-5,5,2\\}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ef945b41-3caa-5828-922f-822c233714fe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ef945b41-3caa-5828-922f-822c233714fe" :answer-field/id #uuid "ef945b41-3caa-5828-922f-822c233714fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e051c3b2-7e07-5470-85d6-e4ed12752211" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e051c3b2-7e07-5470-85d6-e4ed12752211"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e051c3b2-7e07-5470-85d6-e4ed12752211" :answer/id #uuid "e051c3b2-7e07-5470-85d6-e4ed12752211" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-457b4315-a413-5f6e-81e5-9a7aa0226cf4" :question/id #uuid "457b4315-a413-5f6e-81e5-9a7aa0226cf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-343884" :question/problem "![](images/01/01dad591c5f02e0528e9092a058b44806cb0bab6f709347f8f90120c58ad25bc.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[-3,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,4]$ if $f(-3)=-2.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,2,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function  ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of two triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/eb/ebcaf43d56e8854ce17d89ce66a811ad1f505f7ebb855362798d845164a80377.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =-2 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+7.5 \\\\  & =5.5 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+(7.5-1) \\\\  & =4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-2,5.5,4.5\\}=5.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=5.5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-262dabb7-a19e-557c-b02a-f2846842eb57"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-262dabb7-a19e-557c-b02a-f2846842eb57" :answer-field/id #uuid "262dabb7-a19e-557c-b02a-f2846842eb57" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-759a68b1-2538-543b-9c09-1d4d9da7d95c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-759a68b1-2538-543b-9c09-1d4d9da7d95c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-759a68b1-2538-543b-9c09-1d4d9da7d95c" :answer/id #uuid "759a68b1-2538-543b-9c09-1d4d9da7d95c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a52ab505-f7f2-54ed-afb3-b43bed5cfef5" :question/id #uuid "a52ab505-f7f2-54ed-afb3-b43bed5cfef5" :question/math-academy-id "q-343959" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3cb97b20d164c2976ae046f83edbbb499514ff3c7ddfbfa9b703918457eba467.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,6]$ given that $f(-4)=7.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2$ and $x=3.$  Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-2,3,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of  $f(x)$  for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of $3$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/e6/e67e51128fe4392e814eec6d9d6ab17d7d7718af38fd556a0a47c603174e9dcd.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =7, \\\\ f(-2) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =7+2 \\\\  & =9, \\\\ f(3) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =9+15 \\\\  & =24, \\\\ f(6) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =24-4.5 \\\\  & =19.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{7,9,24,19.5\\}=24.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=24,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-28d8d771-54ee-598d-ba3a-511af4adfe5f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-28d8d771-54ee-598d-ba3a-511af4adfe5f" :answer-field/id #uuid "28d8d771-54ee-598d-ba3a-511af4adfe5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-149ea365-6c9e-5896-b572-37eac2a934cb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-149ea365-6c9e-5896-b572-37eac2a934cb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-149ea365-6c9e-5896-b572-37eac2a934cb" :answer/id #uuid "149ea365-6c9e-5896-b572-37eac2a934cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-907c4d01-952e-54f5-819a-03c35c5b1210" :question/id #uuid "907c4d01-952e-54f5-819a-03c35c5b1210" :question/math-academy-id "q-15744" :question/problem "![](images/39/392562d7675b8ebddd33cbdea448481654f9e643b088b949d4337618517c537b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find an $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,5]$ given that $f(0)=4.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1$ and $x=4.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,1,4,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of $3$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/82/82f6ace89a1d880390b5f6c2dbe344a6230d5bc91ba11521ac996b5e59ebc49c.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =4 \\\\ f(1) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}+1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{4,\\frac{11}{2},4,5\\}=\\frac{11}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{11}{2},$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eafdb283-9289-5258-96cf-a740ad61cf63"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eafdb283-9289-5258-96cf-a740ad61cf63" :answer-field/id #uuid "eafdb283-9289-5258-96cf-a740ad61cf63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-619f3b03-eef6-5b71-ac90-870978677b67" :answer-field/choices ["answer-619f3b03-eef6-5b71-ac90-870978677b67" "answer-c2f3b9f0-1089-5653-bb60-63efb1012505" "answer-11fc7f6f-2388-5e11-b63d-7af13c584f0e" "answer-3766ec0d-0113-5d0c-ad91-d63c83936a8c" "answer-04d05ee4-a3f9-5845-aabf-04165b4653e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-619f3b03-eef6-5b71-ac90-870978677b67" :answer/id #uuid "619f3b03-eef6-5b71-ac90-870978677b67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2f3b9f0-1089-5653-bb60-63efb1012505" :answer/id #uuid "c2f3b9f0-1089-5653-bb60-63efb1012505" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11fc7f6f-2388-5e11-b63d-7af13c584f0e" :answer/id #uuid "11fc7f6f-2388-5e11-b63d-7af13c584f0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3766ec0d-0113-5d0c-ad91-d63c83936a8c" :answer/id #uuid "3766ec0d-0113-5d0c-ad91-d63c83936a8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04d05ee4-a3f9-5845-aabf-04165b4653e6" :answer/id #uuid "04d05ee4-a3f9-5845-aabf-04165b4653e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-de089661-beea-5ee0-bfa5-18ccd2aea3c7" :question/id #uuid "de089661-beea-5ee0-bfa5-18ccd2aea3c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-316990" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d4077eba1972d32e7ab9b7bfbe550654395412076f175f48b1d78a49a21641ae.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[0,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,6]$ if $f(0)=5.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,3,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of two trapezoids. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/aa/aade60ca0b004cad7f0bb1e2de4e5ea354e1f87625b3d915e745381c489c41a4.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+2.5 \\\\  & =7.5 \\\\ f(6) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+2.5-4 \\\\  & =3.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{5,7.5,3.5\\}=7.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=7.5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-70d3a2c9-a5db-5dc5-82dc-e70759c371f4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-70d3a2c9-a5db-5dc5-82dc-e70759c371f4" :answer-field/id #uuid "70d3a2c9-a5db-5dc5-82dc-e70759c371f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2b167983-81a9-54ba-b03a-a6f83001fc2b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2b167983-81a9-54ba-b03a-a6f83001fc2b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b167983-81a9-54ba-b03a-a6f83001fc2b" :answer/id #uuid "2b167983-81a9-54ba-b03a-a6f83001fc2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186102025 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to use the graph of a function's derivative to maximize the function. Let's understand how this process works by studying a specific example.\n\nThe graph of $y = f'(x),$ the *derivative* of $f(x),$ is shown in the picture below. \n\n![](images/1e/1ef0f93de1825106880b3fdcd3c704c72e0748df277e5098064d6ee9abc9bdf8.png)\n\nThe areas of the shaded regions are indicated on the diagram. Additionally, it is known that $f(-2)=3.$ Our goal is find the *maximum* value of $f(x)$ on the interval $x\\in [-2,2].$\n\nTo find the maximum value of $f(x),$ we use the candidates test.\n\nAccording to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. Therefore, we proceed as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ so there are no non-stationary critical points. Additionally, we see that $f'(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are \n\n$$\nx=-2,\\quad x=-1,\\quad x=2.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n- The value of $f(x)$ at the left endpoint is given:\n\n$$\nf(-2) = 3\n$$\n\n- To compute $f(-1),$ we apply the fundamental theorem of calculus. Since the area of the left triangle in our picture equals ${\\color{red}{1}},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To compute $f(2),$ we apply the fundamental theorem of calculus once more. Using the fact that the area of the left triangle equals ${\\color{red}{1}}$ and the area of the right triangle equals ${\\color{blue}{\\dfrac32}},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3+1-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we *subtracted* the area of the right triangle since it lies *under* the $x$-axis.\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\left\\lbrace 3, 4, \\dfrac{5}{2} \\right\\rbrace = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{max}}=4,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{max}=-1.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101079 :activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105812 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8118b4f8-280b-5e14-bfe6-ca499d29c282" "question-f73e65ac-e638-502e-b806-c6b1908dab88"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-8118b4f8-280b-5e14-bfe6-ca499d29c282" :question/id #uuid "8118b4f8-280b-5e14-bfe6-ca499d29c282" :question/math-academy-id "q-68052" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4x-5},$ which of the following statements is correct?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}f(x)=+\\infty $\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{-}}f(x)=+\\infty $\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to (-5)}f(x)=-\\infty $" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4x-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{(x-1)(x+5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4x-5}$ has two vertical asymptotes $x=1$ and $x=-5.$\n\nTo compute the one-sided limits as $x\\to (-5),$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=-5$ from the right and from the left.\n\n| $x$ | $-5.1$ | $-5.01$ | $-5$ | $-4.99$ | $-4.9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-8.36$ | $-83.36$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $83.30$ | $8.30$ |\n\nAll values are negative and get smaller and smaller as $x\\to (-5)$ from the left and all values are positive and get larger and larger as $x\\to (-5)$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\\,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}f(x)=+\\infty .\n$$\n\nConsequently, statement I is true and II is false.\n\nFinally, since both left and right-sided limits at $x=-5$ are different, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-5)}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\n$$\n\nand therefore III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true. A plot of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/41/41983c0d07e355a9bd12cf92af158c95f390a5785f4bbc1d08562c240f190623.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-76e1baa0-7895-58ed-a291-bc1c098ba28f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-76e1baa0-7895-58ed-a291-bc1c098ba28f" :answer-field/id #uuid "76e1baa0-7895-58ed-a291-bc1c098ba28f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-afd46965-858b-55ad-b7e4-7b4b130bc8ca" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f24b746d-d027-5e07-8642-d8dffa243892" "answer-dae01dda-cbb0-5a2e-9a59-2709b27b4ecd" "answer-afd46965-858b-55ad-b7e4-7b4b130bc8ca" "answer-1e1c7a04-1844-590e-89eb-92daf805c409" "answer-29dafe97-f2a8-5f11-9a83-2e51d18852d2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f24b746d-d027-5e07-8642-d8dffa243892" :answer/id #uuid "f24b746d-d027-5e07-8642-d8dffa243892" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dae01dda-cbb0-5a2e-9a59-2709b27b4ecd" :answer/id #uuid "dae01dda-cbb0-5a2e-9a59-2709b27b4ecd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-afd46965-858b-55ad-b7e4-7b4b130bc8ca" :answer/id #uuid "afd46965-858b-55ad-b7e4-7b4b130bc8ca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e1c7a04-1844-590e-89eb-92daf805c409" :answer/id #uuid "1e1c7a04-1844-590e-89eb-92daf805c409" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29dafe97-f2a8-5f11-9a83-2e51d18852d2" :answer/id #uuid "29dafe97-f2a8-5f11-9a83-2e51d18852d2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105811 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5c8f7e06-57f9-5abb-8d02-311fe23df033" "question-e490eb4d-0678-501f-a484-46031f028ba4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-5c8f7e06-57f9-5abb-8d02-311fe23df033" :question/id #uuid "5c8f7e06-57f9-5abb-8d02-311fe23df033" :question/math-academy-id "q-78040" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{(x-1)(x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that the function has two vertical asymptotes  $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ \n\nSince $x=1$ is a vertical asymptote, the limit as $x\\to {1}^{-}$ will be either $+\\infty $ or $-\\infty .$\n\nTo find the limit as $x\\to {1}^{-},$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=1$ from the left:\n\n| $x$ | $0.9$ | $0.99$ | $0.999$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $≈-4.74$ | $≈-49.75$ | $≈-499.75$ |\n\nAll values are negative and get smaller and smaller. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f1/f1dbd1e97af1e0fc6a7776fba28f6c4bcb081fa3213291f66aeefc8b3219331e.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a71ab316-18be-5962-b3fa-f8deb2262af0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a71ab316-18be-5962-b3fa-f8deb2262af0" :answer-field/id #uuid "a71ab316-18be-5962-b3fa-f8deb2262af0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d58b2e03-f857-5968-865a-bc3121726950" :answer-field/choices ["answer-56c43f1a-e811-5df5-80d4-0515dde8f4ef" "answer-694b9ed0-79d0-5201-a364-4e466472a49b" "answer-d58b2e03-f857-5968-865a-bc3121726950" "answer-1c755ef0-18b5-547d-8180-967d0f618313" "answer-1d4638dc-f551-5ea6-ad1e-38bbce06b66f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56c43f1a-e811-5df5-80d4-0515dde8f4ef" :answer/id #uuid "56c43f1a-e811-5df5-80d4-0515dde8f4ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "+\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-694b9ed0-79d0-5201-a364-4e466472a49b" :answer/id #uuid "694b9ed0-79d0-5201-a364-4e466472a49b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d58b2e03-f857-5968-865a-bc3121726950" :answer/id #uuid "d58b2e03-f857-5968-865a-bc3121726950" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c755ef0-18b5-547d-8180-967d0f618313" :answer/id #uuid "1c755ef0-18b5-547d-8180-967d0f618313" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d4638dc-f551-5ea6-ad1e-38bbce06b66f" :answer/id #uuid "1d4638dc-f551-5ea6-ad1e-38bbce06b66f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e490eb4d-0678-501f-a484-46031f028ba4" :question/id #uuid "e490eb4d-0678-501f-a484-46031f028ba4" :question/math-academy-id "q-78042" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+4x+3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+4x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+3x+x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{x(x+3)+1(x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{(x+1)(x+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that the function has two vertical asymptotes  $x=-1$ and $x=-3.$ \n\nSince $x=-1$ is a vertical asymptote, the limit as $x\\to (-1)^{-}$ will be either $+\\infty $ or $-\\infty .$\n\nTo find the limit as $x\\to (-1)^{-},$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=-1$ from the left:\n\n| $x$ | $-1.1$ | $-1.01$ | $-1.001$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $≈-23.21$ | $≈-203.02$ | $≈-2\\,003.00$ |\n\nAll values are negative and get smaller and smaller. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/29/2967a50cc89632da9292fc9192232f757ff3f3bec9304042ef6ec074882565b5.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-921f7efe-43d0-57f9-9875-d7de999ae3c7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-921f7efe-43d0-57f9-9875-d7de999ae3c7" :answer-field/id #uuid "921f7efe-43d0-57f9-9875-d7de999ae3c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-99621082-2878-5089-ab1f-aeea1c97f48f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dde611b0-cfe1-5a6c-90c9-dcd8bddf724c" "answer-99621082-2878-5089-ab1f-aeea1c97f48f" "answer-d69edd01-9cb8-5912-94ab-f492c7bb371a" "answer-9836bf91-530a-5113-aa0b-4b54008e48b9" "answer-ff773bb6-564d-50b3-9960-3f4c2adcc7cb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dde611b0-cfe1-5a6c-90c9-dcd8bddf724c" :answer/id #uuid "dde611b0-cfe1-5a6c-90c9-dcd8bddf724c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "+\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99621082-2878-5089-ab1f-aeea1c97f48f" :answer/id #uuid "99621082-2878-5089-ab1f-aeea1c97f48f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d69edd01-9cb8-5912-94ab-f492c7bb371a" :answer/id #uuid "d69edd01-9cb8-5912-94ab-f492c7bb371a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9836bf91-530a-5113-aa0b-4b54008e48b9" :answer/id #uuid "9836bf91-530a-5113-aa0b-4b54008e48b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff773bb6-564d-50b3-9960-3f4c2adcc7cb" :answer/id #uuid "ff773bb6-564d-50b3-9960-3f4c2adcc7cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f73e65ac-e638-502e-b806-c6b1908dab88" :question/id #uuid "f73e65ac-e638-502e-b806-c6b1908dab88" :question/math-academy-id "q-107245" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{6+x-{x}^{2}},$ which of the following statements is correct?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x}{6+x-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{(x-3)(x+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{6+x-{x}^{2}}$ has two vertical asymptotes $x=-2$ and $x=3.$\n\nTo compute the one-sided limits as $x\\to (-2),$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=-2$ from the right and from the left.\n\n| $x$ | $-2.01$ | $-2.001$ | $-2$ | $-1.999$ | $-1.99$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $≈40.12$ | $≈400.12$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $≈-399.88$ | $≈-39.88$ |\n\nAll values are positive and get larger and larger as $x\\to (-2)$ from the left and all values are negative and get smaller and smaller as $x\\to (-2)$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=+\\infty ,\\,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nConsequently, statement I is false and II is true.\n\nFinally, since both left and right-sided limits at $x=-2$ are different, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\n$$\n\nand therefore III is also true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true. A plot of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/25/25e672e71c4268193c128687ba35eeca1e4bce8f0c355922a3b5df022740159c.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-63236c73-41ba-5765-87b1-d084c15dd995"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-63236c73-41ba-5765-87b1-d084c15dd995" :answer-field/id #uuid "63236c73-41ba-5765-87b1-d084c15dd995" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3e4c8745-9f58-5a6c-9cbc-4855147f632e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-be26a0d0-9356-5266-83e1-5aa7d696bdc9" "answer-8024b692-ee13-569d-81cc-14158672262e" "answer-709ab39f-331d-503b-aa95-5e11144f249b" "answer-3e4c8745-9f58-5a6c-9cbc-4855147f632e" "answer-e3b98644-4bed-5e98-8e64-5be6f13b0782"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be26a0d0-9356-5266-83e1-5aa7d696bdc9" :answer/id #uuid "be26a0d0-9356-5266-83e1-5aa7d696bdc9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8024b692-ee13-569d-81cc-14158672262e" :answer/id #uuid "8024b692-ee13-569d-81cc-14158672262e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-709ab39f-331d-503b-aa95-5e11144f249b" :answer/id #uuid "709ab39f-331d-503b-aa95-5e11144f249b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e4c8745-9f58-5a6c-9cbc-4855147f632e" :answer/id #uuid "3e4c8745-9f58-5a6c-9cbc-4855147f632e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3b98644-4bed-5e98-8e64-5be6f13b0782" :answer/id #uuid "e3b98644-4bed-5e98-8e64-5be6f13b0782" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105813 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0bc0c5f8-ceb7-5dd9-9846-93db9391162e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0bc0c5f8-ceb7-5dd9-9846-93db9391162e" :question/id #uuid "0bc0c5f8-ceb7-5dd9-9846-93db9391162e" :question/math-academy-id "q-107281" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4},$ which of the following statements is correct?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to (-4)^{-}}f(x)=\\infty $\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty $" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x+4)}{(x+4)(x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is  $x\\ne -4,1,$ and we notice that the function can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\frac{x}{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the function has the only vertical asymptote $x=1.$ So statement I is false.\n\nTo find  ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x),$ and  ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x),$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=1$ from the left and right.\n\n| $x$ | $0.99$ | $0.999$ | $1$ | $1.001$ | $1.01$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-99$ | $-999$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $1\\,001$ | $101$ |\n\nAll values are negative and get smaller and smaller as $x\\to 1$ from the left and all values are positive and get larger and larger as $x\\to 1$ from the right. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty \n$$\n\nConsequently, statement II is false and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is correct. A plot of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/3d/3de7d0a26f6f171fc83bd885eed9ff9f50bddfc76b2f58e9477241fb874c9b7d.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-04ceef66-3510-5051-9c39-4e1661e3d26d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-04ceef66-3510-5051-9c39-4e1661e3d26d" :answer-field/id #uuid "04ceef66-3510-5051-9c39-4e1661e3d26d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3748cfea-f6c8-51ad-a6c6-518385d62fec" :answer-field/choices ["answer-33eae5f0-498f-582b-b676-c5040fa8abdf" "answer-c0e1e2ae-c02d-5af9-b266-de30c5fe9973" "answer-3521dd0a-297d-5d89-a8e1-a83dd7dd1b81" "answer-b5afdbe2-4f04-54a7-aa79-406d39105261" "answer-3748cfea-f6c8-51ad-a6c6-518385d62fec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33eae5f0-498f-582b-b676-c5040fa8abdf" :answer/id #uuid "33eae5f0-498f-582b-b676-c5040fa8abdf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0e1e2ae-c02d-5af9-b266-de30c5fe9973" :answer/id #uuid "c0e1e2ae-c02d-5af9-b266-de30c5fe9973" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3521dd0a-297d-5d89-a8e1-a83dd7dd1b81" :answer/id #uuid "3521dd0a-297d-5d89-a8e1-a83dd7dd1b81" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5afdbe2-4f04-54a7-aa79-406d39105261" :answer/id #uuid "b5afdbe2-4f04-54a7-aa79-406d39105261" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3748cfea-f6c8-51ad-a6c6-518385d62fec" :answer/id #uuid "3748cfea-f6c8-51ad-a6c6-518385d62fec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-4239e5b8-df98-589b-8983-f1548e3897b5" :activity/id #uuid "4239e5b8-df98-589b-8983-f1548e3897b5" :activity/title "Connecting Infinite Limits and Vertical Asymptotes of Rational Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050785 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-98c1e306-fef3-5b41-a17c-6c928bad83d9" "step-1e9074cc-1f21-51dd-bdc9-fddf1e3aae42" "step-d4ef3044-b4ec-5d12-92c9-015fe1643574" "step-4d566925-ee36-5b29-a942-cfc26e5d09b1" "step-3cb0816b-f02f-5a60-92f2-a2769836ba22"] :activity/first-step "step-98c1e306-fef3-5b41-a17c-6c928bad83d9" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-98c1e306-fef3-5b41-a17c-6c928bad83d9" :step/id #uuid "98c1e306-fef3-5b41-a17c-6c928bad83d9" :step/content "question-8118b4f8-280b-5e14-bfe6-ca499d29c282" :step/next "step-1e9074cc-1f21-51dd-bdc9-fddf1e3aae42" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1e9074cc-1f21-51dd-bdc9-fddf1e3aae42" :step/id #uuid "1e9074cc-1f21-51dd-bdc9-fddf1e3aae42" :step/content "question-5c8f7e06-57f9-5abb-8d02-311fe23df033" :step/next "step-d4ef3044-b4ec-5d12-92c9-015fe1643574" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d4ef3044-b4ec-5d12-92c9-015fe1643574" :step/id #uuid "d4ef3044-b4ec-5d12-92c9-015fe1643574" :step/content "question-e490eb4d-0678-501f-a484-46031f028ba4" :step/next "step-4d566925-ee36-5b29-a942-cfc26e5d09b1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4d566925-ee36-5b29-a942-cfc26e5d09b1" :step/id #uuid "4d566925-ee36-5b29-a942-cfc26e5d09b1" :step/content "question-f73e65ac-e638-502e-b806-c6b1908dab88" :step/next "step-3cb0816b-f02f-5a60-92f2-a2769836ba22" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3cb0816b-f02f-5a60-92f2-a2769836ba22" :step/id #uuid "3cb0816b-f02f-5a60-92f2-a2769836ba22" :step/content "question-0bc0c5f8-ceb7-5dd9-9846-93db9391162e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172089 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4cb23656-32ad-5111-abf0-812956e034d5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172090 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b688c283-ffe1-5279-a25b-e224f4238243" "question-acde0039-9e1f-5668-bb5b-a5df57c98611"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b688c283-ffe1-5279-a25b-e224f4238243" :question/id #uuid "b688c283-ffe1-5279-a25b-e224f4238243" :question/math-academy-id "q-123634" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta }\\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{e}^{2\\theta }+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2{e}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\theta }{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2({e}^{\\pi /2}-{e}^{\\pi /4}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi /4}({e}^{\\pi /4}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c2bc4d54-53d7-5960-8596-76e1189178c5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c2bc4d54-53d7-5960-8596-76e1189178c5" :answer-field/id #uuid "c2bc4d54-53d7-5960-8596-76e1189178c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-51635b1e-3cd9-5e5e-8da8-a31c41a6b5bd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-01eaff75-9bd9-584b-8dac-798d298e7fe1" "answer-51635b1e-3cd9-5e5e-8da8-a31c41a6b5bd" "answer-0d033828-6617-5a30-b0e6-4a3c14d14576" "answer-e233503b-2829-57e2-afff-b160c60d6d3f" "answer-19428a95-6f1a-515e-aa64-ad5b042f4aa6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01eaff75-9bd9-584b-8dac-798d298e7fe1" :answer/id #uuid "01eaff75-9bd9-584b-8dac-798d298e7fe1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2({e}^{\\pi /4}-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51635b1e-3cd9-5e5e-8da8-a31c41a6b5bd" :answer/id #uuid "51635b1e-3cd9-5e5e-8da8-a31c41a6b5bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi /4}({e}^{\\pi /4}-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d033828-6617-5a30-b0e6-4a3c14d14576" :answer/id #uuid "0d033828-6617-5a30-b0e6-4a3c14d14576" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /2}-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e233503b-2829-57e2-afff-b160c60d6d3f" :answer/id #uuid "e233503b-2829-57e2-afff-b160c60d6d3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /4}-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19428a95-6f1a-515e-aa64-ad5b042f4aa6" :answer/id #uuid "19428a95-6f1a-515e-aa64-ad5b042f4aa6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\pi /4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-acde0039-9e1f-5668-bb5b-a5df57c98611" :question/id #uuid "acde0039-9e1f-5668-bb5b-a5df57c98611" :question/math-academy-id "q-123643" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta })^{2}+{(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )^{2}+{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,\\theta {|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-99e9e8ce-81b5-5865-823b-16944100d9f1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-99e9e8ce-81b5-5865-823b-16944100d9f1" :answer-field/id #uuid "99e9e8ce-81b5-5865-823b-16944100d9f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-051f2eaa-30f6-5a12-9a77-9f95ac0b3e71" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bda1540c-a5f2-5558-ad30-970a0713c6a4" "answer-5896cb3f-bb8d-57b4-adca-c7ec3f6be4d2" "answer-dba7e57b-8972-5648-baf2-34e857e37757" "answer-051f2eaa-30f6-5a12-9a77-9f95ac0b3e71" "answer-3257bf5c-fa92-5d61-948f-9e8f3c674e46"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bda1540c-a5f2-5558-ad30-970a0713c6a4" :answer/id #uuid "bda1540c-a5f2-5558-ad30-970a0713c6a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5896cb3f-bb8d-57b4-adca-c7ec3f6be4d2" :answer/id #uuid "5896cb3f-bb8d-57b4-adca-c7ec3f6be4d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dba7e57b-8972-5648-baf2-34e857e37757" :answer/id #uuid "dba7e57b-8972-5648-baf2-34e857e37757" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-051f2eaa-30f6-5a12-9a77-9f95ac0b3e71" :answer/id #uuid "051f2eaa-30f6-5a12-9a77-9f95ac0b3e71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3257bf5c-fa92-5d61-948f-9e8f3c674e46" :answer/id #uuid "3257bf5c-fa92-5d61-948f-9e8f3c674e46" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4cb23656-32ad-5111-abf0-812956e034d5" :question/id #uuid "4cb23656-32ad-5111-abf0-812956e034d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-149800" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\frac{1}{\\theta }$ for $1\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\,?$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\frac{1}{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{\\theta })}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}+1}{{\\theta }^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+1}}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2ddef5b4-7fd6-526e-90d2-54235e050d54"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2ddef5b4-7fd6-526e-90d2-54235e050d54" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ddef5b4-7fd6-526e-90d2-54235e050d54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8b872d30-3b7e-5aae-91f8-0a201257e3ac" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f3baf0b1-8376-594a-b0c2-adace7164539" "answer-c81c2c1a-18d2-5fae-8d05-046e1ef9709a" "answer-5df30dae-9f1b-5169-85f4-352ef7b35830" "answer-8b872d30-3b7e-5aae-91f8-0a201257e3ac" "answer-2d3de7cb-dc94-55e8-a78d-a27f24ecf883"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3baf0b1-8376-594a-b0c2-adace7164539" :answer/id #uuid "f3baf0b1-8376-594a-b0c2-adace7164539" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{2\\sqrt{\\theta }}{{\\theta }^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c81c2c1a-18d2-5fae-8d05-046e1ef9709a" :answer/id #uuid "c81c2c1a-18d2-5fae-8d05-046e1ef9709a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{\\theta }}{\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5df30dae-9f1b-5169-85f4-352ef7b35830" :answer/id #uuid "5df30dae-9f1b-5169-85f4-352ef7b35830" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+1}}{2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b872d30-3b7e-5aae-91f8-0a201257e3ac" :answer/id #uuid "8b872d30-3b7e-5aae-91f8-0a201257e3ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+1}}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d3de7cb-dc94-55e8-a78d-a27f24ecf883" :answer/id #uuid "2d3de7cb-dc94-55e8-a78d-a27f24ecf883" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{4}+1}}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-2e6736bd-345f-5eb1-b1d1-69bf9d392abd" :activity/id #uuid "2e6736bd-345f-5eb1-b1d1-69bf9d392abd" :activity/title "Further Properties of Line Integrals of Scalar Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049041 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-9df4f53a-cfc3-5f5d-9dd3-d0e7ba431674" "step-19f4932e-c75d-5c67-b1d4-b8ff2197751a" "step-ca26c44a-47b3-5082-aab1-eac96843df54" "step-e9a0c7e4-f9f1-5b84-a2be-688ec5da197a" "step-b40a5a22-c678-5bc6-8e83-8d3a7d00ab97"] :activity/first-step "step-9df4f53a-cfc3-5f5d-9dd3-d0e7ba431674" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-9df4f53a-cfc3-5f5d-9dd3-d0e7ba431674" :step/id #uuid "9df4f53a-cfc3-5f5d-9dd3-d0e7ba431674" :step/content 17592186259519 :step/next "step-19f4932e-c75d-5c67-b1d4-b8ff2197751a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-19f4932e-c75d-5c67-b1d4-b8ff2197751a" :step/id #uuid "19f4932e-c75d-5c67-b1d4-b8ff2197751a" :step/content 17592186259520 :step/next "step-ca26c44a-47b3-5082-aab1-eac96843df54" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ca26c44a-47b3-5082-aab1-eac96843df54" :step/id #uuid "ca26c44a-47b3-5082-aab1-eac96843df54" :step/content "question-b688c283-ffe1-5279-a25b-e224f4238243" :step/next "step-e9a0c7e4-f9f1-5b84-a2be-688ec5da197a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e9a0c7e4-f9f1-5b84-a2be-688ec5da197a" :step/id #uuid "e9a0c7e4-f9f1-5b84-a2be-688ec5da197a" :step/content "question-acde0039-9e1f-5668-bb5b-a5df57c98611" :step/next "step-b40a5a22-c678-5bc6-8e83-8d3a7d00ab97" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b40a5a22-c678-5bc6-8e83-8d3a7d00ab97" :step/id #uuid "b40a5a22-c678-5bc6-8e83-8d3a7d00ab97" :step/content "question-4cb23656-32ad-5111-abf0-812956e034d5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186132301 :answer-field/choices 17592186132304] [:db/retract 17592186132301 :answer-field/choices 17592186132305] [:db/retract 17592186132301 :answer-field/choices 17592186132306] [:db/retract 17592186132301 :answer-field/choices 17592186132302] [:db/retract 17592186132301 :answer-field/choices 17592186132303] {:db/id 17592186133896 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fdacb1ae-ddf6-5106-9485-7c1e14bd18a9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134377 :question/problem "![](images/44/4408d1fcee1d168c6ab21ea53cf24845093bad9cc608299a36f0510c1636cc03.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t},\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186132301 :answer-field/correct "answer-e5105f35-40d7-5290-bc6b-8489218cf6ab" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2e996338-8bd4-5f85-acba-798d27184bb8" "answer-1997d82a-e8fd-5876-bff6-f2a494d2c16e" "answer-e5105f35-40d7-5290-bc6b-8489218cf6ab" "answer-91c650e3-c5a3-551b-acac-5cc206d90d87" "answer-a50bd914-811d-5009-9a3f-16c3d9e26a55"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e996338-8bd4-5f85-acba-798d27184bb8" :answer/id #uuid "2e996338-8bd4-5f85-acba-798d27184bb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1997d82a-e8fd-5876-bff6-f2a494d2c16e" :answer/id #uuid "1997d82a-e8fd-5876-bff6-f2a494d2c16e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5105f35-40d7-5290-bc6b-8489218cf6ab" :answer/id #uuid "e5105f35-40d7-5290-bc6b-8489218cf6ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91c650e3-c5a3-551b-acac-5cc206d90d87" :answer/id #uuid "91c650e3-c5a3-551b-acac-5cc206d90d87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a50bd914-811d-5009-9a3f-16c3d9e26a55" :answer/id #uuid "a50bd914-811d-5009-9a3f-16c3d9e26a55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133897 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-053eddb0-5c93-560e-b92c-a89951cbd49c" "question-ad2198cd-84ab-52d9-a653-128de0924655"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-053eddb0-5c93-560e-b92c-a89951cbd49c" :question/id #uuid "053eddb0-5c93-560e-b92c-a89951cbd49c" :question/math-academy-id "q-42239" :question/problem "![](images/93/938f91220033a2174c011c1f8d65ff61e81e79b8af0ef718411169364c885418.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j$ for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=t\\,i+\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ \n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and   \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}x\\,dy \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{t}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0]-[-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cdfd2c93-cc42-5e18-b039-6099f904aa29"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cdfd2c93-cc42-5e18-b039-6099f904aa29" :answer-field/id #uuid "cdfd2c93-cc42-5e18-b039-6099f904aa29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bbaa8abc-d95e-51ca-a4d1-7a1aa516b455" :answer-field/choices ["answer-94960e82-187d-589c-a1c2-ed096c94c497" "answer-6fe86c01-fa33-54c0-919e-aaad045de4dc" "answer-f46a71c6-0e54-5d62-9732-93eefd0150bf" "answer-bbaa8abc-d95e-51ca-a4d1-7a1aa516b455" "answer-91cc57eb-09e7-59eb-aa0f-280a8c492891"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94960e82-187d-589c-a1c2-ed096c94c497" :answer/id #uuid "94960e82-187d-589c-a1c2-ed096c94c497" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6fe86c01-fa33-54c0-919e-aaad045de4dc" :answer/id #uuid "6fe86c01-fa33-54c0-919e-aaad045de4dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f46a71c6-0e54-5d62-9732-93eefd0150bf" :answer/id #uuid "f46a71c6-0e54-5d62-9732-93eefd0150bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{2}-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bbaa8abc-d95e-51ca-a4d1-7a1aa516b455" :answer/id #uuid "bbaa8abc-d95e-51ca-a4d1-7a1aa516b455" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91cc57eb-09e7-59eb-aa0f-280a8c492891" :answer/id #uuid "91cc57eb-09e7-59eb-aa0f-280a8c492891" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fdacb1ae-ddf6-5106-9485-7c1e14bd18a9" :question/id #uuid "fdacb1ae-ddf6-5106-9485-7c1e14bd18a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-42297" :question/problem "![](images/35/35b8ad6fd40daf95ba4f77288321d6a570e2df2944d056b9445e9ba4e36f97b7.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a2eb735b-b5bd-5b47-b78f-f9e6cc0794cf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a2eb735b-b5bd-5b47-b78f-f9e6cc0794cf" :answer-field/id #uuid "a2eb735b-b5bd-5b47-b78f-f9e6cc0794cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-65573630-2003-5f37-a3a0-d25c98f4401a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a5484851-2c51-5273-8db2-309d04de9b82" "answer-d89e0626-3b35-5c77-81ab-f0b1350e2a80" "answer-6e780ffa-2a00-5af3-b5f3-92f795812999" "answer-65573630-2003-5f37-a3a0-d25c98f4401a" "answer-9dbf5317-be7f-5ed0-a334-2b540864d0af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5484851-2c51-5273-8db2-309d04de9b82" :answer/id #uuid "a5484851-2c51-5273-8db2-309d04de9b82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d89e0626-3b35-5c77-81ab-f0b1350e2a80" :answer/id #uuid "d89e0626-3b35-5c77-81ab-f0b1350e2a80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e780ffa-2a00-5af3-b5f3-92f795812999" :answer/id #uuid "6e780ffa-2a00-5af3-b5f3-92f795812999" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65573630-2003-5f37-a3a0-d25c98f4401a" :answer/id #uuid "65573630-2003-5f37-a3a0-d25c98f4401a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9dbf5317-be7f-5ed0-a334-2b540864d0af" :answer/id #uuid "9dbf5317-be7f-5ed0-a334-2b540864d0af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133895 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6ae04e3b-017d-534f-b76c-ac600584e435"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-6ae04e3b-017d-534f-b76c-ac600584e435" :question/id #uuid "6ae04e3b-017d-534f-b76c-ac600584e435" :question/math-academy-id "q-126568" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\nI. $P(x,y)=-y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\n\nII. $P(x,y)=x$ and $Q(x,y)=y$\n\nIII. $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=6{y}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1557ad1a-a45b-592a-8609-24271802b200"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1557ad1a-a45b-592a-8609-24271802b200" :answer-field/id #uuid "1557ad1a-a45b-592a-8609-24271802b200" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f2d4bd2c-dd7d-5ebf-8ab9-0d013cef2b39" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f2d4bd2c-dd7d-5ebf-8ab9-0d013cef2b39" "answer-1e791730-85c4-5ff7-ad54-6e399657153c" "answer-083e8245-b859-5f0f-b493-6e1eb22d754b" "answer-3c70ca77-ec27-57f8-b078-51030fa03414" "answer-02757ddb-6720-5404-868b-7b1166fb2c51"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2d4bd2c-dd7d-5ebf-8ab9-0d013cef2b39" :answer/id #uuid "f2d4bd2c-dd7d-5ebf-8ab9-0d013cef2b39" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e791730-85c4-5ff7-ad54-6e399657153c" :answer/id #uuid "1e791730-85c4-5ff7-ad54-6e399657153c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-083e8245-b859-5f0f-b493-6e1eb22d754b" :answer/id #uuid "083e8245-b859-5f0f-b493-6e1eb22d754b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c70ca77-ec27-57f8-b078-51030fa03414" :answer/id #uuid "3c70ca77-ec27-57f8-b078-51030fa03414" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02757ddb-6720-5404-868b-7b1166fb2c51" :answer/id #uuid "02757ddb-6720-5404-868b-7b1166fb2c51" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ad2198cd-84ab-52d9-a653-128de0924655" :question/id #uuid "ad2198cd-84ab-52d9-a653-128de0924655" :question/math-academy-id "q-126754" :question/problem "![](images/86/86772e27f0a3226a608a1188fd8f58b9a8956f5f02fdbc5f9c2d29e2cef2a54c.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $r(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j$ \nfor $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,dx & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\cdot (-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡2t(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-18{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =6({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0b42e264-51ff-5675-8ac9-a399601959a2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0b42e264-51ff-5675-8ac9-a399601959a2" :answer-field/id #uuid "0b42e264-51ff-5675-8ac9-a399601959a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bb43d5ac-3266-5668-89fa-191462493dae" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bb43d5ac-3266-5668-89fa-191462493dae" "answer-e19c04bb-3f86-514f-ad0d-22da5d1ba873" "answer-a3031b19-d41c-5adf-a1c1-fb61f1fe36c5" "answer-3242ed2a-ee0f-5b6e-92a0-da63b077c141" "answer-482ab86f-4fe2-571e-b0ca-93e2bd5d4903"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb43d5ac-3266-5668-89fa-191462493dae" :answer/id #uuid "bb43d5ac-3266-5668-89fa-191462493dae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e19c04bb-3f86-514f-ad0d-22da5d1ba873" :answer/id #uuid "e19c04bb-3f86-514f-ad0d-22da5d1ba873" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "17\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3031b19-d41c-5adf-a1c1-fb61f1fe36c5" :answer/id #uuid "a3031b19-d41c-5adf-a1c1-fb61f1fe36c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{28}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3242ed2a-ee0f-5b6e-92a0-da63b077c141" :answer/id #uuid "3242ed2a-ee0f-5b6e-92a0-da63b077c141" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-482ab86f-4fe2-571e-b0ca-93e2bd5d4903" :answer/id #uuid "482ab86f-4fe2-571e-b0ca-93e2bd5d4903" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{35}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-abf9c4c3-4981-52ee-b589-3b1c3030a4cf" :activity/id #uuid "abf9c4c3-4981-52ee-b589-3b1c3030a4cf" :activity/title "Using Green's Theorem to Calculate Area" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050567 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-80c380fc-d04f-536e-bc60-f772fa1cc0f7" "step-0fd57101-031a-56f6-9993-cefb5de3fe06" "step-a5cc6a28-05b8-5f80-aaa2-4f93656a403b" "step-eae9ae58-73f8-56ab-9f1a-1264510bfad1" "step-a3e46484-6e96-5940-b74e-e01b342a1b8e"] :activity/first-step "step-80c380fc-d04f-536e-bc60-f772fa1cc0f7" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-80c380fc-d04f-536e-bc60-f772fa1cc0f7" :step/id #uuid "80c380fc-d04f-536e-bc60-f772fa1cc0f7" :step/content 17592186134377 :step/next "step-0fd57101-031a-56f6-9993-cefb5de3fe06" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0fd57101-031a-56f6-9993-cefb5de3fe06" :step/id #uuid "0fd57101-031a-56f6-9993-cefb5de3fe06" :step/content "question-053eddb0-5c93-560e-b92c-a89951cbd49c" :step/next "step-a5cc6a28-05b8-5f80-aaa2-4f93656a403b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a5cc6a28-05b8-5f80-aaa2-4f93656a403b" :step/id #uuid "a5cc6a28-05b8-5f80-aaa2-4f93656a403b" :step/content "question-fdacb1ae-ddf6-5106-9485-7c1e14bd18a9" :step/next "step-eae9ae58-73f8-56ab-9f1a-1264510bfad1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-eae9ae58-73f8-56ab-9f1a-1264510bfad1" :step/id #uuid "eae9ae58-73f8-56ab-9f1a-1264510bfad1" :step/content "question-6ae04e3b-017d-534f-b76c-ac600584e435" :step/next "step-a3e46484-6e96-5940-b74e-e01b342a1b8e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a3e46484-6e96-5940-b74e-e01b342a1b8e" :step/id #uuid "a3e46484-6e96-5940-b74e-e01b342a1b8e" :step/content "question-ad2198cd-84ab-52d9-a653-128de0924655" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162041 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-79df1f11-1c43-51b8-8e99-4f8efb003972"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-79df1f11-1c43-51b8-8e99-4f8efb003972" :question/id #uuid "79df1f11-1c43-51b8-8e99-4f8efb003972" :question/math-academy-id "q-140961" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrix $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-6I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-1I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $V:$\n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c4360e51-0f64-590c-bba8-917045fffada"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c4360e51-0f64-590c-bba8-917045fffada" :answer-field/id #uuid "c4360e51-0f64-590c-bba8-917045fffada" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c0ca0393-d174-5ef6-b704-b99b7d145991" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2ea8b7cd-7674-5bdd-be23-c96008f18ef2" "answer-a39bb8ab-4347-5457-ac3e-775c486b4c6f" "answer-1a42c0f3-23fe-54b3-9812-4384378e4fdf" "answer-c0ca0393-d174-5ef6-b704-b99b7d145991" "answer-f50be767-9717-528b-bc60-edef8d33bf76"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ea8b7cd-7674-5bdd-be23-c96008f18ef2" :answer/id #uuid "2ea8b7cd-7674-5bdd-be23-c96008f18ef2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a39bb8ab-4347-5457-ac3e-775c486b4c6f" :answer/id #uuid "a39bb8ab-4347-5457-ac3e-775c486b4c6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a42c0f3-23fe-54b3-9812-4384378e4fdf" :answer/id #uuid "1a42c0f3-23fe-54b3-9812-4384378e4fdf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0ca0393-d174-5ef6-b704-b99b7d145991" :answer/id #uuid "c0ca0393-d174-5ef6-b704-b99b7d145991" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f50be767-9717-528b-bc60-edef8d33bf76" :answer/id #uuid "f50be767-9717-528b-bc60-edef8d33bf76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-bde3e653-9f22-5e55-948f-85ddf2e516b7" :activity/id #uuid "bde3e653-9f22-5e55-948f-85ddf2e516b7" :activity/title "Singular Value Decomposition of Larger Matrices" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048143 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-3be28e76-1c88-5275-b4a3-f54e97e30cfd" "step-ada82642-7b26-53dc-9e04-7877d6ceb00a" "step-392eb1aa-9b69-5309-8d5f-16ee0b5603a3" "step-2ae9fbe8-190e-52f6-9c10-192410ac4065"] :activity/first-step "step-3be28e76-1c88-5275-b4a3-f54e97e30cfd" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-3be28e76-1c88-5275-b4a3-f54e97e30cfd" :step/id #uuid "3be28e76-1c88-5275-b4a3-f54e97e30cfd" :step/content 17592186443406 :step/next "step-ada82642-7b26-53dc-9e04-7877d6ceb00a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ada82642-7b26-53dc-9e04-7877d6ceb00a" :step/id #uuid "ada82642-7b26-53dc-9e04-7877d6ceb00a" :step/content 17592186371436 :step/next "step-392eb1aa-9b69-5309-8d5f-16ee0b5603a3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-392eb1aa-9b69-5309-8d5f-16ee0b5603a3" :step/id #uuid "392eb1aa-9b69-5309-8d5f-16ee0b5603a3" :step/content "question-79df1f11-1c43-51b8-8e99-4f8efb003972" :step/next "step-2ae9fbe8-190e-52f6-9c10-192410ac4065" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2ae9fbe8-190e-52f6-9c10-192410ac4065" :step/id #uuid "2ae9fbe8-190e-52f6-9c10-192410ac4065" :step/content 17592186370654 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100962 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d62efc40-a923-5983-a507-878080289da7" "question-9b464b81-5baf-5338-aa06-b3e5f256249d" "question-ce30ca10-ec44-5f96-b53c-efeee52b65e4" "question-1b07da73-507b-5a7d-831e-9986becac517" "question-e788b4b9-a09c-5261-9e6a-52c7021cb74e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100582 :question/problem "![](images/b2/b2f034132460f2a6b3a26eb52bd4129fb13ea5a871d766815b83047217b97b3b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,5].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,5]$ given that $f(-2)=4.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-2,5),$ and $f'(x) \\neq 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =4 \\\\ f(5) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-8 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\{4, -4\\} = -4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{min}} = -4,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is $x_{\\text{min}} = 5.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100862 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e74c85cc-5f64-54fa-a3ca-fa87e47bcbd2" "question-6cbda409-a223-57c5-93ac-3f78fe61c842" "question-4760d6e4-5155-5d78-b6d2-f55eb97036fb" "question-20a7e745-4621-5ebb-935e-e596b9f08fba"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100702 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[1,9].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown below, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $3,$ $5,$ and $4$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,9]$ given that $f(1)=5.$\n\n![](images/a8/a86baf9a7cddb9ea44de15d5c87ba01a1b8d68eb233950fb5f22f73ae4dd661b.png)" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,9]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (1,9),$ and $f'(x) = 0$ at $x=3$ and $x=6.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,3,6,9.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+3 \\\\  & =8 \\\\ f(6) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+3-5 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ f(9) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{9}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+3-5+4 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have \n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\{5, 8, 3, 7\\} = 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{min}} = 3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is $x_{\\text{min}} = 6.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100863 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d9b31f47-0173-5340-9051-1c0bbc0f3595" "question-8f946771-a506-530c-b84d-03471b2aa334" "question-d754760c-74db-51d7-aeca-199ef191761d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100703 :question/problem "![](images/0c/0cac30d21bdd2f2cc3aeaa0ed907d6eeb9ff78b3dae92cad40b56fab76bd7237.png)\n\nThe function $f$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,2].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the minimum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,2]$ given that $f(-4)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-4,2),$ and $f'(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x = -4, 0 , 2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nThe function is made up of two trapezoids and one triangle. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/a6/a676f3c026bdae86c6c353c3c5a38f6922dfe5b7dc83d955c1ee2d392d9f2021.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =0 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =0-7 \\\\  & =-7 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =0-7+1+1.5 \\\\  & =-4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\{ 0, -7, -4.5 \\} = -7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{min}} = -7,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{min} = 0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-d62efc40-a923-5983-a507-878080289da7" :question/id #uuid "d62efc40-a923-5983-a507-878080289da7" :question/math-academy-id "q-342845" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9a2a079f471072c80becb1188a2862f6f5d0d699eb40989b01c27a1f079fc42.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-10,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $13.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-10,6]$ given that $f(-10)=-2.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-10,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-10,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-10,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-10) & =-2 \\\\ f(6) & =f(-10)+{\\int }_{-10}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+13 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-2,11\\}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-10.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3aa69f0f-6fad-5e66-afb8-956cd67e5b53"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3aa69f0f-6fad-5e66-afb8-956cd67e5b53" :answer-field/id #uuid "3aa69f0f-6fad-5e66-afb8-956cd67e5b53" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4fc30760-ee1f-55b8-aae6-31bcfde7b6dc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4fc30760-ee1f-55b8-aae6-31bcfde7b6dc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4fc30760-ee1f-55b8-aae6-31bcfde7b6dc" :answer/id #uuid "4fc30760-ee1f-55b8-aae6-31bcfde7b6dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9b464b81-5baf-5338-aa06-b3e5f256249d" :question/id #uuid "9b464b81-5baf-5338-aa06-b3e5f256249d" :question/math-academy-id "q-144837" :question/problem "![](images/66/6684670c5999281044dd1e08faabbf06e7aeb3fef7902835ea9ea6c07d148b5a.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,4]$ given that $f(-2)=-3.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =-3 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3+8 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-3,5\\}=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c936d966-d407-55f3-833f-5a0d48ccc990"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c936d966-d407-55f3-833f-5a0d48ccc990" :answer-field/id #uuid "c936d966-d407-55f3-833f-5a0d48ccc990" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c09c46d9-008d-5f1a-994d-aae5ef1caf70" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cb0c97bf-a701-5927-ad4e-e2760701cfc1" "answer-ec2619ca-cf89-514f-821b-3f08e732ac00" "answer-c09c46d9-008d-5f1a-994d-aae5ef1caf70" "answer-1577bbd3-e41b-5487-8909-19851b51a970" "answer-c681754b-a4e1-554d-bb13-0f1a0241560f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb0c97bf-a701-5927-ad4e-e2760701cfc1" :answer/id #uuid "cb0c97bf-a701-5927-ad4e-e2760701cfc1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec2619ca-cf89-514f-821b-3f08e732ac00" :answer/id #uuid "ec2619ca-cf89-514f-821b-3f08e732ac00" :answer/type :ans