Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =-10 \\\\ f(3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-10+3 \\\\  & =-7 \\\\ f(5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-10+3-5 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-10,-7,-12\\}=-12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-12,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-71d68880-e750-51f3-aaab-c459cfd8a58a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-71d68880-e750-51f3-aaab-c459cfd8a58a" :answer-field/id #uuid "71d68880-e750-51f3-aaab-c459cfd8a58a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fd903076-dead-5638-8545-5cbc8bcb7552" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4d007e8f-8054-5ee3-b94b-d992a6985939" "answer-fd903076-dead-5638-8545-5cbc8bcb7552" "answer-8884fd70-0ce7-57f2-8714-e84fe064ead3" "answer-656b3af1-56aa-563c-a9f2-d823f796cf39" "answer-6f636ec8-1a9f-5adf-abaf-6e27b19d60cc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d007e8f-8054-5ee3-b94b-d992a6985939" :answer/id #uuid "4d007e8f-8054-5ee3-b94b-d992a6985939" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd903076-dead-5638-8545-5cbc8bcb7552" :answer/id #uuid "fd903076-dead-5638-8545-5cbc8bcb7552" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8884fd70-0ce7-57f2-8714-e84fe064ead3" :answer/id #uuid "8884fd70-0ce7-57f2-8714-e84fe064ead3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-656b3af1-56aa-563c-a9f2-d823f796cf39" :answer/id #uuid "656b3af1-56aa-563c-a9f2-d823f796cf39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f636ec8-1a9f-5adf-abaf-6e27b19d60cc" :answer/id #uuid "6f636ec8-1a9f-5adf-abaf-6e27b19d60cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101475 :question/problem "![](images/d6/d652e7ffe301986acf3b5ed02c00cc775d5f9ec2e7849248b7478e90a2a4aacb.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $1$ and $5$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,2]$ given that $f(-2)=-5.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =-5 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5-1 \\\\  & =-6 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5-1+5 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-5,-6,-1\\}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-6,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100011 :answer-field/correct 17592186100015 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-6cbda409-a223-57c5-93ac-3f78fe61c842" :question/id #uuid "6cbda409-a223-57c5-93ac-3f78fe61c842" :question/math-academy-id "q-341244" :question/problem "![](images/73/73610eebf575c612fc5b64ec6df264baffb338bdf3f2dbdc848477262874a54d.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4$ and $7$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,2]$ given that $f(-3)=5.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =5 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+4 \\\\  & =9 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+4-7 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{5,9,2\\}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-508b7401-7584-5847-a9c7-fe4a7c28f982"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-508b7401-7584-5847-a9c7-fe4a7c28f982" :answer-field/id #uuid "508b7401-7584-5847-a9c7-fe4a7c28f982" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d1ecd168-33d4-549d-bb83-5f68dbca2132" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d1ecd168-33d4-549d-bb83-5f68dbca2132"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1ecd168-33d4-549d-bb83-5f68dbca2132" :answer/id #uuid "d1ecd168-33d4-549d-bb83-5f68dbca2132" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4760d6e4-5155-5d78-b6d2-f55eb97036fb" :question/id #uuid "4760d6e4-5155-5d78-b6d2-f55eb97036fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-144866" :question/problem "![](images/db/dbbbcdb54dc8d6f96957ca01f4eeccea0c24c79eb95fc6c10953534d38e4d724.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-8,0].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $6,$ $2,$ and $4$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-8,0]$ given that $f(-8)=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-8,0]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-8,0),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-4$ and $x=-2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-8,-4,-2,0.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-8) & =2 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(-8)+{\\int }_{-8}^{-4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+6 \\\\  & =8 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(-8)+{\\int }_{-8}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+6-2 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-8)+{\\int }_{-8}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+6-2+4 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{2,8,6,10\\}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-46eb44a3-a8e1-5a19-aeb3-6ef173f8b41b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-46eb44a3-a8e1-5a19-aeb3-6ef173f8b41b" :answer-field/id #uuid "46eb44a3-a8e1-5a19-aeb3-6ef173f8b41b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0792ef62-08ea-5707-8327-c6e5163ce588" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d9a946c6-0bd9-50e0-8185-2e9896bd42e4" "answer-b7b47704-e864-540f-bb6b-29319e7d7b5c" "answer-a8805474-e6d8-5e64-b86f-dd449112f5bd" "answer-dc68c10e-ed5b-5324-8eca-dc60da0273a9" "answer-0792ef62-08ea-5707-8327-c6e5163ce588"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9a946c6-0bd9-50e0-8185-2e9896bd42e4" :answer/id #uuid "d9a946c6-0bd9-50e0-8185-2e9896bd42e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7b47704-e864-540f-bb6b-29319e7d7b5c" :answer/id #uuid "b7b47704-e864-540f-bb6b-29319e7d7b5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8805474-e6d8-5e64-b86f-dd449112f5bd" :answer/id #uuid "a8805474-e6d8-5e64-b86f-dd449112f5bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc68c10e-ed5b-5324-8eca-dc60da0273a9" :answer/id #uuid "dc68c10e-ed5b-5324-8eca-dc60da0273a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0792ef62-08ea-5707-8327-c6e5163ce588" :answer/id #uuid "0792ef62-08ea-5707-8327-c6e5163ce588" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-20a7e745-4621-5ebb-935e-e596b9f08fba" :question/id #uuid "20a7e745-4621-5ebb-935e-e596b9f08fba" :question/math-academy-id "q-342903" :question/problem "![](images/31/3123021ea5734c0ff2e8edcb716245b603de6f9dde7ebf0d367e1eeb8383bdf8.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-6,0].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $3$ and $10$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-6,0]$ given that $f(-6)=2.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-6,0]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-6,0),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-6,-3,0.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-6) & =2 \\\\ f(-3) & =f(-6)+{\\int }_{-6}^{-3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+3 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-6)+{\\int }_{-6}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+3-10 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{2,5,-5\\}=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-5,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7bb28b96-8669-5b5e-98ee-1ee25daf74e4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7bb28b96-8669-5b5e-98ee-1ee25daf74e4" :answer-field/id #uuid "7bb28b96-8669-5b5e-98ee-1ee25daf74e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6a5535b1-fd90-5e4a-8596-20391200d35b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6a5535b1-fd90-5e4a-8596-20391200d35b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a5535b1-fd90-5e4a-8596-20391200d35b" :answer/id #uuid "6a5535b1-fd90-5e4a-8596-20391200d35b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d9b31f47-0173-5340-9051-1c0bbc0f3595" :question/id #uuid "d9b31f47-0173-5340-9051-1c0bbc0f3595" :question/math-academy-id "q-15776" :question/problem "![](images/52/527c042c57a965b22f4e9841e59087291382704d6ea29f5f444926257444d5f6.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-1,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find an $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ on the interval $[-1,4]$ given that $f(-1)=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-1,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-1,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0,3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-1,0,3,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of three triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/f5/f512273895da627c314f00a55bd507d6ae43cd1c6af95d98bd4b475903303e7d.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(-1) \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(-1)+3 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(-1)+3+(-1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{2,1,4,3\\}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=1,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a6dc031b-5260-5385-b1ed-663b87376e54"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a6dc031b-5260-5385-b1ed-663b87376e54" :answer-field/id #uuid "a6dc031b-5260-5385-b1ed-663b87376e54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ddac1aa6-f011-564e-bebf-e731b91c1c69" :answer-field/choices ["answer-68c5f4d8-ebe7-53a0-b429-1c1c5cdacd4d" "answer-ddac1aa6-f011-564e-bebf-e731b91c1c69" "answer-2395c4b7-bcdc-5eb7-b5ef-f78390bd16a3" "answer-03319aea-2d62-5506-acf1-b821d450c5a9" "answer-16e8f84b-a22c-5b49-a071-bac5c3c9a64f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68c5f4d8-ebe7-53a0-b429-1c1c5cdacd4d" :answer/id #uuid "68c5f4d8-ebe7-53a0-b429-1c1c5cdacd4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ddac1aa6-f011-564e-bebf-e731b91c1c69" :answer/id #uuid "ddac1aa6-f011-564e-bebf-e731b91c1c69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2395c4b7-bcdc-5eb7-b5ef-f78390bd16a3" :answer/id #uuid "2395c4b7-bcdc-5eb7-b5ef-f78390bd16a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03319aea-2d62-5506-acf1-b821d450c5a9" :answer/id #uuid "03319aea-2d62-5506-acf1-b821d450c5a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16e8f84b-a22c-5b49-a071-bac5c3c9a64f" :answer/id #uuid "16e8f84b-a22c-5b49-a071-bac5c3c9a64f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8f946771-a506-530c-b84d-03471b2aa334" :question/id #uuid "8f946771-a506-530c-b84d-03471b2aa334" :question/math-academy-id "q-343853" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b1d5208f46af834deda70086b9ef4b91defe7359f625d9fb264b370965196a0b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[-4,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ if $f(-4)=-1.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in [-4,3],$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $2$ trapezoids and $2$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/ae/aea529468efff36403aca0bf9ee9cc6bc4c5680476ccb81314388bb315f700df.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =-1 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+2.5+0.5 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+2.5+0.5+(-1.5)+(-4) \\\\  & =-3.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-1,2,-3.5\\}=-3.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-3.5,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7aa6557a-d133-596a-b431-eb4eace2e7f0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7aa6557a-d133-596a-b431-eb4eace2e7f0" :answer-field/id #uuid "7aa6557a-d133-596a-b431-eb4eace2e7f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-cb4d2bf2-2853-5cad-97a9-4d01b5dc71de" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cb4d2bf2-2853-5cad-97a9-4d01b5dc71de"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb4d2bf2-2853-5cad-97a9-4d01b5dc71de" :answer/id #uuid "cb4d2bf2-2853-5cad-97a9-4d01b5dc71de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d754760c-74db-51d7-aeca-199ef191761d" :question/id #uuid "d754760c-74db-51d7-aeca-199ef191761d" :question/math-academy-id "q-19084" :question/problem "![](images/2a/2aea7b223ec18094d626e8d8937a5844e786cb1b936fb3b6b478f672e4d0fb9d.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the minimum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,3]$ given that $f(-2)=1.5.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,-1,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of one triangle and one trapezoid. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/d8/d811c4920b4ddb05170afc1f0c38fe216c23139556b6f7e10829b663e894a7fb.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =1.5 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1.5+(-0.5) \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1.5+(-0.5)+3 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{1.5,1,4\\}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=1,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cbfdf821-69fb-57a9-a5a9-97d7a8ead96a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cbfdf821-69fb-57a9-a5a9-97d7a8ead96a" :answer-field/id #uuid "cbfdf821-69fb-57a9-a5a9-97d7a8ead96a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-54a9f437-47aa-5650-a6f4-a78a12c4e80a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fd276e11-0fed-5dbd-b2a9-4d15eb6ab208" "answer-ad934404-4176-5896-bb92-6ac4ba4eb09c" "answer-54a9f437-47aa-5650-a6f4-a78a12c4e80a" "answer-7f930dd7-377b-5995-ba4d-b11719784356" "answer-51efe52f-7214-5bbb-9fc5-08d1743885d2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd276e11-0fed-5dbd-b2a9-4d15eb6ab208" :answer/id #uuid "fd276e11-0fed-5dbd-b2a9-4d15eb6ab208" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad934404-4176-5896-bb92-6ac4ba4eb09c" :answer/id #uuid "ad934404-4176-5896-bb92-6ac4ba4eb09c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54a9f437-47aa-5650-a6f4-a78a12c4e80a" :answer/id #uuid "54a9f437-47aa-5650-a6f4-a78a12c4e80a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f930dd7-377b-5995-ba4d-b11719784356" :answer/id #uuid "7f930dd7-377b-5995-ba4d-b11719784356" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51efe52f-7214-5bbb-9fc5-08d1743885d2" :answer/id #uuid "51efe52f-7214-5bbb-9fc5-08d1743885d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186102026 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to use the graph of a function's derivative to minimize the function. Let's understand how this process works by studying a specific example.\n\nThe graph of $y = f'(x),$ the *derivative* of $f(x),$ is shown in the picture below. \n\n![](images/2e/2ee1721d439ee0234b0f8a99ed0e4a56c63041e36919b00978267cf361a5322c.png)\n\nThe areas of the shaded regions are indicated on the diagram. Additionally, it is known that $f(-2)=5.$ Our goal is find the *minimum* value of $f(x)$ on the interval $x\\in [-2,2].$\n\nTo find the minimum value of $f(x),$ we use the candidates test.\n\nAccording to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. Therefore, we proceed as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ so there are no non-stationary critical points. Additionally, we see that $f'(x)=0$ at $x=1.$ Therefore, the candidates are \n\n$$\nx=-2,\\quad x=1,\\quad x=2.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\n- The value of $f(x)$ at the left endpoint is given:\n\n$$\nf(-2) = 5\n$$\n\n- To compute $f(1),$ we apply the fundamental theorem of calculus. Since the areas of the rectangle and middle triangle in our picture equal ${\\color{red}{2}}$ and ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac12}},$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5-2-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we *subtracted* these areas since both lie *under* the $x$-axis.\n\n- To compute $f(2),$ we apply the fundamental theorem of calculus once more. Using the fact that the area of the right triangle equals ${\\color{blue}{\\dfrac12}},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5-2-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\left\\lbrace 5, \\dfrac52, 3 \\right\\rbrace = \\dfrac52.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{min}}=\\dfrac52,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{min}=1.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101090 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192712 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f302fed8-a5b6-5ab4-978d-2dce7c91e287" "question-0272db1e-3587-51d4-ba80-cbc263d01f1e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f302fed8-a5b6-5ab4-978d-2dce7c91e287" :question/id #uuid "f302fed8-a5b6-5ab4-978d-2dce7c91e287" :question/math-academy-id "q-149437" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[0,12].$ \nUsing a right Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals, estimate the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=12$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $4$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $3\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n*Hint: The volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by*\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=12$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{12}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}.$ The (regular) step size $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{12-0}{3}=4.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *right* Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals and step size $Δx$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{12}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x≈\\pi \\cdot (g(4)+g(8)+g(12))\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nWe set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $4$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $3\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ |\n| $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}$ | $1$ | $18$ | $0$ | $8$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our volume:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{12}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\pi \\cdot (g(4)+g(8)+g(12))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot (18+0+8)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 26\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =104\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1e36c481-1a7d-539b-a5da-9e256e0e030d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1e36c481-1a7d-539b-a5da-9e256e0e030d" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e36c481-1a7d-539b-a5da-9e256e0e030d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d2a5fb43-9733-54d4-ae70-87014652f121" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d2a5fb43-9733-54d4-ae70-87014652f121" "answer-8bfc6107-bba4-5df6-bb3d-40aae12b2fd0" "answer-f127f49a-4ab2-5bca-8efb-9ad5241da47a" "answer-504c6583-6b60-5cc7-a4f8-49752ce0621b" "answer-29e0ebeb-fefa-5de4-b8b4-c14ae210cc54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2a5fb43-9733-54d4-ae70-87014652f121" :answer/id #uuid "d2a5fb43-9733-54d4-ae70-87014652f121" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "104\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8bfc6107-bba4-5df6-bb3d-40aae12b2fd0" :answer/id #uuid "8bfc6107-bba4-5df6-bb3d-40aae12b2fd0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f127f49a-4ab2-5bca-8efb-9ad5241da47a" :answer/id #uuid "f127f49a-4ab2-5bca-8efb-9ad5241da47a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "52\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-504c6583-6b60-5cc7-a4f8-49752ce0621b" :answer/id #uuid "504c6583-6b60-5cc7-a4f8-49752ce0621b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "76\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29e0ebeb-fefa-5de4-b8b4-c14ae210cc54" :answer/id #uuid "29e0ebeb-fefa-5de4-b8b4-c14ae210cc54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "96\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192702 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a8228bcb-8e29-55e3-8d2e-2b38cb08cc3b" "question-dc84fa2c-48fe-510a-8646-e9ae7a753f1e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a8228bcb-8e29-55e3-8d2e-2b38cb08cc3b" :question/id #uuid "a8228bcb-8e29-55e3-8d2e-2b38cb08cc3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-149498" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be positive and strictly increasing for $x\\in [5,10],$ and differentiable for $x\\in (5,10).$ Suppose we approximate the area under the curve $y=g(x)={x}^{3}\\cdot f(x)$ over the interval $[5,10]$ using a right Riemann sum. Given that $g(x)>0,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. ${f}^{′}(x)<0$\n\nII. ${g}^{′}(x)<0$\n\nIII. The Riemann sum overestimates the true area." :question/worked-solution "We're given that $g(x)={x}^{3}\\cdot f(x)>0.$ Now, recall the following:\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly increasing, the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing, the right Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\nLet's now consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Differentiating $g(x)={x}^{3}\\cdot f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3})^{′}\\cdot f(x)+{x}^{3}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)+{x}^{3}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x)$ is positive and strictly increasing over the interval $(5,10),$ we have \n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}>0,\\,f(x)>0,\\,{x}^{3}>0,\\,{f}^{′}(x)>0.\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)={{3{x}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{{x}^{3}}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{{f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}={{3{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{{x}^{3}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${g}^{′}(x)>0,$ the function $g(x)$ is strictly increasing. Therefore, the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y={x}^{3}\\cdot f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4633a0d6-26c7-541d-b8e0-d9639f7db195"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4633a0d6-26c7-541d-b8e0-d9639f7db195" :answer-field/id #uuid "4633a0d6-26c7-541d-b8e0-d9639f7db195" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2d0f9bd4-1c42-518e-8a85-6fd510f818c7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2e7b5dd1-45ce-5207-ba2f-a7fe58afd9ca" "answer-ce91986b-ea40-5ee9-8cff-03eb4608ba97" "answer-a6cd4858-b628-5fda-bb17-ebfb005faec1" "answer-fa544d09-b82a-5a0e-a5ce-9b72cfaee527" "answer-2d0f9bd4-1c42-518e-8a85-6fd510f818c7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e7b5dd1-45ce-5207-ba2f-a7fe58afd9ca" :answer/id #uuid "2e7b5dd1-45ce-5207-ba2f-a7fe58afd9ca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce91986b-ea40-5ee9-8cff-03eb4608ba97" :answer/id #uuid "ce91986b-ea40-5ee9-8cff-03eb4608ba97" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6cd4858-b628-5fda-bb17-ebfb005faec1" :answer/id #uuid "a6cd4858-b628-5fda-bb17-ebfb005faec1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa544d09-b82a-5a0e-a5ce-9b72cfaee527" :answer/id #uuid "fa544d09-b82a-5a0e-a5ce-9b72cfaee527" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d0f9bd4-1c42-518e-8a85-6fd510f818c7" :answer/id #uuid "2d0f9bd4-1c42-518e-8a85-6fd510f818c7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-dc84fa2c-48fe-510a-8646-e9ae7a753f1e" :question/id #uuid "dc84fa2c-48fe-510a-8646-e9ae7a753f1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-149499" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be positive and strictly increasing for $x\\in [2,6],$ and differentiable for $x\\in (2,6).$ Suppose we approximate the area under the curve $y=g(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)$ over the interval $[2,6]$ using a right Riemann sum.  Given that $g(x)>0,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. ${f}^{′}(x)>0$\n\nII. ${g}^{′}(x)>0$\n\nIII. The Riemann sum underestimates the true area." :question/worked-solution "We're given that $g(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)>0.$ Now, recall the following:\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly increasing, the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing, the right Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\nLet's now consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Differentiating $g(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot f(x)+{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =2x\\cdot f(x)+{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x)$ is positive and strictly increasing over the interval $(2,6),$ we have \n\n$$\n2x>0,\\,f(x)>0,\\,{x}^{2}>0,\\,{f}^{′}(x)>0.\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)={{2x}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{{x}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{{f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}={{2x\\cdot f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${g}^{′}(x)>0,$ the function $g(x)$ is strictly increasing. Therefore, the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y={x}^{2}\\cdot f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-afc9afa2-0355-55d7-ab22-0b155eda768a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-afc9afa2-0355-55d7-ab22-0b155eda768a" :answer-field/id #uuid "afc9afa2-0355-55d7-ab22-0b155eda768a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fa9f2869-4ec7-5467-98e1-21e24df7e12a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fa9f2869-4ec7-5467-98e1-21e24df7e12a" "answer-28b4554c-46dc-510f-ac58-1873cdfd838c" "answer-6f44229b-81f7-51d2-a925-37e447f0f037" "answer-8475dc1b-d081-5b20-ad32-695e8ecb76fb" "answer-3a3204e2-55cf-5d83-b42c-28d5493becc3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa9f2869-4ec7-5467-98e1-21e24df7e12a" :answer/id #uuid "fa9f2869-4ec7-5467-98e1-21e24df7e12a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28b4554c-46dc-510f-ac58-1873cdfd838c" :answer/id #uuid "28b4554c-46dc-510f-ac58-1873cdfd838c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f44229b-81f7-51d2-a925-37e447f0f037" :answer/id #uuid "6f44229b-81f7-51d2-a925-37e447f0f037" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8475dc1b-d081-5b20-ad32-695e8ecb76fb" :answer/id #uuid "8475dc1b-d081-5b20-ad32-695e8ecb76fb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a3204e2-55cf-5d83-b42c-28d5493becc3" :answer/id #uuid "3a3204e2-55cf-5d83-b42c-28d5493becc3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0272db1e-3587-51d4-ba80-cbc263d01f1e" :question/id #uuid "0272db1e-3587-51d4-ba80-cbc263d01f1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-346315" :question/problem "The volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[5,20].$ \n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $8$ | $11$ | $14$ | $17$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nUsing a left Riemann sum with $5$ equal subintervals, estimate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when the region bounded by the curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=5$ and $x=20$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=5$ and $x=20$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{5}^{20}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}.$ The (regular) step size $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{20-5}{5}=3.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *left* Riemann sum with $5$ equal subintervals and step size $Δx$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pi {\\int }_{5}^{20}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x≈\\pi \\cdot (g(5)+g(8)+g(11)+g(14)+g(17))\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nWe set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $8$ | $11$ | $14$ | $17$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}$ | $1$ | $4$ | $1$ | $3$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our volume:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{5}^{20}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\pi \\cdot (g(5)+g(8)+g(11)+g(14)+g(17))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot (1+4+1+3+4)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 13\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =39\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2a18cebd-84e4-5a3a-96a0-241925ce8033"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2a18cebd-84e4-5a3a-96a0-241925ce8033" :answer-field/id #uuid "2a18cebd-84e4-5a3a-96a0-241925ce8033" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5e1dfa21-2b09-596a-bddf-9846939710c2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5e1dfa21-2b09-596a-bddf-9846939710c2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e1dfa21-2b09-596a-bddf-9846939710c2" :answer/id #uuid "5e1dfa21-2b09-596a-bddf-9846939710c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "39\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192701 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f43ac4b9-2815-5dee-ad9d-013b2abdd370"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f43ac4b9-2815-5dee-ad9d-013b2abdd370" :question/id #uuid "f43ac4b9-2815-5dee-ad9d-013b2abdd370" :question/math-academy-id "q-346317" :question/problem "The arc length of a smooth curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ and its derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ over the closed interval $[-4,2].$\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $\\sqrt{63}$ | $\\sqrt{15}$ | $\\sqrt{8}$ | $\\sqrt{48}$ |\n\nUsing a left Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals, estimate the arc length $L$ of the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-4$ and $x=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have that the arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-4$ and $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{-4}^{2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $g(x)=\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}.$ The (regular) step size $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-(-4)}{3}=2.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *left* Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals and step size $Δx$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{2}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x≈(g(-4)+g(-2)+g(0))\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nNow, we set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $\\sqrt{63}$ | $\\sqrt{15}$ | $\\sqrt{8}$ | $\\sqrt{48}$ |\n| $g(x)=\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}$ | $8$ | $4$ | $3$ | $7$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our arc length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-4}^{2}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈(g(-4)+g(-2)+g(0))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(8+4+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b068ad65-9909-5808-87be-751b4ea3f48f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b068ad65-9909-5808-87be-751b4ea3f48f" :answer-field/id #uuid "b068ad65-9909-5808-87be-751b4ea3f48f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0c930864-9f25-5293-a719-9a80599eef5c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0c930864-9f25-5293-a719-9a80599eef5c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c930864-9f25-5293-a719-9a80599eef5c" :answer/id #uuid "0c930864-9f25-5293-a719-9a80599eef5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-c82e0f22-6e6f-5ddc-90a0-55c3a1ddea8c" :activity/id #uuid "c82e0f22-6e6f-5ddc-90a0-55c3a1ddea8c" :activity/title "Approximating Areas Under Graphs of Composite Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048304 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-715fe870-9c23-5686-9db6-2f539d5d9cb7" "step-0ed82c2f-3101-544e-b938-9685c699089a" "step-9982e8c2-d630-5fcb-a2a6-f49134260361" "step-a2816dcc-c5bf-5d5d-91a7-508f9fb5354d" "step-48ceae03-b320-5028-a92a-40ef95f39f8d"] :activity/first-step "step-715fe870-9c23-5686-9db6-2f539d5d9cb7" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-715fe870-9c23-5686-9db6-2f539d5d9cb7" :step/id #uuid "715fe870-9c23-5686-9db6-2f539d5d9cb7" :step/content "question-f302fed8-a5b6-5ab4-978d-2dce7c91e287" :step/next "step-0ed82c2f-3101-544e-b938-9685c699089a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0ed82c2f-3101-544e-b938-9685c699089a" :step/id #uuid "0ed82c2f-3101-544e-b938-9685c699089a" :step/content "question-a8228bcb-8e29-55e3-8d2e-2b38cb08cc3b" :step/next "step-9982e8c2-d630-5fcb-a2a6-f49134260361" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9982e8c2-d630-5fcb-a2a6-f49134260361" :step/id #uuid "9982e8c2-d630-5fcb-a2a6-f49134260361" :step/content "question-dc84fa2c-48fe-510a-8646-e9ae7a753f1e" :step/next "step-a2816dcc-c5bf-5d5d-91a7-508f9fb5354d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a2816dcc-c5bf-5d5d-91a7-508f9fb5354d" :step/id #uuid "a2816dcc-c5bf-5d5d-91a7-508f9fb5354d" :step/content "question-0272db1e-3587-51d4-ba80-cbc263d01f1e" :step/next "step-48ceae03-b320-5028-a92a-40ef95f39f8d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-48ceae03-b320-5028-a92a-40ef95f39f8d" :step/id #uuid "48ceae03-b320-5028-a92a-40ef95f39f8d" :step/content "question-f43ac4b9-2815-5dee-ad9d-013b2abdd370" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186129756 :question/answer-fields 17592186128017] [:db/retract 17592186129523 :question/answer-fields 17592186126637] {:db/id 17592186129264 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d7a0c5c0-c3dc-5ed9-a9e6-253b15c1791f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129756 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $3$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-d7a0c5c0-c3dc-5ed9-a9e6-253b15c1791f" :question/id #uuid "d7a0c5c0-c3dc-5ed9-a9e6-253b15c1791f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161749" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)^{2}\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f8b1c6d3-79e8-5764-9d1a-bcce12f2ad8b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f8b1c6d3-79e8-5764-9d1a-bcce12f2ad8b" :answer-field/id #uuid "f8b1c6d3-79e8-5764-9d1a-bcce12f2ad8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-354ac988-d8c3-57cf-b78f-6912e9ac0585" :answer-field/choices ["answer-959f6e54-dd4c-5921-b12b-794921892ac0" "answer-442f019f-9950-54ef-b315-5fed1c784a9b" "answer-db09b7e2-e0ce-5f11-ad20-482fba461cc7" "answer-354ac988-d8c3-57cf-b78f-6912e9ac0585" "answer-494e353c-817b-5ca7-a101-f7151bd4954d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-959f6e54-dd4c-5921-b12b-794921892ac0" :answer/id #uuid "959f6e54-dd4c-5921-b12b-794921892ac0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-442f019f-9950-54ef-b315-5fed1c784a9b" :answer/id #uuid "442f019f-9950-54ef-b315-5fed1c784a9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db09b7e2-e0ce-5f11-ad20-482fba461cc7" :answer/id #uuid "db09b7e2-e0ce-5f11-ad20-482fba461cc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-354ac988-d8c3-57cf-b78f-6912e9ac0585" :answer/id #uuid "354ac988-d8c3-57cf-b78f-6912e9ac0585" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-494e353c-817b-5ca7-a101-f7151bd4954d" :answer/id #uuid "494e353c-817b-5ca7-a101-f7151bd4954d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129523 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $5$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{{\\rho }^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129281 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3eee5be8-fdde-5369-b6f0-771204d5bd54"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-3eee5be8-fdde-5369-b6f0-771204d5bd54" :question/id #uuid "3eee5be8-fdde-5369-b6f0-771204d5bd54" :question/math-academy-id "q-161775" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=3$ for $y\\le 0$ and $z\\ge 0.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/e6/e63035cbe368a4f41b7197d70880d0639f004e26996fe6b5055e69966e0f66ee.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-024d0b3c-4d66-50af-a789-68c23cbe417a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-024d0b3c-4d66-50af-a789-68c23cbe417a" :answer-field/id #uuid "024d0b3c-4d66-50af-a789-68c23cbe417a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-557a8050-44a3-59e1-8a70-aa6bd1dd5460" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3756a535-be12-51cb-a4f4-eebb7d355566" "answer-c886c152-17ed-511e-a809-d63c2213db33" "answer-557a8050-44a3-59e1-8a70-aa6bd1dd5460" "answer-5b788302-0dfd-54bf-a49b-6d49283dd409" "answer-1888a529-95b0-58f6-9146-6b50fc190c6e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3756a535-be12-51cb-a4f4-eebb7d355566" :answer/id #uuid "3756a535-be12-51cb-a4f4-eebb7d355566" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c886c152-17ed-511e-a809-d63c2213db33" :answer/id #uuid "c886c152-17ed-511e-a809-d63c2213db33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-557a8050-44a3-59e1-8a70-aa6bd1dd5460" :answer/id #uuid "557a8050-44a3-59e1-8a70-aa6bd1dd5460" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b788302-0dfd-54bf-a49b-6d49283dd409" :answer/id #uuid "5b788302-0dfd-54bf-a49b-6d49283dd409" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1888a529-95b0-58f6-9146-6b50fc190c6e" :answer/id #uuid "1888a529-95b0-58f6-9146-6b50fc190c6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129265 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b6ef3a51-3ce7-571a-8d2a-d26c504b1810"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b6ef3a51-3ce7-571a-8d2a-d26c504b1810" :question/id #uuid "b6ef3a51-3ce7-571a-8d2a-d26c504b1810" :question/math-academy-id "q-161923" :question/problem "![](images/98/989c9452bebc4ee6c2253c920655830a0b3d0adf0ddb7b90c1ea6a9528ef1448.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=-6x$ for $y\\le 0,$ shown above. \nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$  represents one-half of the sphere of radius $3$ centered at $(-3,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=-6x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =-6x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =-6\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/df/df8a9da3b88f3b40d85005b156215f7fa7760257f284acf98ec186d5a2e3ab8d.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function  $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{{(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-60daa8fe-705e-5990-9f0d-a1092f6eb497"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-60daa8fe-705e-5990-9f0d-a1092f6eb497" :answer-field/id #uuid "60daa8fe-705e-5990-9f0d-a1092f6eb497" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1619dc0b-d53c-58c4-92e8-5b68a431da0f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c72bf197-8bb8-5cea-9e15-63920f550d30" "answer-4c7493ae-3582-5f1c-82f8-0c2dae976bc5" "answer-1619dc0b-d53c-58c4-92e8-5b68a431da0f" "answer-90ead743-a1fc-5f4f-9075-c4fd3b6866f5" "answer-35312181-3164-564e-951d-368b81aed2bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c72bf197-8bb8-5cea-9e15-63920f550d30" :answer/id #uuid "c72bf197-8bb8-5cea-9e15-63920f550d30" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c7493ae-3582-5f1c-82f8-0c2dae976bc5" :answer/id #uuid "4c7493ae-3582-5f1c-82f8-0c2dae976bc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1619dc0b-d53c-58c4-92e8-5b68a431da0f" :answer/id #uuid "1619dc0b-d53c-58c4-92e8-5b68a431da0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90ead743-a1fc-5f4f-9075-c4fd3b6866f5" :answer/id #uuid "90ead743-a1fc-5f4f-9075-c4fd3b6866f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35312181-3164-564e-951d-368b81aed2bc" :answer/id #uuid "35312181-3164-564e-951d-368b81aed2bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-b12c0b41-6524-5f7e-98fb-035095d4ffdd" :activity/id #uuid "b12c0b41-6524-5f7e-98fb-035095d4ffdd" :activity/title "Triple Integrals in Spherical Polar Coordinates" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050390 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-a7f96869-1eb8-56ee-b2b0-027fb5f4ea19" "step-418bbe3c-695f-5e7f-9845-18733413d41c" "step-c99c6fff-0e07-5d5a-9f2b-bd9cf145afcd" "step-170b5eaa-6d1a-59ae-8ad5-3639ac55bed9" "step-b242ffec-298a-56b8-b2ef-a020d44a7334"] :activity/first-step "step-a7f96869-1eb8-56ee-b2b0-027fb5f4ea19" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-a7f96869-1eb8-56ee-b2b0-027fb5f4ea19" :step/id #uuid "a7f96869-1eb8-56ee-b2b0-027fb5f4ea19" :step/content 17592186129756 :step/next "step-418bbe3c-695f-5e7f-9845-18733413d41c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-418bbe3c-695f-5e7f-9845-18733413d41c" :step/id #uuid "418bbe3c-695f-5e7f-9845-18733413d41c" :step/content "question-d7a0c5c0-c3dc-5ed9-a9e6-253b15c1791f" :step/next "step-c99c6fff-0e07-5d5a-9f2b-bd9cf145afcd" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c99c6fff-0e07-5d5a-9f2b-bd9cf145afcd" :step/id #uuid "c99c6fff-0e07-5d5a-9f2b-bd9cf145afcd" :step/content 17592186129523 :step/next "step-170b5eaa-6d1a-59ae-8ad5-3639ac55bed9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-170b5eaa-6d1a-59ae-8ad5-3639ac55bed9" :step/id #uuid "170b5eaa-6d1a-59ae-8ad5-3639ac55bed9" :step/content "question-3eee5be8-fdde-5369-b6f0-771204d5bd54" :step/next "step-b242ffec-298a-56b8-b2ef-a020d44a7334" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b242ffec-298a-56b8-b2ef-a020d44a7334" :step/id #uuid "b242ffec-298a-56b8-b2ef-a020d44a7334" :step/content "question-b6ef3a51-3ce7-571a-8d2a-d26c504b1810" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124653 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3e112cc1-03f8-5d0c-87ca-e5043bf5d793" "question-8d70bf0e-d43a-5715-a8f9-251ae990c8fe" "question-c0460f02-5116-53e0-8333-fa6ec98b4c5e" "question-524ccb21-d45d-53ab-aaf4-d88936372977"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124655 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1640e015-4c92-5aff-b3e3-71cc75c19c43"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-3e112cc1-03f8-5d0c-87ca-e5043bf5d793" :question/id #uuid "3e112cc1-03f8-5d0c-87ca-e5043bf5d793" :question/math-academy-id "q-103946" :question/problem "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y)=x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.$" :question/worked-solution "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} & -2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=0 \\\\ -2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\ne 0$ for any point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1-2{x}^{2}=0 \\\\ -2xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n1-2{x}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these values into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})y=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6b84b635-e2f3-5571-acbc-aee2c5448411"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6b84b635-e2f3-5571-acbc-aee2c5448411" :answer-field/id #uuid "6b84b635-e2f3-5571-acbc-aee2c5448411" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3afe7dca-912b-5e49-87ff-b074e745dad4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ab75a4fc-2e7f-5073-8208-5bb2a1704cd8" "answer-0ccb0a46-7f08-564c-91f7-36ac15556815" "answer-ec041ce7-4b13-5a33-befa-4c27a0b8b07b" "answer-3afe7dca-912b-5e49-87ff-b074e745dad4" "answer-e5e0f804-7571-501b-87e2-55aaee4fef64"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab75a4fc-2e7f-5073-8208-5bb2a1704cd8" :answer/id #uuid "ab75a4fc-2e7f-5073-8208-5bb2a1704cd8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ccb0a46-7f08-564c-91f7-36ac15556815" :answer/id #uuid "0ccb0a46-7f08-564c-91f7-36ac15556815" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec041ce7-4b13-5a33-befa-4c27a0b8b07b" :answer/id #uuid "ec041ce7-4b13-5a33-befa-4c27a0b8b07b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(2,0)$ and $(-2,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3afe7dca-912b-5e49-87ff-b074e745dad4" :answer/id #uuid "3afe7dca-912b-5e49-87ff-b074e745dad4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5e0f804-7571-501b-87e2-55aaee4fef64" :answer/id #uuid "e5e0f804-7571-501b-87e2-55aaee4fef64" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(\\sqrt{2},1)$ and $(-\\sqrt{2},-1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8d70bf0e-d43a-5715-a8f9-251ae990c8fe" :question/id #uuid "8d70bf0e-d43a-5715-a8f9-251ae990c8fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-103926" :question/problem "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-4x+8y.$" :question/worked-solution "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={y}^{2}-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2xy+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & 2xy+8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4=0 \\\\ 2xy+8=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n2xy+8=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-4=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into $x=-\\frac{4}{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-2 & \\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{(-2)}=2 \\\\ y=2 & \\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(2,-2)$ and $(-2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(2,-2)$ and $(-2,2).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a60f7d70-b0fc-5169-a4bc-8635f6350892"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a60f7d70-b0fc-5169-a4bc-8635f6350892" :answer-field/id #uuid "a60f7d70-b0fc-5169-a4bc-8635f6350892" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-21293cc9-ef4b-5127-9c54-f35b9ae4554d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-372af7e2-d50e-5e72-a499-a308d43de668" "answer-00f64e4d-81e4-5c72-84a2-8f9d4f4b7003" "answer-21293cc9-ef4b-5127-9c54-f35b9ae4554d" "answer-8cb7f350-b88e-5213-8cd6-2c223d8a97e6" "answer-769e3ce2-5299-535a-a0be-e380fc4706e8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-372af7e2-d50e-5e72-a499-a308d43de668" :answer/id #uuid "372af7e2-d50e-5e72-a499-a308d43de668" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00f64e4d-81e4-5c72-84a2-8f9d4f4b7003" :answer/id #uuid "00f64e4d-81e4-5c72-84a2-8f9d4f4b7003" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(2,-2)$ $" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21293cc9-ef4b-5127-9c54-f35b9ae4554d" :answer/id #uuid "21293cc9-ef4b-5127-9c54-f35b9ae4554d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(2,-2)$ and $(-2,2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8cb7f350-b88e-5213-8cd6-2c223d8a97e6" :answer/id #uuid "8cb7f350-b88e-5213-8cd6-2c223d8a97e6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $(-2,-2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-769e3ce2-5299-535a-a0be-e380fc4706e8" :answer/id #uuid "769e3ce2-5299-535a-a0be-e380fc4706e8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(4,-2)$ and $(-4,2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c0460f02-5116-53e0-8333-fa6ec98b4c5e" :question/id #uuid "c0460f02-5116-53e0-8333-fa6ec98b4c5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-36677" :question/problem "Determine the stationary point of $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for any point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(0,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(0,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9ae32f88-30e5-5210-86d4-1850ad11001c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9ae32f88-30e5-5210-86d4-1850ad11001c" :answer-field/id #uuid "9ae32f88-30e5-5210-86d4-1850ad11001c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e5993b94-e08d-5156-ba17-8c6f8836c6bc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e5993b94-e08d-5156-ba17-8c6f8836c6bc" "answer-0749c3d7-da1b-587a-a3df-69d235451d3c" "answer-39259c10-6efb-5a51-b653-92f9766862ec" "answer-12398915-d63f-5e27-afdb-be1f6db70182" "answer-002b97a2-a6b7-5cd7-9616-af11e4231fd3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5993b94-e08d-5156-ba17-8c6f8836c6bc" :answer/id #uuid "e5993b94-e08d-5156-ba17-8c6f8836c6bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0749c3d7-da1b-587a-a3df-69d235451d3c" :answer/id #uuid "0749c3d7-da1b-587a-a3df-69d235451d3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39259c10-6efb-5a51-b653-92f9766862ec" :answer/id #uuid "39259c10-6efb-5a51-b653-92f9766862ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12398915-d63f-5e27-afdb-be1f6db70182" :answer/id #uuid "12398915-d63f-5e27-afdb-be1f6db70182" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-002b97a2-a6b7-5cd7-9616-af11e4231fd3" :answer/id #uuid "002b97a2-a6b7-5cd7-9616-af11e4231fd3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No stationary points" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-524ccb21-d45d-53ab-aaf4-d88936372977" :question/id #uuid "524ccb21-d45d-53ab-aaf4-d88936372977" :question/math-academy-id "q-36799" :question/problem "Determine the stationary point of $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+4xy+{y}^{2}+4y.$" :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn tihs case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =4x+4y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =4x+2y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+4y & 4x+2y+4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+4y=0 \\\\ 4x+2y+4=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4x+4y=0\\,⟹\\,x=-y.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting $x=-y$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-y)+2y+4 & =0 \\\\ -4y+2y+4 & =0 \\\\ -2y & =-4 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting this value into $x=-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny=2\\,⟹\\,x=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(-2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(-2,2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a7580df7-3ea9-5eba-8784-550ab709f1c8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a7580df7-3ea9-5eba-8784-550ab709f1c8" :answer-field/id #uuid "a7580df7-3ea9-5eba-8784-550ab709f1c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1af52b2f-5397-5a81-abc1-978645b4d1a2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1af52b2f-5397-5a81-abc1-978645b4d1a2" "answer-89248e74-a748-5084-86b6-3604e1ebdb8d" "answer-878c880e-3b54-584e-b46c-b726a887844e" "answer-32a95250-bdf5-5705-8ecc-b001eba9f20b" "answer-7eca7f5a-6e66-5afe-a187-bb1fac895726"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1af52b2f-5397-5a81-abc1-978645b4d1a2" :answer/id #uuid "1af52b2f-5397-5a81-abc1-978645b4d1a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89248e74-a748-5084-86b6-3604e1ebdb8d" :answer/id #uuid "89248e74-a748-5084-86b6-3604e1ebdb8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-878c880e-3b54-584e-b46c-b726a887844e" :answer/id #uuid "878c880e-3b54-584e-b46c-b726a887844e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32a95250-bdf5-5705-8ecc-b001eba9f20b" :answer/id #uuid "32a95250-bdf5-5705-8ecc-b001eba9f20b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7eca7f5a-6e66-5afe-a187-bb1fac895726" :answer/id #uuid "7eca7f5a-6e66-5afe-a187-bb1fac895726" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No stationary points" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1640e015-4c92-5aff-b3e3-71cc75c19c43" :question/id #uuid "1640e015-4c92-5aff-b3e3-71cc75c19c43" :question/math-academy-id "q-105280" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & -2y{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & -2y{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2y{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2})=0 \\\\ -2y{e}^{x}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n-2y{e}^{x}=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{0}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(-2,0)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}))={e}^{x}({x}^{2}+4x-{y}^{2}+2)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y{e}^{x})=-2y{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}))=-2y{e}^{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y{e}^{x})=-2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+4x-{y}^{2}+2) & -2y{e}^{x} \\\\ -2y{e}^{x} & -2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=2\\cdot (-2)-{0}^{2}=-4<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-2,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-2,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-2,0)=det({f}^{″}(-2,0))=(-2{e}^{-2})^{2}-{0}^{2}=4{e}^{-4}>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(-2,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-2,0)=({f}^{″}(-2,0))_{11}=-2{e}^{-2}<0,$ the function $f$ has a local maximum at $(-2,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(-2,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a2a97630-0fc1-580b-b276-59f04f851b20"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a2a97630-0fc1-580b-b276-59f04f851b20" :answer-field/id #uuid "a2a97630-0fc1-580b-b276-59f04f851b20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2193002c-a5f9-5f43-9491-8ce090c6ce8e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cda095a0-bf38-5307-9122-32f99a1602b7" "answer-8002492d-5e55-5026-af75-c4385bd4e90f" "answer-04ac1472-02e4-5f9b-96d9-0b4fd8a918e3" "answer-2193002c-a5f9-5f43-9491-8ce090c6ce8e" "answer-4493e258-0fda-5c69-96f7-243298d2e5b6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cda095a0-bf38-5307-9122-32f99a1602b7" :answer/id #uuid "cda095a0-bf38-5307-9122-32f99a1602b7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-1,0)$ and a local maximum at $(1,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8002492d-5e55-5026-af75-c4385bd4e90f" :answer/id #uuid "8002492d-5e55-5026-af75-c4385bd4e90f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-1,0)$ and a saddle point at $(1,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04ac1472-02e4-5f9b-96d9-0b4fd8a918e3" :answer/id #uuid "04ac1472-02e4-5f9b-96d9-0b4fd8a918e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(-2,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2193002c-a5f9-5f43-9491-8ce090c6ce8e" :answer/id #uuid "2193002c-a5f9-5f43-9491-8ce090c6ce8e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(-2,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4493e258-0fda-5c69-96f7-243298d2e5b6" :answer/id #uuid "4493e258-0fda-5c69-96f7-243298d2e5b6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has saddles points at $(0,0)$ and $(-2,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125891 :tutorial/content "The **second partial derivatives test** helps to determine whether a stationary point of a function of two variables gives rise to a local maximum, a local minimum, or a saddle point.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a}$ is a critical point of $f(\\mathbf x),$ and let $H(\\mathbf{a})$ denote the determinant of $\\boldsymbol f''(x,y)$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{a}$ (recall that $H(\\mathbf a)$ is called the *Hessian determinant* of $f$ at $\\mathbf a$).\n\nThe second partial derivative test states the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$  then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a},$ and\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$  then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive, and we need to classify the point using some other method.\n\nAs an example, consider the function $f (x, y) = 3 + 2x^2 - x y + y^2.$ Let's find the critical points of this function and then classify them using the second partial derivatives test.\n\nWe start by computing the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 4x -y, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = 2y -x\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-y=0 \\\\ 2y-x=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $x=0, y=0.$ Hence, $(0,0)$ is the only stationary point.  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-y)=4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-x)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(0,0)) = 4 \\cdot 2 - (-1)^2 = 7 > 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(0,0) > 0$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(0,0) = (\\boldsymbol f''(0,0))_{11}= 4 >0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162110 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-af95ddae-1d2c-5a3d-9f8a-8a341d008cae"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162378 :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{50}∼N(5,{10}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $50$ from a normal population, calculate $P(4.8<\\overset{―}{X}<5.2).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5398\\,$ | $\\,0.5438\\,$ | $\\,0.5478\\,$ | $\\,0.5517\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5793\\,$ | $\\,0.5832\\,$ | $\\,0.5871\\,$ | $\\,0.5910\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6179\\,$ | $\\,0.6217\\,$ | $\\,0.6255\\,$ | $\\,0.6293\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ | $\\,0.6664\\,$ | $\\,0.6700\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\n then the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(5,\\frac{{10}^{2}}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(5,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(4.8<\\overset{―}{X}<5.2).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4.8<\\overset{―}{X}<5.2) & =P(\\frac{4.8-5}{\\sqrt{2}}<Z<\\frac{5.2-5}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =P(-0.14<Z<0.14) \\\\  & =Φ(0.14)-Φ(-0.14)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.14)=0.5557,\n$$\n\nand by the symmetry of the normal distribution,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,Φ(-0.14) & =1-Φ(0.14) \\\\  & =1-0.5557 \\\\  & =0.4443.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4.8<\\overset{―}{X}<5.2) & =Φ(0.14)-Φ(-0.14) \\\\  & =0.5557-0.4443 \\\\  & =0.1114.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186159470 :answer-field/correct 17592186159473 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-af95ddae-1d2c-5a3d-9f8a-8a341d008cae" :question/id #uuid "af95ddae-1d2c-5a3d-9f8a-8a341d008cae" :question/math-academy-id "q-143298" :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{18}∼N(2,{6}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $18$ from a normal population, calculate $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<1.05).$\n\n*Hint: Value tables of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2578\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2546\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2514\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2912\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2877\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2843\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3264\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3228\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3192\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0119\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0116\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0113\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0154\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0150\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0146\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0197\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0192\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0188\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(2,\\frac{{6}^{2}}{18}) \\\\  & ∼N(2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<1.05).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<1.05) & =P(-1.05<\\overset{―}{X}<1.05) \\\\  & =P(\\frac{-1.05-2}{\\sqrt{2}}<Z<\\frac{1.05-2}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =P(-2.16<Z<-0.67) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.67)-Φ(-2.16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.67) & =0.2514, \\\\ Φ(-2.16) & =0.0154.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<1.05) & =Φ(-0.67)-Φ(-2.16) \\\\  & =0.2514-0.0154 \\\\  & =0.2360.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4cf4ed49-e9a9-55e6-9767-0a26348a3bfb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4cf4ed49-e9a9-55e6-9767-0a26348a3bfb" :answer-field/id #uuid "4cf4ed49-e9a9-55e6-9767-0a26348a3bfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5efb2903-8839-5c75-956f-1aa308f45c7c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5efb2903-8839-5c75-956f-1aa308f45c7c" "answer-15bc03b3-bcc4-5b74-8002-f27cc815242f" "answer-3aceefc6-ab68-5b37-9369-311c93768227" "answer-3e6a7853-622c-56fa-9435-d57c96f2c7fb" "answer-675b1964-64e8-5858-82bc-e2eb491dfd52"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5efb2903-8839-5c75-956f-1aa308f45c7c" :answer/id #uuid "5efb2903-8839-5c75-956f-1aa308f45c7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2360" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15bc03b3-bcc4-5b74-8002-f27cc815242f" :answer/id #uuid "15bc03b3-bcc4-5b74-8002-f27cc815242f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2401" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3aceefc6-ab68-5b37-9369-311c93768227" :answer/id #uuid "3aceefc6-ab68-5b37-9369-311c93768227" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2459" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e6a7853-622c-56fa-9435-d57c96f2c7fb" :answer/id #uuid "3e6a7853-622c-56fa-9435-d57c96f2c7fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2727" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-675b1964-64e8-5858-82bc-e2eb491dfd52" :answer/id #uuid "675b1964-64e8-5858-82bc-e2eb491dfd52" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3036" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-56b16376-f7f5-5212-a656-88557c1c7bb6" :activity/id #uuid "56b16376-f7f5-5212-a656-88557c1c7bb6" :activity/title "Sample Means From Normal Populations" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049180 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-c214b669-97ef-54cc-8cd6-c94b9c9263d3" "step-8418b995-b694-559d-b207-8c157fbab2dd" "step-279033db-58c9-524d-a19b-5f81359775c6" "step-25ee6b33-b92b-5ff3-b7e2-048a85b255ce" "step-2e904f97-f40d-52d6-8b17-ef7a206a51c3"] :activity/first-step "step-c214b669-97ef-54cc-8cd6-c94b9c9263d3" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-c214b669-97ef-54cc-8cd6-c94b9c9263d3" :step/id #uuid "c214b669-97ef-54cc-8cd6-c94b9c9263d3" :step/content 17592186162378 :step/next "step-8418b995-b694-559d-b207-8c157fbab2dd" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8418b995-b694-559d-b207-8c157fbab2dd" :step/id #uuid "8418b995-b694-559d-b207-8c157fbab2dd" :step/content 17592186421851 :step/next "step-279033db-58c9-524d-a19b-5f81359775c6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-279033db-58c9-524d-a19b-5f81359775c6" :step/id #uuid "279033db-58c9-524d-a19b-5f81359775c6" :step/content "question-af95ddae-1d2c-5a3d-9f8a-8a341d008cae" :step/next "step-25ee6b33-b92b-5ff3-b7e2-048a85b255ce" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-25ee6b33-b92b-5ff3-b7e2-048a85b255ce" :step/id #uuid "25ee6b33-b92b-5ff3-b7e2-048a85b255ce" :step/content 17592186444045 :step/next "step-2e904f97-f40d-52d6-8b17-ef7a206a51c3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2e904f97-f40d-52d6-8b17-ef7a206a51c3" :step/id #uuid "2e904f97-f40d-52d6-8b17-ef7a206a51c3" :step/content 17592186421628 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167293 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\pi .$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{-}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\pi .$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=1 \\\\ {f}^{+}(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(\\pi )+{f}^{+}(\\pi )}{2}=\\frac{1+0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164979 :answer-field/correct "answer-735db7a5-7bcb-5fa9-a24e-2f32aa107ab9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-735db7a5-7bcb-5fa9-a24e-2f32aa107ab9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-735db7a5-7bcb-5fa9-a24e-2f32aa107ab9" :answer/id #uuid "735db7a5-7bcb-5fa9-a24e-2f32aa107ab9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186375034 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & x\\in [0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\pi .$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\pi .$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166961 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9ad7e028-7ba2-59c0-89a8-df188134628e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186402302 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-1, & x\\in [-4,0] \\\\ 3, & x\\in (0,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi x}{4}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi \\cdot (-2)}{4})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=-2,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(-2)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-9ad7e028-7ba2-59c0-89a8-df188134628e" :question/id #uuid "9ad7e028-7ba2-59c0-89a8-df188134628e" :question/math-academy-id "q-343448" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-3, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi )=(-1)^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ 1 & =-1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n+1} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b8a017d0-45f8-582e-844c-12a17fcdf030" "answer-field-0de15137-bf56-596a-a38a-ac01796200ee" "answer-field-8864a19e-a3dc-5d3a-b104-94d3cbc88d3b" "answer-field-253cae4c-287a-57dd-940c-b84a887327b5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b8a017d0-45f8-582e-844c-12a17fcdf030" :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a017d0-45f8-582e-844c-12a17fcdf030" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-3aabc6bb-ca4a-5cb9-9ccd-4e3aecda5a5f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3aabc6bb-ca4a-5cb9-9ccd-4e3aecda5a5f" "answer-dde8b71b-c391-5