l $[8,29].$\n\n![](images/14/14acd67d0b773864d39aaafcc6b41c9d8f3f10317b5e3f4584711baf157f346d.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186132864 :answer-field/correct "answer-e3ad0ec0-5eeb-55bb-90c8-6a2b407c2776" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e3ad0ec0-5eeb-55bb-90c8-6a2b407c2776" "answer-8f9ffd2c-dd45-507a-817e-8dcd915bedb7" "answer-f36c1b2f-f8a1-5e34-a62d-d5939d67d3a8" "answer-3bd58c2f-9a5c-56a0-b502-6fad91d539f6" "answer-d6b03ebb-7afa-5de2-a056-ca74b06f0be2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3ad0ec0-5eeb-55bb-90c8-6a2b407c2776" :answer/id #uuid "e3ad0ec0-5eeb-55bb-90c8-6a2b407c2776" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f9ffd2c-dd45-507a-817e-8dcd915bedb7" :answer/id #uuid "8f9ffd2c-dd45-507a-817e-8dcd915bedb7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f36c1b2f-f8a1-5e34-a62d-d5939d67d3a8" :answer/id #uuid "f36c1b2f-f8a1-5e34-a62d-d5939d67d3a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bd58c2f-9a5c-56a0-b502-6fad91d539f6" :answer/id #uuid "3bd58c2f-9a5c-56a0-b502-6fad91d539f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6b03ebb-7afa-5de2-a056-ca74b06f0be2" :answer/id #uuid "d6b03ebb-7afa-5de2-a056-ca74b06f0be2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8c198801-a87e-5360-90a9-0643872362f6" :question/id #uuid "8c198801-a87e-5360-90a9-0643872362f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-47084" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=(x-1)(x+1)(x-2).$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem **not** guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $[0,3]?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,3],$ therefore, the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(0)$ and $f(3).$\n\nSince $f(0)=2$ and $f(3)=8,$ we must have \n$k\\in [2,8].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=0$ does not belong to the interval $[2,8].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cd25d2f7-fa41-5617-b9af-782c028c8030"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cd25d2f7-fa41-5617-b9af-782c028c8030" :answer-field/id #uuid "cd25d2f7-fa41-5617-b9af-782c028c8030" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c0b2d814-242e-5411-8cbb-8d9b3d6ebce1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c0b2d814-242e-5411-8cbb-8d9b3d6ebce1" "answer-d21efb8c-0299-51f7-b5d9-24f0b2b1ed5a" "answer-0da21742-bc6f-5a5c-949b-2c60da707f2b" "answer-0224e1ad-7d28-51e0-b5be-99fdaf9f354d" "answer-46cb9080-7932-5380-a15f-5c65924138ab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0b2d814-242e-5411-8cbb-8d9b3d6ebce1" :answer/id #uuid "c0b2d814-242e-5411-8cbb-8d9b3d6ebce1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d21efb8c-0299-51f7-b5d9-24f0b2b1ed5a" :answer/id #uuid "d21efb8c-0299-51f7-b5d9-24f0b2b1ed5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0da21742-bc6f-5a5c-949b-2c60da707f2b" :answer/id #uuid "0da21742-bc6f-5a5c-949b-2c60da707f2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0224e1ad-7d28-51e0-b5be-99fdaf9f354d" :answer/id #uuid "0224e1ad-7d28-51e0-b5be-99fdaf9f354d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46cb9080-7932-5380-a15f-5c65924138ab" :answer/id #uuid "46cb9080-7932-5380-a15f-5c65924138ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-14a3bc41-5c72-5f24-9c07-376f58252a5e" :question/id #uuid "14a3bc41-5c72-5f24-9c07-376f58252a5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-105670" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=(x-2)(x+1)^{2}.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem **not** guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $[-2,2]?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,2],$ therefore, the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-2)$ and $f(2).$\n\nSince $f(-2)=-4$ and $f(2)=0,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-4,0].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=1$ does not belong to the interval $[-4,0].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4936f945-a8a7-583d-b9fa-25b703a244c3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4936f945-a8a7-583d-b9fa-25b703a244c3" :answer-field/id #uuid "4936f945-a8a7-583d-b9fa-25b703a244c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-db5d1df6-982e-58c9-bcf7-f4b59e4c781a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cca99c35-d37a-5aac-91ef-5d9cf74bb486" "answer-a6595a77-7bd1-5d35-857f-6104adfa786e" "answer-ffcab920-346d-5305-b97c-85f83eb95244" "answer-7a16361d-c7f3-5d0a-a9a2-83b038b0c4b9" "answer-db5d1df6-982e-58c9-bcf7-f4b59e4c781a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cca99c35-d37a-5aac-91ef-5d9cf74bb486" :answer/id #uuid "cca99c35-d37a-5aac-91ef-5d9cf74bb486" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6595a77-7bd1-5d35-857f-6104adfa786e" :answer/id #uuid "a6595a77-7bd1-5d35-857f-6104adfa786e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffcab920-346d-5305-b97c-85f83eb95244" :answer/id #uuid "ffcab920-346d-5305-b97c-85f83eb95244" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a16361d-c7f3-5d0a-a9a2-83b038b0c4b9" :answer/id #uuid "7a16361d-c7f3-5d0a-a9a2-83b038b0c4b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db5d1df6-982e-58c9-bcf7-f4b59e4c781a" :answer/id #uuid "db5d1df6-982e-58c9-bcf7-f4b59e4c781a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9d898f3e-75a2-51d9-91a1-d58badf3f268" :question/id #uuid "9d898f3e-75a2-51d9-91a1-d58badf3f268" :question/math-academy-id "q-47053" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-1,8].$ Some values of $f(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $5$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-5$ | $0$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $8$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $f(x)=-2?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is a continuous function on $[-1,8]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-1,8].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[-1,8]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-1,0],$ we have $f(-1)=-5$ and $f(0)=0.$ Since $-2\\in [-5,0],$ the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[0,2],$ we have $f(0)=0$ and $f(2)=4.$ Since $-2\\notin [0,4],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[2,4],$ we have $f(2)=4$ and $f(4)=-1.$ Since $-2\\notin [-1,4],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[4,5],$ we have $f(4)=-1$ and $f(5)=-4.$ Since $-2\\in [-4,-1],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[5,8],$ we have $f(5)=-4$ and $f(8)=8.$ Since $-2\\in [-4,8],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $f(x)=-2$ on the subintervals $[-1,0],$ $[4,5],$ and $[5,8].$ Therefore, $f(x)=-2$ has at least three solutions." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9e0a4333-6056-5794-b8d1-b1934cbb02e6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9e0a4333-6056-5794-b8d1-b1934cbb02e6" :answer-field/id #uuid "9e0a4333-6056-5794-b8d1-b1934cbb02e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-31dc79ee-ee5b-500d-a119-9ce1a09bd88d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-604be48b-5aad-5ab0-83bb-0f89ed9fb60b" "answer-2d71d7a5-84f6-5c9e-bb9e-45ee005734d5" "answer-31dc79ee-ee5b-500d-a119-9ce1a09bd88d" "answer-bdf793b6-7c2c-54c6-b01c-c14073dbc0c5" "answer-9a5cb47f-1426-50ff-b362-c08943c4bf70"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-604be48b-5aad-5ab0-83bb-0f89ed9fb60b" :answer/id #uuid "604be48b-5aad-5ab0-83bb-0f89ed9fb60b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d71d7a5-84f6-5c9e-bb9e-45ee005734d5" :answer/id #uuid "2d71d7a5-84f6-5c9e-bb9e-45ee005734d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31dc79ee-ee5b-500d-a119-9ce1a09bd88d" :answer/id #uuid "31dc79ee-ee5b-500d-a119-9ce1a09bd88d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bdf793b6-7c2c-54c6-b01c-c14073dbc0c5" :answer/id #uuid "bdf793b6-7c2c-54c6-b01c-c14073dbc0c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a5cb47f-1426-50ff-b362-c08943c4bf70" :answer/id #uuid "9a5cb47f-1426-50ff-b362-c08943c4bf70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-75621f90-4584-51d0-a7ef-873c52eae724" :question/id #uuid "75621f90-4584-51d0-a7ef-873c52eae724" :question/math-academy-id "q-344753" :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-3,4].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $-4$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $-1$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=1?$\n\nThe equation $g(x)=1$ has at least\n{{field-1}} solutions." :question/worked-solution "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-3,4],$ we can apply the Intermediate Value Theorem (IVT) in each subinterval of $[-3,4].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[-3,4]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-3,-2],$ we have $g(-3)=-4$ and $g(-2)=3.$ Since $1\\in [-4,3],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-2,-1],$ we have $g(-2)=3$ and $g(-1)=0.$ Since $1\\in [0,3],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-1,1],$ we have $g(-1)=0$ and $g(1)=-2.$ Since $1\\notin [-2,0],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[1,3],$ we have $g(1)=-2$ and $g(3)=-5.$ Since $1\\notin [-5,-2],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[3,4],$ we have $g(3)=-5$ and $g(4)=-1.$ Since $1\\notin [-5,-1],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=1$ on the subintervals $[-3,-2]$ and $[-2,-1].$ \n\nTherefore, $g(x)=1$ has at least $2$ solutions." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-543d11e1-6cdb-5661-b09d-c8b41c9f2d71"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-543d11e1-6cdb-5661-b09d-c8b41c9f2d71" :answer-field/id #uuid "543d11e1-6cdb-5661-b09d-c8b41c9f2d71" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-be33c5e4-2086-5d44-a684-333e21729caf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-be33c5e4-2086-5d44-a684-333e21729caf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be33c5e4-2086-5d44-a684-333e21729caf" :answer/id #uuid "be33c5e4-2086-5d44-a684-333e21729caf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2715d4ad-4bbd-59ce-9226-02bf5ba9575d" :question/id #uuid "2715d4ad-4bbd-59ce-9226-02bf5ba9575d" :question/math-academy-id "q-105705" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-9}?$" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have ${x}^{2}-9=0,$ which corresponds to $x=-3$ or $x=3.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the points $x=-3$ or $x=3.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-8,-4]$ is allowed. The intervals $(3,4)$ and $(-7,-3)$ are not closed, the interval $[-6,-1]$ contains the point $x=-3,$ while the interval $[3,5]$ contain the point $x=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-417d21e0-0d55-5aa1-8095-733c8c96dbdc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-417d21e0-0d55-5aa1-8095-733c8c96dbdc" :answer-field/id #uuid "417d21e0-0d55-5aa1-8095-733c8c96dbdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b454dec1-2be2-50f7-9bb7-ec9fac4d67c9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-332285b0-e91b-57b0-a576-f6ca5cf0f2bc" "answer-01e518e9-b753-57d2-80df-fe41348834f2" "answer-03808112-580d-56e4-b2a4-2317d31bbee4" "answer-b454dec1-2be2-50f7-9bb7-ec9fac4d67c9" "answer-79fcb8ae-30d6-5841-a620-17613af8e635"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-332285b0-e91b-57b0-a576-f6ca5cf0f2bc" :answer/id #uuid "332285b0-e91b-57b0-a576-f6ca5cf0f2bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-7,-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01e518e9-b753-57d2-80df-fe41348834f2" :answer/id #uuid "01e518e9-b753-57d2-80df-fe41348834f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03808112-580d-56e4-b2a4-2317d31bbee4" :answer/id #uuid "03808112-580d-56e4-b2a4-2317d31bbee4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-6,-1]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b454dec1-2be2-50f7-9bb7-ec9fac4d67c9" :answer/id #uuid "b454dec1-2be2-50f7-9bb7-ec9fac4d67c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-8,-4]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79fcb8ae-30d6-5841-a620-17613af8e635" :answer/id #uuid "79fcb8ae-30d6-5841-a620-17613af8e635" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[3,5]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0a118e6f-2768-508a-b130-7e64bda76208" :question/id #uuid "0a118e6f-2768-508a-b130-7e64bda76208" :question/math-academy-id "q-105709" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-4x+4}?$" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which makes the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have ${x}^{2}-4x+4=0,$ which corresponds to $x=2.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=2.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-1,1]$ is allowed. The intervals $(-2,1]$ and $[-1,2)$ are not closed, while $[0,2]$ and $[-1,3]$ contain the point $x=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-51eb9745-3497-568e-b210-1b5ebb30ffe7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-51eb9745-3497-568e-b210-1b5ebb30ffe7" :answer-field/id #uuid "51eb9745-3497-568e-b210-1b5ebb30ffe7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0dab59d9-8d5f-5743-b2ba-ac1d189d7f25" :answer-field/choices ["answer-869751bf-0fab-560d-b2e0-38fe5be8abba" "answer-0dab59d9-8d5f-5743-b2ba-ac1d189d7f25" "answer-13d6152b-91ad-53ff-8e73-d4dd221064be" "answer-c4532b91-ed3c-5f76-b794-27184920a48e" "answer-b79dd5d8-dee2-5d68-8fd9-5ab751b2a6bb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-869751bf-0fab-560d-b2e0-38fe5be8abba" :answer/id #uuid "869751bf-0fab-560d-b2e0-38fe5be8abba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,1]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0dab59d9-8d5f-5743-b2ba-ac1d189d7f25" :answer/id #uuid "0dab59d9-8d5f-5743-b2ba-ac1d189d7f25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-1,1]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13d6152b-91ad-53ff-8e73-d4dd221064be" :answer/id #uuid "13d6152b-91ad-53ff-8e73-d4dd221064be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-1,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4532b91-ed3c-5f76-b794-27184920a48e" :answer/id #uuid "c4532b91-ed3c-5f76-b794-27184920a48e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-1,3]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b79dd5d8-dee2-5d68-8fd9-5ab751b2a6bb" :answer/id #uuid "b79dd5d8-dee2-5d68-8fd9-5ab751b2a6bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[0,2]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134472 :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{x-1}?$" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have $x-1=0,$ which corresponds to $x=1.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=1.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-1,0]$ is allowed.  The intervals $(1,2]$ and $[-1,1)$ are not closed, $[-2,2]$ and $[0,2]$ contain the point $x=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186132870 :answer-field/correct "answer-e0b29a61-0410-5720-bc50-a49bb3285d7c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9cb83e26-31fc-58df-adb4-932057c64208" "answer-e0b29a61-0410-5720-bc50-a49bb3285d7c" "answer-859cd724-e6e0-57c5-9905-18920a9c1447" "answer-a5f19c97-cdf6-5e14-aa26-e22cb035f7a1" "answer-3397312b-322c-5838-add4-4c3ef92f9289"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9cb83e26-31fc-58df-adb4-932057c64208" :answer/id #uuid "9cb83e26-31fc-58df-adb4-932057c64208" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,2]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0b29a61-0410-5720-bc50-a49bb3285d7c" :answer/id #uuid "e0b29a61-0410-5720-bc50-a49bb3285d7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-1,0]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-859cd724-e6e0-57c5-9905-18920a9c1447" :answer/id #uuid "859cd724-e6e0-57c5-9905-18920a9c1447" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-1,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5f19c97-cdf6-5e14-aa26-e22cb035f7a1" :answer/id #uuid "a5f19c97-cdf6-5e14-aa26-e22cb035f7a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-2,2]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3397312b-322c-5838-add4-4c3ef92f9289" :answer/id #uuid "3397312b-322c-5838-add4-4c3ef92f9289" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[0,2]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135022 :tutorial/content "The **intermediate value theorem (IVT)** states that:\n\n*If $f(x)$ is continuous on $\\color{blue}[a,b],$ and $k$ is a number between $\\color{red}{f(a)}$ and $\\color{red}{f(b)}$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in \\color{blue}{[a,b]}$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nThe intuition behind the IVT is that if a function is continuous between two inputs, then between those two inputs, we must be able to draw the function without picking up our pen off the page, so the function must cover all values between the two corresponding outputs.\n\nThe IVT is often used to prove that some equation has a solution. For example, if we want to prove that there is a solution to $f(x)=3,$ and we know that $f(x)$ is a continuous function with $f({\\color{blue}{1}})={\\color{red}{2}}$ and $f({\\color{blue}{6}})={\\color{red}{6}},$ then the IVT tells us that there exists some solution $c \\in \\color{blue}{[1,6]}$ such that $f(c)=3.$\n\nWe can gain further intuition by visualizing the situation. First, let's plot what $y=f(x)$ *might* look like.\n\n![](images/98/9896ea1c3d3d7c461e78ce4b4b232ce1f13f329468cd28b2d8e743132de093df.png)\n\nFor this particular $f(x)$ there is one solution to $f(x)=3$, which is $x\\approx 2.$ Note that our solution lies inside the interval $\\color{blue}[1,6].$\n\nNow let's consider another *possible* $f(x).$\n\n![](images/de/dea5683d5b18bccf9623fb6a107e8ef818ddd1cef4adbef08be471869791ec27.png)\n\nThis time, there are three solutions to $f(x)=3$ which lie in the interval $\\color{blue}[1,6].$ \n\nWe can keep changing our $f(x),$ but no matter how we draw it, we will always have some solution to $f(x)=3$ which lies in the interval $\\color{blue}[1,6].$\n\n![](images/4d/4d4aaec84baff10a48f715eeae7e06af21beccd29b38c42b3283032ef0726fec.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135023 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)$ given below.\n![](images/0d/0d18168108342ef8232063b58e46ef88cdff9fe54aa730dca872bc9755a42995.svg)\nCan we apply the intermediate value theorem to $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,4]?$\n\nRemember, the intermediate value theorem assumes that the function is continuous. Here, the function $f(x)$ not continuous on $[1,4].$ So, we cannot apply the intermediate value theorem.\n\nIf we plot the function, we can get an idea of why the intermediate value theorem cannot be applied.\n\n![](images/9b/9b9543ec11b350cea4ca18e24a8db6ae8f72ffc38e64f973f9af2f83f1226400.png)\n\nThe graph shows that, for example, the equation $f(x)=1.5$ has no solution on $[1,4]$ despite the fact that $1.5$ lies between $f(1)=4$ and $f(4)=0.$\nSo, the intermediate value theorem does not hold.\n\n**Watch out!** The interval on which we apply the intermediate value theorem must *always* be closed. For example, we can't apply the theorem on an interval like $[1,3).$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134019 :activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
